      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <p>McDOUGALL'S Patett SELF-RAISING FLOUR MAKES TBEST PASTRY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>fASHIONS OF TO-DAL</head>
        <p>fASHIONS OF TO-DAL</p>
        <p>Dress in all its departments is seen at its best at this festive season of the year, and as each season ensues a greater perfection is to be observed, not only in the texture of the many beautiful materials, but in the delicacy of the nountless shades of colouring and the wonder- ful embroideries of all kinds which are also very reasonable in price. Indeed, a danger to those who have not a liberal dress allowance is to be found in this very profusion, for it in- duces the purchasing of many trifles which are not really necessary, but which prove an irresistible temptation by reason of their beauty and cheapness. It is undoubtedly the best plan to avoid purchasing the odds and ends which only in themselves cost a shilling or two unless we set aside a sum out of each</p>
        <p>BLOt7S'.</p>
        <p>quarter's allowance for such small extrava- gances. They form welcome Christmas gifts to most young people, and the pretty cravats, embroidered and lace collars, dainty handker- chiefs, and belts, not to mention the hundred and one pretty trifles for the toilet table, are suitable to all tastes and all purses. Blouses are if anything more popular than ever, and I have seen some really lovely ones in soft white silk for half a guinea which are well worth in- vesting in. There are, of course, many on view at even a smaller price than this, but the silk is apt to be of a very thin description which does not stand much wear, and the material is also rather skimped and they are therefore only suited for very petite and slight figures. Another weakness of the very cheap blouse lies in the fastening arrangements which seem specially designed to gape at in- tervals without any possibility of remedying the defect. My first sketch depicts the charm of a very pretty blouse for afternoon wear. It is made of a faint shade of turquoise crete de chine over which tiny cream coloured spots in silk are sprinkled. The feature of the blouse lies in the deep collar of ecru lace over white silk which falls from the shoulders over the arms to the elbows and is held in place across the figure by a scraf of ecru coloured chiffon. The full sleeves are caught into .close fitting lace. cuffs at the wrists and a</p>
        <p>NTY •GOWN. collar of the same lace finishes the throat, a narrow turquoise velvet ribbon being run through the lace and tied in a dainty little knot at the back of the neck. I should sug- gest that it would be a great advantage to make the collar detachable as it would be seriously crushed if worn underneath a coat. A few pretty little ornamental safety pins could be arranged to keep it in place. The front of the blouse is fulled and slightly pouched over the belt. It is noticeable that though we were warned some months ago that the pouched blouse would soon be a thing of the past, that it is still in favour, although in a modified form, and there is no probability that it will be otherwise as most. women have proved its becomingness to every variety of figure. A blouse with a tight front is in- artistic and gives a short look to the upper part of the figure which, as a, rule, does not produce a modish effect. Of course in the case of a gown for evening wear such as is shown in my second sketch, the ion.- effect is brought about by the deep pointed of folded silk, over which the full lace frill which finishes the decolletage falls. These deep belts are sometimes used with afternoon blouses of a rather elaborate description, but they require to be carefully selected or, if possible, made to measure to ensure the right effect. They must also be well boned to keep them in place. The dress itself is made of coffee coloured silk trimmed with lace of a rather lighter \ tone, while a deep oflunce of the silk accordion pleated is headed by a band of lace edged by a narrow ruching</p>
        <p>of bluish green chiffon. The belt is of the same shade of blue, while blue enamel and paste buckles catch the, lace at intervals over the shoulders and where it falls in a Aaterfall frill at either side. Tile sleeves are cut to afford a glimpse of the shoulders between the straps and the sleeves, a fashion which is very becoming to most women. A bow of silk in the hair matches the touch of blue in the dress.</p>
        <p>My last illustration illustrates a smart hat of fine black chip trimmed with a couple of handsome ostrich feathers, finished where they meet by a chou of black chiffon caught by an ornamental buckle. A couple of strings made of chiffon are tied in a dainty little bow at the left side. An upward tilt is given to the hat by a bandeau under the brim which is cora.e.aled by a large creamy rose which is in iteth a realistic imitation of the real article. It is a stylish hat which could be worn with any sort of gown and is not in any of the modes which for a month or two attain to a great vogue and then pass altogether out of fashion. The mushroom hat with its stiff ruching of silk or satin has been rather too (popular of late, and, as is the fate of anything which, is worn by all and sundry without regard to whether it suits them individually or not, there are already signs that its popu• %arity has brought about its doom. • CABLIBiak</p>
