y of our ancesto'Canada, and among other things it presents to the rate rs. They also carried out their Christmas decerations more payers at home, the advantage of reducing greatly the thoroughly than we do now. This custom, like so may I expenditure on maintenance. I of those associated with our festivals, was heathen in Coreless Apples.—We are shortly to have a new kind origin, as long before the first Christmas it was the of apple, like that the urchin was eating' and how, when practive to hang up greenery about dwelling places, so asked by another to leave him the core, replied that that the woodland spirits might find a warm place until "there ain't going to be no core." That is to be liter- the leaves came again in the spring, ally true of the new Upples, which are being grown in When Royalty is present,—Royal visits to country the United States and Canada, and which are seedless houses all help to make it good for trade,', both yi and coreless. Not only will the fruit be better, but locally, and in a wider circle, as gowns, cloaks, etc., all the trees will be almost blossomless and will therefore have little to fear from the spring frosts, or from the 'have to be new and recherctie. The guests as a rule are not very numerous when the King and Queen are apple worm which destroys the fruit to get at the seed on which it exists. Orchards it is true will be a sorry present, but a list has to be submitted to their Majesties beforehand,and every woman who is honoured sight without the lovely apple blossom, but if the new with an invitation has to change her toilette several fruit comes up to expectation it will more than corn- times during the day. Morning, luncheon, and dainty j pensate for this drawback. tea gowns, all have to be donned and discarded, and, I Legacies to Cats.—Men as a rule seldom make pets of under no circumstances can the dinner toilette make al cats, but a barrister of Bath recently left an annuity to second appearance. On such occasions all visitors in- I his housekeeper provided she looked after his cats, the eluded in the house party, become the guests of the payment to cease after the death of the last of them. Sovereign incuding the host and hostess, and if the Those cats may therefore safely look forward to be well Queen is present, it is her Majesty who gives the signal taken care of, and the same means have been adopted to retire from the dinner table. Then it is not only by many an old maiden lady to ensure the kindly treat- upon dress and ornaments that there is lavish expendi- ment of her pets. One of these was the Oxfordshire ture. There are the decorations, sports, and entertain- lady who left a legacy to the late Lord Randolph -„ments, specially provided, and which all go to make a Churchill" in recognition of his commanding political visit from his Majesty a very cosy affair, buts which genius." Fe left a large number of cats with grandi- necessarily brings grist to the mill for a large number pains." names, for each of which was left £l2 annually of tradesmen, and people in business, for its maintenance so long as it lived. Concerning Lace.—Queen Alexandra possesses many valuable collections, but first and foremost comes He 4 Majesty's assortment of lace which is one of the finest the world contains. Each Christmas, and again on the Queen's birthday, additions are made to this particular collection, by members of her family, who never lose an opportunity of securing some rare and beautiful pieces, likely to be approved by her Majesty. Princess Henry of Battenberg also collects lace, and is said to possess some of the most valuable in existence. Rare treasures came to her from Queen Victoria, but these she seldom wears, indeed Her Royal Highness rarely dons any of her wonderful specimens in the way of lace. Among the many Society women who make ahobby of lace collect- ing are, the Duchess of Portland, Lady Granby, Lady Wolseley, Lady Londonderry, and Mrs.Asquith,the latter possessing a wonderful dinner dress of old Alencon lace. Fashions for Young Girls.—Societv mothers have returned to the low bodices and short leeves for their girls' evening wear, quite tiny children wearing frocks so fashioned for" going down to dessert." It is main- tained, and not without reason, that to keep girls in high, or almost high frocks, and long sleeves until they are sixteen or seventeen, and then plunge them into an unceasing round of gaieties demanding low gowns,is not a wise procedure, and that the Society girl should be trained from an early age to expose her neck and arms every evening, when there will be little or no risk of her taking chills thereby. Transparent lace or chiffon tops are added to"the bodices, and falling draperies to the sleeves of "honey" girls, bet fashionable mothers who can expose their young daughters' necks and arms, nowadays, do so during the period between tea and bedtime. Powdered Hain—Fancy dress is a great feature this year, especially those examples of it demanding powdered hair, which proves so very becoming to the great majority of girls. To make a success of a powdered head, the hair should be well brushed with brilliantine before the powder—violet riowder preferably is applied, this being done with a well-filled puff, care being taken to whiten every part equally. To put down Keptomania.—ln London the great drapery establishments have organised a vigorous cam- phign against shop lifting during the January Sales. For this purpose young girls have been trained as detectives and their employers declare that they will prosecute all feminine depredators without mercy. Repeated warn- RUNCORN CYCLIST IN TROUBLE. ings seem to have no effectupon a certain class of women Alfred Jolley, tinsmith, Byldge street, Runcorn, was many of them comparatively well-to-do, who cannot brought before the Runcorn magistrates on Tuesday understand that taking things without paying for thorn charged with ridinga'bicycle to the danger of foot- is common and 