      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, December 31, 1904. TIE STORY OF THE YEAR</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 31, 1904. TIE STORY OF THE YEAR</p>
        <p>The year 1904 will be most memorable for the discovery of Japan as a first-class fighting power against a European nation. It may I stand out also for two incidents which in themselves are small but may be far-reaching in destiny—the rocking of cradles at St.l Petersburg and at Rome for heirs to two thrones. Another event which should cast its I influence through the corridors of time is the Anglo-French Convention, which, indeed, had an almost immediately beneficial effect on : our relations with Russia. No more —need ' be said in regard to the actual terms of the clearing up of old-standing differences than that the Parliamentary debates in the two countries left the impression that a very equitable bargain had been made. In Paris a few grumbled that M. Delcasse had been yielding as to Egypt: and at Westminster a few about Lord Lansdowne and Morocco. So they balanced, and both nations were glad to have the matters out of the way. , Meanwhile King Edward, "himself his Igreatest Minister, continued his admirable diplomacy in Europe. At his instance the Prince and Princess of Wales made a visit to Vienna, and their reception strengthened the good feeling between us and the Court' of Austria-Hungary, who in truth have been our friends through good and ill report. King Edward some months later met the venerable Emperor Francis Joseph at Marienbad. The sentiment between our country and Germany has not been so agreeable, but the two Govern- ments have been studiously courteous; and another instance of our Sovereign's tact was seen in a meeting with Kaiser Wilhelm at Kiel. A few months later it was announced that an Anglo-German Treaty of Arbitration on the same lines as those we had already made with France and Italy had been con- cluded. Another—with Portugal—commemo- rates the welcome which the King and Queen of that kingdom have so lately had amongst us. Still another pleasing event was Lord Lansdowne's announcement at the Guildhall Banquet that Mr. Choate had called upon him and asked whether we would be willing to enter into a similar treaty with the United States. It will be remembered that an approach made by us a few years ago failed of its purpose. Other Royal activities, of which space forbids more than a mention, are the tour of the King and Queen through the West of Ireland with so much' simplicity and kind- ness; the happy arrangements made for the pleasure of the Alake of Abeokuta ; and the several stone-layings for a new St. Bartholo- mew's Hospital, for a Cathedral at Liverpool, and for new doeks at Swansea. His Majesty was prevented by a cold from going to the centenary service of the British and Foreign Bible Society at St. Paul's; but the Queen and the Prince and Princess of Wales gave it their presence. In world politics the most noteworthy features were the overwhelming victory of Mr. Roosevelt in the United States Presidential Election; the short reign of Mr. Watson at the head of a Labour Ministry in Australia; the Labour riots in Italy (soon over) ; and the wise appointment of Lord Grey to be Governor-General of Canada. THE ANGLO-RUSSIAN CRISIS.</p>
        <p>The feeling of indignation aroused in this country on the morning of October' 24th has now completely passed; but it is too fresh to call for paany words here. Near midnight on the previous Friday the Russian Baltic Fleet, on its way to the Far East, had reached the Dogger Bank, in the North Sea, by a round- about course, and had passed through the Gamecock fishing fleet from Hull, which had its trawls down. One of these fishing-boats came into Hull on the Sunday morning with the news that the fleet had been fired upon; that one boat, the Crane, had been sunk; that her skipper and third hand had been killed and the rest lof her crew wounded; and that other injuries and 'damage had been done. Needless to say, the sailors were able to declare positively that no provocation had been offered by them in their peaceful work, and the worst part of the affair was that the Russian fleet had sailed on without offering any assistance or staying to see the extent of the havoc wrought. It was difficult to restrain the fury at what almost everyone then called the North Sea "Outrage." Cabinet Councils were held daily; the innocent Russian Ambassador, coming back to London from the Continent, was hooted at the railway station; and the air was explosive with cries of anger. The Czar, as was believed on a hint from our King as to the state of feeling in this country I and the best way to meet it for a time, sent a telegram of great regret at the occurrence. The people, however, were in no mood for slow negotiation, and there was national pressure behind the Government's demand for prompt apology and a promise that the officers con- cerned should be punished. Ships were mobilised, the men in the dockyards were set working night and day, and the situation became critical. What added to the difficulty was that the Premier had an engagement to speak at Southampton on the Friday of the same week, and something definite was expected of him. The Russian Government was unwilling to allow itself to be dictated to as to the punishment of its naval officers; and, moreover, it was said in St. Petersburg that the fleet had done what it did because it had been attacked by Japanese torpedo-boats. Happily M. Cambon, the French Ambassador, as a friend of both parties, was able to relieve the tension. His fittings to and from Downing-street, Lansdowne House, and the French Embassy were only in part related. On the Friday morning London was very excited, although a whisper on the previous night had given the thought that , a way out had been found. A dramatic incident was that while the Cabinet Council was proceeding the Russian' Ambassador dashed up Whitehall in a- hansom cab with despatches to see the Fofeign Secretary. Mr. Balfour, at the close, went over to Buckingham Palace in response to a message from the Queen, and so lost his intended. train. The Premier withheld public disclosure of what had happened until he faced his audience at Southampton. Then it came out that the Czar ii-nd the Russian Government had formally expressed profound regret, and that it had been agreed the matter should be referred to an inquiry by repre- sentatives of nations under The Hague Con- vention. These were subsequently appointed .as under: one by Great Britain one by Russia, one by France, one by the United States, and one by the Emperor of Austria. The terms of the reference were slightly modified, so as to leave the fixing of responsi- bility entirely open. Admiral Rozhestvensky afterwards admitted that one of his ships had fired on one of their own, under the idea that it was a Japanese torpedo-boat. The fact had been very well, known, inasmuch as several Russian sailors were injured and a chaplain was _killed • but it had not until then been officially acknowledged. This was no doubt the origin of the whole trouble.</p>
        <p>cruiser Variag and the gunboat Korietz. Japan's fleet had started from Nagasaki im- j mediately on war being declared, and part of it, under Admiral Urin, was deputed to attendl to whatever . force might be at Chemulpho. The Korietz, cruising outside the harbour, sighted the Japanese and fired at them. Thereupon Urin sent notice to the Russiansl to leave the harbour, intimating that he would enter it and engage them unless they did so.l Several European warships were in the harbour, and the commanders of these, headed by the captain of his Majesty's ship Talbot, sent notice to the Japanese that no infringe- ment of the neutrality of the port would be permitted. International complications were avoided by the Russians deciding gallantly to fight. In the action which followed both Russian ships were severely punished, and sank on returning to the harbour. Meanwhile, Admiral Togo with the main body of the fleet had gone to Port Arthur, and the same night delivered a torpedo attack, which was completely successful, and by which four Russian battleships and three cruisers were disabled, and a blow struck from which the Port Arthur fleet never recovered. The block- ade of Port Arthur was afterwards maintained, and two attempts were made, with only partial success, to close the harbour by sinking merchantmen. Meanwhile, General Kuropatkin had been appointed to command the Russian Army of the East; and General Kuroki, with a large Japanese force, had encamped on the Wiju. On April 13th, the Russian battle- ship Petropavlovsk, with Admiral Makharoff and all on board, including the famous painter Verestchagin, was sunk by a Japanese mine. On May Day, Kuroki forced the passage of the Yalu, defeating General Sassulitch with great loss. On May 27th, a Japanese force which had landed on the Liaotung Peninsula to invest Port Arthur, defeated the Russians at Kinchau and captured Nan-shan ; and on June 15th Baron Stackelberg, who had been detached by Kuropatkin with a considerable force to relieve Port Arthur, was totally defeated by General Oku at Telissu, with a loss of 7,000 men, and driven northward. The Vladivostock squadron of three cruisers made several sorties, and sank some Japanese transports. A great Japanese army, under the supreme command of Marshal Oyama, formed a semicircle, , stretching,. roughly, from Niuchwang to the road leading from the Yalu to Liao-yang, the right wing being under General Kuroki, the centre under General Nodzu, and the left under General Oku. On August 3rd, the Japanese investing Port Arthur under General Nogi drove the Russians to the inner defences, and the real siege began. A week later, the Port Arthur fleet, acting on instructions from Admiral Skrydloff at Vladivostock, attempted to escape, but was intercepted by Togo and defeated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>When 'the year, opened the War-cloud was already gathering in the Far East. Admiral Alexeieff had just received from his Imperial master the supreme power as "Viceroy of the East," with the command of all the Russian forces on sea and land beyond Siberia. The negotiations between St. Petersburg and Tokio had been protracted mainly through the indis- position of the Czar's advisers to give the definite assurances demanded by the Emperor of Japan that the integrity of Korea would not be interfered with, and that Manchuria would, in accordance with the repeated promises of the Russian Government made since the suppression of the Boxer outbreak in 1900, be restored without delay to China. The first shots wee() fired on February Bth. At Cheniulpho were two Russian vessels. the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>MANCHURIAN CAMPAIGN,</head>
        <p>THE MANCHURIAN CAMPAIGN,</p>
        <p>• The Vladivostock squadron, trying to effect a juncture with Witgeft, was intercepted by Kamimura, and the cruiser Rurik was sent to the bottom, the Rossia and Gromoboi return- ing to their base in a shattered condition. Late in August Oyama began to advance northwards, drawing his wings together on Liao-yang. At that place Kuropatkin gave battle; but after a blo?dy struggle, in which the losses on both sides were very heavy, was compelled to evacuate the town, and conducted a masterly retreat on Ta-ling. Liao-yang was occupied by Oyama, who pushed forward his right wing and occupied the Yentai coal mines, fortifying his front. From September 19th to ;the 30th Nogi delivered a fierce attack on Port Arthur, gallantly defended by General Stoessel, which gained him considerable ground. In the opening week of October, Kuropatkin issued a .bombastic proclamation, announcing h;s intention of relieving Port Arthur and thiving the Japanese out of Manchuria. He immediately advanced against Oyama; but after a terrible battle on the Sha-ho River, lasting for nine days and costing some 80,000 casualties on both sides, he was compelled again to retreat. During November the armies rested; but towards the end of the month General Nogi, who had made steady prbgresa against the Russian works at Port Arthur, captured 203-Metre Hill, an im- portant eminence commanding the harbour. From here the remnant of the Russian fleet was disposed of.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>FOREIGN POLITICS.</head>
        <p>FOREIGN POLITICS.</p>
        <p>The Russo-Japanese business, apart from the North Sea affair, has touched us but indirectly. In February King Edward issued a proclama- tion formally enjoining his subjects to remain neutral. The seizure and searching of a number of British ships by the Russians, however, caused much anxiety in maritime circles, and threatened our Eastern over-sea trade. In July the Russian Volunteer Fleet, which had passed through the Dardanelles flying the merchant flag, stopped and seized the P. and 0. steamer Malacca, and took it to Algiers with a Russian prize crew. This brought matters to a head; out the Russian Government, on strong repre- sentations, not only gave up the vessel, but ageed to recall the fleet. As the course of the latter was not known in St. Petersburg, the Czar gave authority for a message to be delivered by English cruisers, which eventually performed this curious commission in the Indian Ocean. There were other causes of irritation of a similar character, though some- what smaller. Just before the prorogation Mr. Balfour delivered what was by far his most statesmanlike speech of the session, reassuring the British shippers, and at the same time making conciliatory allowance for the Russian point of view. The contraband question came up in another way late in the autumn. The open talk that German colliers were shipping from Wales—under cover of private consignment-L.-coals for the Baltic Rest brought a semi - official protest from the Japanese. Lord Lansdowne promptly re - issued a warning, and some colliers were detained for explanations. A sensational incident was the report in the Press about this time that two British citizens (the Hon. J. RoChe and Mr. Sinnett) had succeeded in .purchasing, ostensibly for America, from Messrs. Yarrow's shipyard a vessel called the Caroline, and taking it to the Russian port of Libau, where it could be refitted as a Russian ship of war. The story ran that this was done for a wager, but summonses were issued for the matter to be investigated at Bow-street. Qur own war engagements in the year have been small. The chase of the Mad Mullah in Somaliland was officially declared to have served its purpose when he had removed himself a certain distance, and an expedition rather costly for its size, both in money and physique, given up, the force of troops being reduced to the minimum which is deemed necessary to guard against further risings. The Thibetan peaceful mission at one time threatened to be a more serious affair. The small military body iound itself opposed by 2,000 natives' who spened fire upon them; but these people knew little about the use of firearms, and 'were scattered, with a loss on their side of 300 men. Eventually relief troops under General Macdonald got through to the sacred city of Ll!assa ; and terms were made which included a re-acknowledgment of our commercial rights, and the gradual payment of an indemnity. Mr. Lyttelton's proposal to introduce Chinese indentured labour into the Transvaal stirred the House of Commons very much. The Liberals fought this vigorously, and with a buoyancy which the by-elections increased, and there was much sharp talking across the floor. Major Seely and Mr. Churchill (then on the Unionist side) did not spare the assault from below the gangway; and a highly dramatic scene occurred when Major Seely announced that he should resign his seat in order to get a renewed vote of confidence from his con- stituents as to the course he had taken. He was returned again unopposed.</p>
        <p>THE FISCAL CONTROVERSY.</p>
        <p>Parliament met amid general expressions ol opinion of great uncertainty as to whother Mr. 13alfour woUld be able to keep his party</p>
        <p>together. On the eve of the opeying the. Premiez was seized with illness—a sharp attack of influenza—and this seemed at the time to add to the embarrassment, though in truth it helped him, by bringing him a rally of personal sympathy when he recovered, as well as relieving him from a searching cross- examination for the first few weeks of the swing. Mr. Akers-Douglas was Deputy Loader, and admittedly was not in a position to declare the attitude of the Government. So time was gained. Mr. Chamberlain appeared very little on the scene. The com- paratively sudden death of his old friend and supporter, Mr. Powell Williams, who was taken ill while leaving the House of Commons one Friday afternoon, and passed away in Westminster Hospital, hastened his departure for a rest in Egypt; and he did not return until near the close. During the year the Government lost the support of eight members by secession, viz., Mr. Winston Churchill, Mr. Ivor Guest, Colonel Kemp, Sir John Dickson-Poynder, Mr. T. W. Russell, Major Seely, Mr. J. iVilson (Falkirk) and Mr. J. W. Wilson, of Worcestershire. On the other hand, they gained by secession Major Irameson, the Nationalist member for West The eight all declared themselves Free Traders; but in some cases the change was also determined by the Chinese labour policy. Meanwhile there was a good deal of organisa- tion and counter-organisation in the country. Lengthy correspondence between the Duke of Devonshire and Mr. Chamberlain as to the attitude of the Liberal Unionist Association under the new conditions ended in the Duke's ' retirement; and he was followed by the other Unionist Free Traders. Mr. Chamberlain was elected President and Lord Lansdowne and the Earl of Selborne Vice-Presidents, the ' three addressing a great meeting in the Albert Hall. Mr. Chamberlain also spoke at meetings at the Guildhall, in the City of London, at Welbeck, Luton, and, just before Christmas, after his return from a holiday in Italy, at a meeting in Mile End, besides attending several public dinners, including a memorable complimentary banquet to him on his birthday. The Duke of Devonshire made answer in the City, and addressed several other meetings, notably one in Lancashire. He also, late in the autumn, called together Liberal Unionst Free Traders to a meeting at Devonshire House to form a club, whose influential character can be seen at a glance at its roll. During/ the year, too, the Tariff Commission was busy, and in the autumn Mr. Balfour declared himself in favour of a Colonial Conference.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>k 2</head>
        <p>k 2</p>
        <p>tR074.-,47:</p>
        <p>DOMESTIC LEGISLATION.</p>
        <p>The Statute-book is the smallest of any for a long period of years. The chief matter of controversy was the Licensing Bill. Here, again, the Liberals (with irregular help from the Nationalists) worked unitedly in resistance, and had some help from Church of England Temperance Unionists. The principle of the Act is that it provides a scheme for the local authorities whereby they can reduce the number of existing public-houses, with compensation to the brewers and others affected. The compen- sation money is to be drawn from additional taxation of present licences' the gain of the brewers, on the other hand, being that exist- ing licences are now recognised by law as property which cannot be taken away without compensation, except on account of misconduct. The trade welcomed the bill, but some of the C.E.T.S. supportors were with the Opposition in wishing to put a time limit on the "property recognition" of old licences. The bill was closured by compartment, and in the Upper House the Bishops again attempted in vain to get a time limit. One other bill—the Welsh Defaulting Authorities Measure -- was also dosured by compartment. Mr. Balfour's use 'of the instrument here was answered by a refusal at a certain stage by the Welsh Nonconformist members to take any further part in the proceedings. They walked out in a body, and the official Liberals, with Mr. Asquith at their head, joined with them. The Aliens Bill, a large and elaborate measure, did not go to Committee upstairs until the Session was far advanced, and its reception settled its fate. A Scotch Education Bill had also to be withdrawn, after much time had been spent on it. The Shop Hours (Early Closing) Bill, which is a "permissive" measure, is one of the few enactments.</p>
        <p>ARMY REFORM.</p>
        <p>The year, like so many of its immediate predecessors, has been very productive of "Reforms of the Army." As early as January 21st Mr. Arnold-Forster, Secretary for War, outlined a scheme of reorganisation. On February Ist the War Office Reconstitution Committee, consisting of Lord Esher, Sir John Fisher, and Sir George Sydenham Clarke, issued its report, recommending the abolition of the post of Commander-in- Chief, and the substitution therefor of the office of Inspector-General of the Forces, with strictly limited inspectorial duties, and the construction of a General Staff, under the name of the "Army Council," to administer the Army. The recommenda- tions of the committee were immediately put into force. Lord Roberts resigned, the Duke of Connaught was appointed Inspector-General, and the Army Council was constituted. On May 28th the Commission on the Auxiliary Forces, of which the Duke of Norfolk was chairman, reported recommending certain reforms in the Volunteers and Militia, and advocating the adoption of the principle of universal service by the country. The report raised a storm of controversy in the country, and on June 2nd in the House of Commons Mr. Arnold - Forster announced that the Government would have nothing to do with conscription. During July Mr. Arnold-Forster published his Army Scheme, which abolished the Army Corps system set up by Mr. Brodrick, and foreshadowed the introduction of a new condition of servicl3 on home and foreign stations, as well as great reductions of the Militia and Volunteers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>NAVY.</head>
        <p>THE NAVY.</p>
        <p>• The year was fruitful of events as far as the Navy was concerned. On February 18th the submarine Al was sunk during operations at Portsmouth, her crew being all killed. In the summer the Mediterranean Fleet became active in consequence of the stoppage of British ships by the Russian Volunteer cruisers. In October the Navy gave a gratifying exhibition of its state of efficiency in connection with the incident in the North Sea; and on December 10th Lord Selborne published an important Memorandum announcing changes in the dis- tribution and organisation of the fleet. By this document the name ef the Home Fleet was changed to the "Channel Fleet," and the Channel Fleet to the "Atlantic Fleet " ; the strength of the Mediterranean Fleet was reduced from twelve to eight battleships, and that of the new Channel Flpet raised to twelve battleships; while three cruiser squadrons and a Particular Service squadron were constituted. Great reforms were made in the Fleet Reserve and in the training of cadets and youths. On Trafalgar Day Sir John Fisher became First Sea Lord of the Admiralty, being succeeded as Commander-in-Chief at Portsmouth by Sir Archibald L. Douglas. Sir Edward Seymour was promoted Admiral of the Fleet in December, and Sir L. A. Beaumont was appointed to succeed him at Plymouth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>RELIGIOUS, PERSONAL, AND SOCIAL.</head>
        <p>RELIGIOUS, PERSONAL, AND SOCIAL.</p>
        <p>The judgment of the House of Lords, hand- ing over the property of the United Presby- terian Churches buildings and capital representing ten millions sterling—to twenty- seven "Wee, Kirks," is fresh in the public memory. The decision was by five votes to two, and reversed one given by the Scotch Court, on the construction of the Trust Deeds as-to doctrine, the matter arising through the union of the United Presbyterians with the Free Church of Scotland. From that uhion the twenty-seven churches named had dis- sented. The •Lord Chancellor's deliverance, in its grasp and lucidity, was a most masterly</p>
        <p>performance for a man in his eightieth year. But though its severe interpretatiou of the Trust was 'generally accepted as sound, that did not lessen the indignant feeling at the situation which was created. The Wee Kirkers, however, declined invitations to au. agreement on fresh terms, and in December a Royal Commission was appointed, with Lord Elgin as Chairman, to report with a view to legislation, a Commissioner being also directed' to take such Steps as were possible in the meantime to meet the stai eof affairs. The visit of the Archbishop of Canterbury to the United States and Canada, and the King's command to General Booth to Buckingham Palace, in token of sympathy with his social work, mark two departures from the ordinary religious course of the year. General Booth's motor-car tour through England elicited, too, in a remarkable manner the regard of people of all classes for what he has been doing to improve the moral wellbeing of the people. The strength of conviction of. the Free Churches was evidenced by the growth of the Passive Resistance move- ment. The number of summonses had risen by the end of the year to close on 38,000, and some seventy persons had suffered imprisonment rather than voluntarily pay the Education Rate which would assist denominational schools. The agitation as to Ritualism in the Church of England at last, after many years, secured from Mr. Balfour the appointment of a Royal Commission to inquire into alleged illegal practices. The Lord Wardenship of the Cinque Ports, with the occupation of Weimer Castle, was conferred on Lord Curzon on his return home from India • but, alas! soon after taking up residence there Lady Curzon was stricken with illness, which, with its extreme antemia nearly proved fatal, but from which she has now happily almost completely recovered, Lord Curzon having been able to go back to India to take up a fresh term as Viceroy. Captain Scott and the other members of the Antarctic Expedition, returning at the end of two years' exploration, had a hearty welcome back and compliments. The distress among the unem- ployed became so acute in London during the autumn that Mr. Long called a meeting of the local authorities to organise work, the money to be supplied from a Mansion House Fund, which the Lord Mayor opened in the first week in December. In the provinces notably at Leeds, there were some slight disturbances; but the conditions were so exceptional that they were treated with great forl:c:arance, and quietly subsided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
