        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>LAW AND JUSTICE</head>
        <p>LAW AND JUSTICE</p>
        <p>The leading event in the history of justice during the year was of course the remarkable, and, happily, exceptional, Beck case. Mr. Adolf Beck, a Norwegian by birth, was charged during April with perpetrating certain frauds upon women in London. He was brought before Sir. Forrest Fulton, and subse- quently Sir William Grantham tried him at the QM Bailey. Beck told an extraordinary story in defence. He declared that he was the victim of a mistake on the part of the police; that he had already served five and a-half years on a similar charge, of which he was not guilty; and that the guilty man was. one John Smith, who had been convicted in 1877 of frauds on women. The matter was then taken up by the newspapers, and on July 29th; Mr. Justice Grantham having postponed sentence meanwhile, Beck was granted a free "pardon" for crimes he had not committed. John Smith had in the meantime been appre- hended, and was sentenced for the crimes. In response to the outcry which was raised, a Commission was appointed to inquire into the circumstances of the case; and the report, which was issued in November, blamed the Home Office for disregarding Mr. Beck's petition, and recommended certain changes, to give prisoners an opportunity to prove their innocence in cases in which mistaken identity is alleged. Early in January Mr. Whitaker Wright, a well-known financier, was put upon his trial on charges connected with the promo- tion of certain companies. He was convicted and sentenced on January 26th, and on removal from the dock committed suicide by taking poison, which, .he had managed to conceal. Another financier was arrested later in the year, - Mr. Ernest Terah Hooley, who, with Mr. Lawson, was charged with defrauding a Mr. Paine. The trial lasted for many days, and was marked by some extraordinary scenes. Mr. Hooley was acquitted, but Mr. Lawson, was sentenced to twelve months' hard labour, subject to appeal on point of law. Mrs. Maybrick, who was sentenced in 1889 for poisoning her husband at Liverpool, was released from Aylesbury Prison during the year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>ILLUSTRIOUS DEAO.</head>
        <p>THE ILLUSTRIOUS DEAO.</p>
        <p>Several grand old men, whose careers had gone almost through the last century, passed away. The Duke of Cambridge, cousin of Queen Victoria, ex-Commander-in-Chief of the Forces, and warm friend of military charit- able institutions, passed away in March in his eighty-fifth year. There was a State service in Westminster Abbey, but the people were still more touched by the burial of his Royal Highness by the side of his morganatic wife in a London cemetery—testimony as it was to his lifelong fidelity of sentiment for her, though, through not being of Royal blood, she never had his status. Two recipients of that unique distinction the Order of Merit also passed away—Admiral Sir Harry Keppel, Father of the Fleet, ninety-four years of age, and Mr. G. F. Watts, our greatest painter of portraits and allegorical subjects' who lived up to his precept, the "utmost for the highest." He was eighty-six years of age, but had a marvellous piece of statuary in the Royal Academy, completed but a few weeks before his death. Among the Parliamentarians the removal of Sir William Harcourt, the Nestor of English Liberals, and ext Chancellor and Leader of the Opposition, stirred a crowd of thoughts of splendid political battles in which he had figured; Lord Northbrook, ex-Viceroy- of India; the Earl Of Cork, ex-Master of the, Horse; Lord Ridley, ex-Home Secretary; Lord: Hardwicke (one of the most amiable as well as diligent of young Conservative Ministers) ;; ' Mr. Seale Hayne, the leader of West-Country Liberals; bluff Sir William Allan, whose: rugged eloquence used to cast a spell on the. Commons; Mr. Spencer Charrington, who in his eighty-sixth year did all the divisions in the twenty-six hours' sitting; Mr. James Lowther (ex-Chief Secretary for Ireland), and Sir William Rattigan, the first Parliamen- tarian to become a victim to a motor-car acci- dent—these are a few of the political names. that will recur. Literature lost Sir Leslie: Stephen, first' editor of the "Dictionary of National Biography " ; Sir Edwin Arnold,. author of "The Light of Asia " ; Sir Henry 'Stanley, dauntless traveller; and Mr. Herbert Spencer, 'the world-famed philosopher,, whose- reminiscences, given to the public soon after his death, was one of the few great books of the year. Lord Hobhouse, Mr. Justice Byrne, Mr. Justice Wright and Mr. Inderwick (for so many years leader in the Divorce Court), are among the great lawyers departed; Sir John, Robinson, late manager of the Daily News, and the originator of the war correspondent;. Mr. Clement Scott, dramatic critic; Dean Hole, of Rochester, famous for his wit and his rose-growing; Bishop Bardesley, of Carlisle;. and Dr. Perowne, ex-Bishop of Worcester; "Ir. Wilson Barrett, dramatist and favourite, 'actor; Mr. Dan Leno, the King's Jester, as he was called after his command to Sandring- ham; Mr. Herbert Campbell, his quondam companion at Drury Lane; Mr. John Hollings- head, of Gaiety fame and author of the phrase, "the sacred flame of burlesque " ; Dr. Samuel Smiles, author of " Self-Help " ; Sir Henry ' Thompson, the physician who went motoring at tile cr e age' of eighty-three;l an Madame Antoinette Sterling, the songstress, must also be included. There was a mild sensation at th. 3 death of ex-President Kruger on the French littoral; but it made no difference in diplomacy, and permission was given for the body to be taken to Pretoria.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
