      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <p>r _ - . -</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>JJOCKBY.—Ii one firm of manufalturers more than -1-1- another keeps abreast of the times in the matter of 4mPlements for Sport, that firm is GEO- G. BUSSE &amp; Co• of London, who are leading the way again with their Hockey Clubs and Balls which bear their well-known trade 'mark. Ask the local Sports Dealer for G.G.B. &amp; Co." Special Hockey List. If ,not procurable apply to 36 &amp; 38, Queen Victoria it., London. Factory—Peckham, S.R. (6 0.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday afternoon the servant of Mr. Petherick solicitor, Exmouth, heard the sound of firemen in the boothouse, and, on calling neighbours, they found Mr. George Axon, a retired captain from the Merchant Service, shot through the head, the back part of which was blown away, both barrels of the gun having been • discharged. A very intering relic of Roman London will shortly be placed in the Guildhall Museum. It consists of an amphora and urn, recently brought to light in the Minories, during excavations at a depth of fourteen feet or more below the surface.</p>
        <p>Write at once to FRANK SUGG, Lord Street, Liverpool. England's Premier Outfitter, For Catalogue of Football, Hockey, Golf, Exercisers, Boxing Gloves, &amp;c., &amp;c. Football King Bail .. 9/6 Kiynker Ball 6/6 gunior League . 5/- -lion this Paper and receive an Annual by Return Post.</p>
        <p>4,411 Mlot JO - Men,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>First Division.</p>
        <p>RESULTS OF SATURDAY'S MATCHES.</p>
        <p>- - G. T. P. G. T. P. Br'ghton Rangers 1 2 8 Wigan Leigh 1 3 11 Salford 0 2 6 Oldham Bradford 1 3 11 Batley Warrington Hunslet Hull Kingston R.... 2 0 4 Leeds St. Helens 0 4 9 Runcorn Widnes WARRINGTON v. WAKEFIELD TRINITY. This match was played at Warrington on Saturday before about 4,000 people. Warrington were at full strength, but Wakefield were short of Metcalfe and McPhail. Warrington started very strongly and in the first minute Duckworth took the ball to the line. Then from a scrum the home backs did some finepassing, Fish 'finally running over at the corner, Preston failing at goal Wakefield pressed after this, but Hallam beat them back The home backs again got going, but Dickenson failed to take a pass. The Wakefield forwards excelled in I some loose work, and clever dribbling gave Hallam some trouble, but the home custodian played well. Then Harris was given a chance, but Measor was safe, and maintained his reputation. Half-time score: Warring- ton, one try (three points) ; Wakefield Trinity, nil. Just befere the interval Isherwood was hurt, and was not able to take his place when the game was restarted. Wakefield were the first to make headway,ani dribbling down the left wing Dickenson was hard pressed to cope with the invasion, The fog was now fast enveloping the ground, and the players on the far side were scarcely discernible. In the closing stages Warrington attacked strangly, Fish and Lunt both being nearly over, but Wakefield set up a good defence, and the score at the close was: Warrington, one try (three points) ; Wakefield Trinity, nil. WIDNES v. HALIFAX. _</p>
        <p>• • • --- - These teams met at Widnes on Saturday, and after a very keen struggle the homesters won by six points to nil. In the opening half there was no score, and play WS on the whole fairly equally divided, Little twice failed to lower the Widnes goal from penalties, whilst Taylor and Wailing were pulled up on the Halifax line when almost over. In the second half play was more in favour of the homesters. Shortly after three-quarter time Tilley carried the leather over the Halifax lines at the corner. He was robbed at a critical moment, but McDonald raced up and scored to the dead ball line. Tilley made a grand attempt to attempt to improve. A short time later Wailing got over and scored and was injured in doing so. No goal followed and the game ended :—Widnes, two tries (six points); Halifax, nil. ST. HELENS v. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>--- Played at St. Helens on Saturday, before a fair attendance of spectators. St. Helens won the toss, and Tomlinson started for Runcorn, play eventually settling down in the Runcorn half. Traynor marked, and his shot at goal just went wide. Runcorn dribbled away, and when a certain try seemed inevitable Carney saved on the line. Traynor, Liversage, and Johnson passed and repassed, the latter player crossing the line. The try was disallowed by the referee, he claiming that the try was scored three yards short of the line. Shortly after this Mavity scored from a rash of .forwards on the Runcorn line. Jones failed to improve. Just before the interval Johnson scored for St. Helens, Rothwell fail- ing. Half-time score: St. Helens, two tries; Runcorn, .nil. Resuming, play for some time was even, until one of the Runcorn backs kicked across to Moran, who 'failed to hold the leather, and the result being that Creevey dashed up and scored the third try for St. Helens. Traynor failed at the goal kick. Runcorn, with rushing tactics, entered the home half, and Hesketh intercepting a St. Helens pass, had little difficulty in scoring an easy try which Butterworth improved. Result: St. Helens, three tries (nine points) ; Runcorn one goal and one try (five points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>BOXING DAY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>BOXING DAY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON DRAW AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>The first meeting this season between the above teams took place on Monday at St. Helens in fine weather. Warrington were short of Isherwood, for whom Brooks appeared, while the Saints were at full strength. About a thousand people accompanied the Warrington team, the gate being about 4000. Mr. Moore was referee and the teams were : St. Helens—Warner, Liversage, Traynor, Creevey, and Jones,Johnson and Rothwell,Briers,Corney,Lee,Mavitty, Mooney, Thompson, Prescott and Manson. Warrington—Hallam, Fish, Brooks, Dickenson and Harris, Hockenhall and Duckworth, Boardman, Thomas, Belton, Shugars, Swift, Harmer, Cook and Jolley. Preston started and after exchange Brooks found touch. A penalty to Warrington was the first thing of note, Fish kicking across and Dickenson put the ball in touch near the line. A penalty gave the Saints relief and a dribble gained further ground. Then Fish got away and passed across Harris galloping over, but being brought back for a forward pass. A clever cross kick by Hockenhall put Warrington on the attack again, the same player being pulled down on the line from the next pack. Then War- rington got away again, Preston running over from Fish's cross kick, only to be recalled. Fish had a penalty in front of goal, but failed. A long push by St. Helens putYthem on the attack, Hallam saving finely 'St. Helens were nearly over a moment later, Hallam bringing Jones down. Then Fish got away at full speed but laid was out in a tackle. St. Helens`-again ran down, Harris marking nicely. St. Helens were -persistently however, and Hallam had a busy time. By good kicking Warrington resumed the offensive. A free kick to 'Warrington was taken by Preston who placed wide. St. Helens once retaliated, Liversage being pushed in touch at the corner. The homesters now had a free kick, Jones making a poor attempt. Warrington next attacked and carried the ball over,but again the try was disallowed. Warrington still pressed but were stopped at the corner. Half time no score. Traynor restarted and after exchanges Fish raced down touch and nearly beat Warner for possession. A bad kick by Duckworth gave Traynor possession, but Warrington were soon down again, Fish being rolled over at the corner. The attack was persistent but fruitless. Warrington fell away in back play and were penalised in front of goal, Traynor placing outside. Harris was next at fault and St. Helens attacked, only -to be driven back by Hallam. Then Warrington nearly lost the game, a five yards scrimmage following. War- rington cleared with a penalty and Fish went centre only to see his side hard pressed again. The Warrington forwards relieved finely and along punt by Preston put them on the attack. Play was of an even character, and • the match ended in a pointless draw. LEEDS V. BRADFORD. _ _ _</p>
        <p>At Headingley in foggy weather, before 15,000 spectators. The game was exciting but not brilliant from start to finish. Only one score was registered, this being obtained 'by Leeds in the first three minutes, Evans forcing his way over after getting the ball from Llewellyn. Result :—Leeds, three point; Bradford none.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>HULL v. KINGSTON ROVERS.</head>
        <p>HULL v. KINGSTON ROVERS.</p>
        <p>At Hull,before 14,000 spectators. Starks kicked off,and the Rovers had the best of the opening exchanges. Madley intercepted a pass and scored a second try for the Rovers, and Carmicnael kicked a goal. Mullineaux</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>Soap News</head>
        <p>Soap News</p>
        <p>Fells - Naptha saves half the labour of washing and half the wear on clothes. Other soap is an expense.</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptha 39 Wilson street London C</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <p>added another try, Carmichael friling at goal. The Rovers showed themselves to be the better men, and their win was merited. Result :—Hull Kingston Rovers eight points ; Hull, none. BROUGHTON RANGERS DEFEAT RUNCORN. I This match on the Rangers ground on Monday was won by the Rangers with two goals and two tries (10 points) against Runcorn's one goal and one try (5 points). The try scorers for the Rangers were Harris and Harry, • but the fog was too thick to see who scored Runcorn's try. The recent displays of Runcorn did not inspire much confidence in the team's ability to overcome the Rangers' strong combination, but the form they dis- played was quite surprising. For forty minutes in the second half they kept the Rangers at bay, and ultimately broke through their defence. They were, however, out- classed as regards back play, but the forwards held a decided advantage over the Rangers, and by smart following up and loose dribbling they often frustrated the Rangers attempts at passing. OLDHAM v. SWINTON. At Oldham, before 10,000 spectators. Oldham had the best of the game all through. In the first half Topham and Lewis scored tries, Ferguson failinc, twice at goal. Afterwards Dixon and M`Lean each scored, the latter's being a very fine effort behind the posts, and Ferguson kicked a goal. The only points !scored by Swinton were a penalty goal, kicked by Gartrell, and Oldham won easily by 14 points to two. HALIFAX v. HUNSLET. At Hanson-lane, under very unfavourable conditions Goldthorpe kicked a penalty goal immediately after the start. Halifax had several openings, but failed to cross the line. From a mark Goldthorpe addeda second goal. After the interval the game was keenly contested. Halifax scored a try, which was unconverted. Hunslet reached the Halifax line, but were penalised. Result: —Hunslet, four points; Halifax, three. HUNSLET v. BRADFORD. The Hunslet Club gained their third success within four days at Hunslet on Tuesday, their opponents being Bradford. Alfred Goldthorpe in the first half dropped a goal, and kicked another from a try by his brother Walter. Albert Goldthorpe later scored a try and dropped another goal, and placed a fourth from a try by Whitley. Result :—Hunslet, four goals and three tries (17 points) ; Bradford, nothing.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION. WARRINGTON " A " ST. HELENS "A."</p>
        <p>This combination match was decided at Warrington on Monday and attracted a large holiday crowd. From the start it was evident St. Helens had no chance against the locals who played a vigorous game and who combined well. The visitors' line was crossed after a few minutes' play and for the remainder of the game Warrington had matters all their own way, running out winners by 33 points to nil. The scorers were—Kenyon (3 tries), Short (2 tries), Savage, Forster, Roberts and Morrison (I try each), and Knight (3 goals).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>ST. VITUS' DANCE CURED</head>
        <p>ST. VITUS' DANCE CURED</p>
        <p>By Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>From St. Vitus' dance to Paralysis and Locomotor Ataxy, these pills have effected %lcoders in nervous dis- orders. They enrich and purify the blood, and feed the starved nerves through the blood. Sciatica, Rheuma- tism, Eczema, Rickets, Atmmia, Decline and Consump- tion have been cured repeatedly, as well as the ailments of ladies. Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., Holborn-viaduct, London, will forward, post free, one box for 2s. 9d., or six for 13s. 9d. ; but there is no difficulty in purchasing of any dealer, if care be taken to avoid substitutes.</p>
        <p>Daily growing more common in school children, St. Vitus' Dance is one of the most difficult disorders to cure. Ordinary medicine often fails to touch it. The great success of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills in this ailment makes them important to parents. Mrs. Owen, 24, Bath-street, Newton Heath, Manches- ter, mother of a child cured, said "My little girl had a slight accident in the Physical Drill lesson. For a time r;a7"v•-..., I kept her at home, and -,,---,(- ---.—,, .....,-._-•4 when she went to school ,41'• - ' . ------:,.....--:-.., again the poor little thing could not do the A i , slightest thing without t _ trembling with nervous- -4 ,7-----,,. -1 ness. Sometimes she -4---'-- \(' ''• nearly ' fainted. The School Board inspector :- -,-.,, el/44% saw that she had St. --7. - -, -;- A ' Vs' Vitus' dance. Her arms andlneck began to twitch; ----,..„_..., . 0i,;(6 *A,..._ it was pitiful to see her ' She would flush about )I,A, the face, and was often " 1I " 'll.-"-sick. She never went put to play with other children. She was very innie Owen, cured of weak, too, and I did not St. Vitus' Dance by know what to do. St. Dr. Williclaue Pink ilill• Vitus' dance is a terrible affliction. The sufferer is moving, twitching, starting, all the time. You can nothing but watch her waste away, never at rest. That was Minnie's state before I gave her Dr. Williams' Pink Pills far Pale People. She was better after the very first dose, and from that time she kept on steadily improving. I have great faith in Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. For ten years I have seen them effect many wonderful cures,even more marvellous than Minnie's."</p>
