      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, December 31, 1904.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 31, 1904.</p>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>IF poverty makes a man groan, wealth makes him yawn. MAN'S inhumanity to man makes countless lawyers happy. THE lender frequently finds that borrowing dulls the edge Of memory. SOME men who *believe in the "missing link" idea raise an awful howl when their cuff button can't be found. "'How did Bluffer so easily win Miss Goldbag's heart?" "He sent her 22 roses on her 30th birthday. IT is quite true that virtue is its own reward, but that does not seem to be a. sufficient induce- ment for most people. UNCLE GEORGE: "Do you really think you love the girl?" Harry: "Love her? Why, I actually enjoy her mother's company." CLARA : "Mr. Nicefellow said my face was classic. What did he mean? Dora: "Oh, any- thing old is classic, you know-" "My character," said an alderman, who had cleared himself from a charge of bribery, "my character, sir, is like my boots—all the brighter for blacking." "REMEMBER," once said a distinguished strate- gist, "that a defeat which you can turn to account is often to be preferred to a victory which you cannot." TEACHER—" What is a fort?" Pupil —"A place for soldiers to live in." Teacher—" And a fortress?" Pupil—" A place for saldiers' wives to live in." As Irishman asks why a railway engine is called "She."—" Perhaps it's on account of the horrible noise it makes when it tries to whistle." "DOES your husband look after things while you are away, Mrs. Dwiggs ? " "He takes care of the chickens and children, but I always send my house plants over to mother's." LITTLE WIFE.—" I saved 30s. to-day." Loving Husband: "You are an angel. How ?" Little Wife "I saw a perfectly lovely easy chair that I knew you'd like, and I didn't buy it. • "Au," softly hummed the fly, as the sleeping i victim restlessly turned over n his bed, "the other cheek! He must be a good man !" And the grateful insect settled gently down again. TEACHER.—" Johnny, how many different kinds of force are there ? ' Johnny: "Bodily force, mental force, and the police force." BACII.—" Do you think a man who has his way to make ought to get married ? " Dick: " Well, it depends on the wife he gets. Some women, you know, would object to his having his way." "I SEEM to have lost all hold on him," she wailed. The other married lady looked sympa- thetic. "You are not referring, she asked, "to Isis having shaved off his beard ?" ' "WHAT is a sanguine disposition. Uncle Chris- topher ? " "Sanguine disposition? Well, its your mother, Bobby, thinking she can drive a picture nail into the wall with a banana."- StlE.—" It's strange how the smallest things in life make the biggest difficulties." He : "That's so. Last night, for instance, I could find the house all right, but for the life of me I couldn't find the keyhole." "WILLIE, how did you get along at school to- day ?" " First rate." Now, Willie," his mother I%re:it on severely, don't tell stories. I heard you had to be punished." "Yes, but it didn't hurt as much as usual." PATIENT: "Doctor, I am troubled witheheu- matic pains, caused, I believe, by tbe dampness of a new house. What would you advise me to do?" Doctor (laconically) : "Move." OFFICE Boy: The grocer is downstairs, and wants to know why you didn't answer his letter about last month's bill." Editor: "Tell him he forgot to enclose a stamp." MAMMA: "What is Willie crying about ?" Bridget: "Shure, ma'am, he wanted to go to Tommy Green's. They were having charades, he said, ma'am, and I wasn't sure as he'd had 'em yet." "IT strikes me that invention is the mother of necessity." "How do you make that out?" "Take the bicycle. Nobody needed it before it was invented, and now we couldn't get along vfithout it" "AND what did papa say ? " "Well, I asked him for you and he said, 'Call round in about a month, and I'll try to have her ready.' Now, what in Klondike did he mean by that ? ' " Don't you know? Why, papa made his fortune in a bicycle shop." " l'vE got something nobody else has got," said the suburbanite, who has been experimenting with raising fancy fowls. " What is it ?" I bought a £3O incubator, put £5 worth of eggs in it, and hatched out a blue-bottle fly. I've got the only £35 blue-bottle fly in this or any other country." DEALER: "Now, here's a showcase that will magnify the goods you put into it." Customer: "Great Scott! Then I don't want it. I'm a shoe. dealer, and I want a case for displaying women's shoes." , " PERKIN'S, your wife seems very devoted to her flowers." " Devoted ! Well, sir, many an October night that woman has dragged the blankets off my bed to keep those weazened little geraniums from getting frost-bitten." • "YOUR Majesty," said the chief cook timidly to - the cannibal king, "the new, missionary is en- veloped•in a complete suit of armour from head to foot." "Then send him away," returned his Majesty indifferently, "I never could bear tinned goods." DOCTOR: "Troubled with insomnia, eh? Eat something before going to bed." Patient: "Why doctor, you once told me never to eat anything before going to bed." Doctor (with dignity) : "That, madam, was away back in 1899. science has made great strides since then." SAXON PASSENGER (on Highland coach) : "Of course, you're well acquainted with the country round about here. Do you know Glen Accron ?" Driver: "Ay, wed." Saxon Passenger (who has bought the estate) : "What sort af a place is it?" Driver: "Wee!, if 37e saw the deil tethered on't ye'd just say, Puir brute PARVNOO (at his first swell dinner, and eat- ing consommé for the first time) : "Isn't the soup thin, Mary !" Mrs. Parvnoo (with a sneer) : " Yes ; it's done so you can see the decoration on the bottom of the plate while you are eating it." GUIDE "At this spot you may hear the famous echo that repeats a sound twenty-four times. A man Arent crazy here last year." Tourist : " Crazy ? Why ?" Guide; "His mother-in-law 'called out to him, and— Well, you understand —to hear one's mother-in-law call -twenty-four tunes in succession." "I HAVE just heard that Flasher broke his leg while out cycling yesterday." "That doesn't sur- prise me in the least." "I was told, too' that it was broken in several places." "I don't doubt it, for when Flasher scorches, he goes so fast that he'd probably be in several places before his leg had time to get thoroughly broken." "BUT why don't you settle down? Why do you tramp, tramp, tramp for ever?" "It's this way, ma'a.tn:• I'm under orders. In the battle of Tel- el-Kobir my commandin' officer says, 'Forward, march,' an'. we started. Then he an all the officers was killed, an' no one's ever commanded us to halt. I marches on, on for ever, ma'am, from a sense of duty." "IT'S very funny," said the housewife, "that the potatoes you bring should be so much biggeir,; on the top of the basket than they are at the bot- tom." "Miss," said the honest .farmer, "It comes about this way. P'taters is growin' so fast just now that by the time I get a basketful dug the last ones is ever so much larger than the fust ones." -A DETECTIVE, hearing that at a certain restau- rant game was. being supplied out of season, called and asked to be served with partridges. His order was executed, and after dining with great pleasure he -called the waiter. "Now, waiter, I am from Scotland Yard, and as you have served ' the with game out of season, I intend to make a char:le against this house." "You need not bother, sii,. said the waiter. "Why ?" asked the-detec- tive. "Because, sir, that wasn't partridge you got. sir ; it was only crow !" visiting' London was taken into St. P.,u I's Cathedral. He stared about jihn in amaze, ment. and his astonishment at the magnitude of the building seemed too great for utterance. It was only when he stood under the dome and gazed down the vast nave and up to the dim and misty roof that his admiration at last found vent in words: "My ! what a foine barn this 'ud make !"</p>
        <p>The Northumberland wolf is reported to have been found dead on the Midland line at Cumwhinton Cumberland. .</p>
        <p>The Rev. Richard Lovett, one of the secretaries of the Religions Tract Society, died suddenly on Thursday morning. He formerly held a ministerial charge at Locndale.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>MOTOR CAR FATALITY.</head>
        <p>MOTOR CAR FATALITY.</p>
        <p>MAN RUN OVER IN THE DARK NEAR WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Inquest and Verdict.</p>
        <p>Returning to Warrington .from St. Helens by motor car on Monday evening, Mr. W. H. Bellian, the well- known cycle and motor dealer, Sankey-street, had a trying experience. When passing through the village of Bold the wheels of the car struck some object in the middle of the road. The sudden jerk extinguished the lamps, and Mr. Bellian, who was accompanied by four friends, immediately brought the car to a standstill. The roadway was searched and a man was found lying insensible. He was placed in the car and conveyed to the Warrington Infirmary but died shortly after admis- sion from internal injuries. He was subsequently identified as William Cartledge, toolmaker, Back-lane Penketh.</p>
        <p>The inquest was conducted at the Police Station on Wednesday evening by Mr. S. Brighouse, County Coroner. Mr. P. G. Taylor (Messrs. Henry Greenall and Co.) represented Mr. W. H. Bellian, and Mr. T. S. Steel appeared on behalf of the relatives of the deceased. _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>The Coroner stated the facts of the case as detailed above. He added that, according to the police report, Cartledge left his house at three o'clock on Monday afternoon' and it was stated that he was under the influ- ence of drink. About five o'clock he called at the Butcher's Arms, Bold-road, and having had a glass of beer, left for home. The next that was seen of him was when Mr. Bellian found him in the roadway. Mr. Brig- house informed the jury that he was cycling on the Liverpool-road himself about seven o'clock on Monday night, and he knew all about the conditions of the night and of the road.</p>
        <p>Philip Cartledge, tool maker, Back-lane, Penketh, said deceased was his father., and he was fifty-four years of age. Witness last saw lam alive about eleven o'clock on Sunday night. Deceased left the house on Monday morning before witness got up. The Coroner was informed that no one from the house could tell the jury in what condition deceased was when he again left the house in the afternoon. Deceased's widow was troubled with religious mania, and was not in a fit condition to attend the inquest. Thomas Moss, Knocker Green, Great Sankey, said he went to Cartledge's house on Sunday and stayed there until Tuesday afternoon. Witness last saw deceased between 11 and 12.30 on Monday morning and he was then quite sober. He had nothing to drink with his dinner, He heard deceased leave the house between 3 and 3.30 in the afternoon, but did not see him. The , Coroner: Mrs. Cartledge, says you saw him leave the house, and that you know he was under the influence of drink. Witness: I did not.</p>
        <p>Further questioned witness said deceased took drink in moderation. He was not a big drinker except occa- sionally. By Mr. Steel: Deceased was generally a steady man. He was in the employ of Messrs. Thewlis and Griffiths for a great number of years, and he was not troubled with deafness or shors-sightedness. The sound of his footsteps seemed quite right when he left the house in the afternoon.</p>
        <p>By Mr. Taylor: Deceased went to bed after dinner but witness did not know why. Alice Gallimore, general servant at the Butcher's Arms, said deceased did not come to that house on Christmas Day or on Monday morning. He put in an appearance at 5.15 on Monday evening. Witness could not see that he had had any drink, and after being served with a glass of beer he left at from 5.30 to 5.45. He appeared to be quite sober and walked all right. By Mr. Steel: She did not suppose she would have had any hesitation in serving deceased with more beer, had he asked for it. Deceased was a steady man, and she had never seen him the worse for drink. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Taylor: Do you remember having told anyone that deceased was ejected from this public-house? Witness: No. Sergeant Barlow (Coroner's officer) said witness told him that she led deceased to the door and saw him outside.</p>
        <p>Witness denied that she made that statement and said she was in the tap room when deceased went out. She closed the door after him. . . _ .</p>
        <p>Sergeant Barlow: She told me that she saw him outside. Nurse Dobson said deceased was admitted to the Infirmary at 6.30 and died a quarter of an hour later from internal injuries. In answer to Mr. Taylor, witness said that the man Moss on visiting the Infirmary on Tuesday told her either that deceased was under the infiftence of drink when he left home at three o'clock or that he (Moss) heard that he was. He added that he heard that deceased was seen at 4.50 under the influence of drink. Moss was recalled and said he told Nurse Dobsonthat he had heard some people say that deceased had. been seen under the influence of drink. In answer to Mr. Taylor, Nurse Dobson said deceased had superficial wounds on the head which she did not consider would be inflicted by the motor. It was more likely that a fall would cause them. There were no big external injuries. The Coroner: If there is any,question as to whether the motor killed the man, I will adjourn the inquest to have a post mortem. Mr. Taylor I don't think there can be any doubt that the motor ran over the man. Continuing, he pointed out that if deceasc I was lying in the road there must have been some cause, either that he was drunk or that he had been knocked down by a previous vehicle. His questions were put with the object of elucidating the matter, and he was unable to call the doctor as he was out of town. There were five passengers in the car and none of them saw the deceased, although the lamps were strong ones. The Coroner: If Mr. Bellian's experience was like mine, he must have met the most idiotic people that breath was put into. I cycled to St. Helens and I am I quite certain I was sober. With two broad footpaths on each side, people would deliberately walk into the road. I nearly ran into a man myself, and I was only going at seven miles an hour. I should think there was no part of the night darker than between six and eight. Mr. Steel: If a man was sober and stepped out into the roadway— The Coroner: The man I overtook was either drunk or a fool. I made use of effusive language that I would not repeat to the jury. (Laughter). Mr. W. H. Bellian was then called and said the car was al2 h.-p. Darmcq. Before leaving St. Helens he lit two lamps on the front of the car and one behind. When near the Butcher's Arms he felt the wheels strike some object in the road and the sudden jerk put the lights out. He was only driving at the rate of twelve miles an hour. He pulled up and on going back about 15 yards found deceased lying in the road. Seeing that the man was badly injured he placed him in the car and conveyed him to the Infirmary. The othe- passengers in the car were Messrs. G. H. Smith, F. Barber W. Litton, and F. Garnett. If deceased had been standing up witness must have seen him. Witness had driven all descriptions of cars during the past six years, and he had covered 5,000 or 6,000 miles this year. He had never experienced an accident before. The night in question was extremely dark, and it would have been suicidal to have driven more quickly. Just before the accident he had not seen a man walking on the right hand footpath.He thought the man might have be3n drunk and lurched into the road. By Mr. Steel: Deceased was lying straight across the road. Witness could see 15 clear yards in front of the car. Mr. Steel questioned Mr. Bellian as to the attention he devoted to watching the man on the foot- path. , The Coroner: Surely no one ever suggests that Mr. Bellian on this dark night drove this car along the road and deliberately and wilfully ran into the man. He would be a fool to do it. Mr. Steel: I don't suggest that at all. If he was looking at a man whq was apparently drunk and walk- ing towards him— The Coroner interposed with a question as ,to what Mr. Steel's allegation was. Mr. Steel: Negligence. The Coroner: What is the negligence. Mn! Steel: lam suggestitg that his attention was drawn to this second man 7.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: Suppose his attention was drawn to a manon the parapet and while he w&amp; looking at him, Mr. Bellian ran into another, you could not call that negligence could you? Mr. Steel : Not criminal negligence, The Coroner: Could you call it censurable? Mr. Steel: Perhaps not. The Coroner: Is it that class of negligence which would enable you to bounce a sum of money out o hits afterwards. Mr. Steel: It is not a question of bouncing. ,Perhap it is negligence that may be actionable. The Coroner: Up to a certain point I am quite willing for you to make use of the Court as a fishing pond, but I want to get down to the issue. I want to know what your allegation is. I don't see any so far. Mr. Steel: I don't propose to carry that any further now. The Coroner: If the gentlemen who were with Mr. Bellian give the same evidence, even assuming the deceased was-sober' it doesn't matter. How can you blame Mr. Bellian?</p>
        <p>Further questioned Mr. Bellian said he was too upset to notice whether deceased smelled of drink. On examining the car he found it was damaged up to 51 inches from the ground. The front left-hand mud-guard struck the deceased. The Coroner said that had it been his own son who had been killed under the same exciting circumstances, and Mr. Bellian had assured him that his statement, corroborated by his friends, was correct, he (Mr. Brig- house) would have said that Mr. Bellian could not possibly be to blame. Mr. George H. Smith, cashier to Messrs. Henry Greenall and Co., corroborated Mr. Bellian's evidence. In reply to the Coroner he said all the occupants of the car were quite sober. Mr. Bellian was one of the most careful and experienced drivers in the North of England.</p>
        <p>Henry Newton, who was walking on the footpath at the time of the accident,said he did not see anything of the deceased until Mr. Bellian called his attention to him, neither did he hear anyone walking. The jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death," and exonerated Mr. Bellian from all blame.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Pickavance, a member of the jury, said that from his personal experience he did not think there was a more careful or experienced motorist than Mr. Bellian in the North of England. He was,in fact,acknowledged to be such and he (Mr. Pickavance) did not think he would have run the slighted risk. Mr. A. Booth another juror, endorsed these remarks. _ _</p>
        <p>, The Coroner said he did not know Mr. Bellian per- sonally but he knew some of his clients and they told him that he was an experienced man and a very good driver. He was perfectly satisfied that Mr. Bellian would take every care on the night in question, and the verdict was absolutely right on the evidence sub- mitted.</p>
        <p>Mr. Taylor formally expressed Mr. Bellian's great regret that the accident should have happened. Mr. Steel said he was extremely obliged for the expression, and he hoped Mr. Bellian with his usual good nature, would put his sympathy . into practical form. Deceased had left a widow and six children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>RASH AND SCALP SORES.</head>
        <p>RASH AND SCALP SORES.</p>
        <p>Zam-Buk Cures a Wigan Child.</p>
        <p>A " Wigan Examiner" reporter has just investigated the remarkable cure by Zam-Buk of the three-year-old son of Mr. John Mawdsley, of 411, Bickershaw Lane, Abram, near Wigan. For twelve months the poor child suffered irritating scalp sores, a complaint which often attacks adults as well as young children. Only Zara- guk was able to grow new, healthy skin in place of the mass of itching sores, and make the scalp clean and sweet.</p>
        <p>"For the first eighteen months of his life," said Mr* Mawdsley to the reporter, " my baby was as healthy as could be desired. Then a rash broke out on his head; and soon small pimples formed. These seemed to fill up with water,and they became so irritable that we couldn't keep his fingers away from them. Consequently his head became one mass of sores. He was in perfect misery, and his mother and myself were at our wits' end to know what we could get to relieve his sufferings. We tried four different doctors, but to no avail. Neither salve nor medicine ' did him any good, his life continued to be a perfect misery until he was nearly three years old. Then we saw Zam-Buk adver- tised, and got a free sample. Such an improvement was seen after the application of even so small a quantity, that we got a is. lid. box and persevered with the applications. As we rubbed it in the irritation was allayed, and the scalp by degrees assumed a healthier tone. The child was soon able to rest better, and now thanks to Zam-Buk, he has been completely cured. He is now as healthy-looking a boy as can be found in the country. Both my wife and I seize every opportunity to tell of Zam-Buk's wonderful healing power, and we hope other parents will be guided by our experience." Zam-Bak makes the skin healthy and soft. It is unequalled for running sores, sore heads, inflamed or irritating patches, raw chapped hands, chills, chafing, eczema, boils, ulcers, bad legs piles, diseased ankles, festering sores, poisoned wound, cuts, bruises, burns, scalds, sprains, stiffness, sore throat and chest, cold- sores, and nerveTpains. Of all chemists, or direct from the Zam-Buk Co., 4, Red Cross-street, London, E.C., for is. 11d. per box, or 2s. M. for large family size (2s. 9d. box contains nearly four times the is. lid).</p>
        <p>NATIONAL COUNCIL OF THE EVANGELICAL FREE CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>- fO's t;1 '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>Programme of Manchester Council.</head>
        <p>Programme of Manchester Council.</p>
        <p>The arrangements for the Tenth Annual Council to be held in Manchester from 6th to 9th are now nearly complete. The wide and varied scope of the interests now touched by the federation movement is strikingly shown.</p>
        <p>The sessions will be held in the Free Trade Hall, and the St. James' Hall, Central Hall and the Churches in Manchester and district will be used for pnblic meet- ings &amp;c• Rev. R. F. Horton, M.A., D.D., will preside. Special sermons will be preached simultaneously on the Wednes- day evening by Rev. J. Flanagan, Rev. W. Y. Fullerton, Rev. George Jackson 8.A., Rev. J. D. Jones M.A., B.D. Rev. J. H. Jowett M.A, Rev. G. Campbell Morgan, D.D., Rev. John McNeill, Rev. C. Ensor Walters. Sessional Sermons will be preached by the Rev. Dr. Aked, Rev. F. B Meyer, 8.A., and Rev. pinsdale T. Young on Tuesday, Wednesday, and Thursday mornings. Amongst the important subjects that will be brought forward for consideration are the following :—The Education Question, in which Dr. Clifford and others will take a prominent part; The Growth of Monastic Establishments in Great Britain will be dealt by Mr. R. W. Perks, M.P., and Rev. R. Harrison (President Primitive Methodist Conference) ; ,The Institutional Church will be discussed by the Rev. C. Silvester Horne, 31. A., and the Rev. J. Eißatienburgo; A National Temperance Policy will be formulated; Mr. J, Compton Rickett, M.P., D.L., will deal with The Crisis in Free Church Trust Deeds; The Sunday School Problem is to be introduced by Rev. J. Monro and Mr. J. C. Meggitt, J.P. ' • The Young Free Church- men's Auxiliary IN ill be treated by the Rev. R. J. Cam- bell, M,A. and Rev. Thomas Phillips, 8.A., the Rev. G. Campbell l‘lorgan, D.D., will speak on the Place of the Bible in the Wor k of the Churches.</p>
        <p>Free Churches and the Universities. By Dr. J. Hope iSioniton.</p>
        <p>The National Council and Foreign Missions, with special reference to the Federation of Mission Work will be specially dealt with by Rev. Ch3rles Wenyon, D.D.</p>
        <p>The Devotional Session on Thursday afternoon will be addressed by Rev. S. Chadwick, Rev. Charles Brown, and Rev. H. Arnold Thomas, M.A. The Official Report of the year's work will be pre- sented on the Tuesday afternoon by the General Secretary, Rev, Thomas Law. Its adoption will be moved by Rev. Silas K. Hocking and seconded by Rev. W. J. Dawson.</p>
        <p>The Finance report and balance sheet will also be presented on Thursday afternoon bathe Chairman of the Finance Committee, Alderman Evan Spicer, J.P., L.C.C., Its adoption will be moved by Dr. Rawlings and seconded by Rev. W. Rigby Murray, Moderator, Presbyterian Synod). GipsylSmith will given special report of his remark- able mission tour in South Africa.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>PUBLIC DEMONSTRATION IN MANCHESTER.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC DEMONSTRATION IN MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>The evening public meetings to be held in Manchester include the following:—Young People's Demonstration, men's meeting, women's meeting. midnight meeting,and great mass meeting in St. James' Hall. Amongst the speakers who will address these meetings are :—Rev.</p>
        <p>Dr. Clifford, Rev. J. Morgan Gibbon, Gipsy Smith, Mr. Will Crooks, M.P., Mr. H. R. Mansfield 1‘1.P., Miss Marianne Farningham, Mrs. H. Prior Hughes, Rev. Thomas Yates, Rev. J. Tolefree Parr, Mr. D. Lloyd George M.P.,141r. W.P. Hartley J.P., Rev. John McNeill, Rev. John Wilson, (President Baptist Union), Dr. Ambrose Shepherd, Mr. William Bean L.C.C., Rev. A. T. Guttery, Mr. W.R. Lane.</p>
        <p>DISTRICT MEETINGS. It is expected that public meetings will be held, on the closing Thursday night, at the following, among other towns :—Altrincham,Bacup, Blackburn, Blackpool, Bolton, Bury, Buxton, Chortley, Crewe, Darwen, Hey- wood, Macclesfield, Middleton, Mossley, Oldham, Preston, Rochdale, Southport, Stockport, Warrington and Wigan.</p>
        <p>SPEAKERS AT SESSIONS AND PUBLIC MEETINGS. In addition to the speakers already mentioned, the foliovving have consented to take part either during the sessions or at the public meetings :—Mr. W. Angus,J.P., Mr. Moses Atkinson, Rev. Frank Ballard, M.A., B. Sc., Rev. W. H. K. Baukwill (president, Bible Christian Con- ference), Mr. Robert Bird, J.P.. Rev. E. J. Brailsford, Rev. D.D. Braimbridge, Rev. R. Baldwin Brindley, Mr H. Broadhurst, M.P., Rev. David Brook, D.C.L., Mr P. W. Bunting, ALA., Rev. J. C. Carlile, Rev. H. T. Chapman, Rev. W. Cuff, Rev. George, Eayrs, F.R.H.S., Rev. W. Justin Evans, Rev. J. W. Ewing, M.A. Rev. Dr Forsyth (Chairman, Congregational Union), M.A., J. E. Flower, M.A., Rev. W. Glover, Rev. J. G. Greenhongh, , M.A., Rev. Dr. Hanson, Dr. Bendel Harris, Rev. W- I Hardy Harwood. Mr. N. W. Helme, M.P.' Rev. Joseph !Hocking, Rev. J. Hirst Hollowell, Rev. Geo. Hunter (President, Independent Methodist Conference), Rev. , C. H. Kelly, Rev. Jabez King (President,United Metho- dist Free Churches), Ray. S. B. Lane, Rev. H. Elvet Lewis, Mr. Herbert Lewis, M.P., Rev. J. Scott Lidgett, M.A., Rev. J, Gregory Mantle, Dr. John Massie, M.A. Rev. H. Howard May, Rev. T. Mitchell, Rev. J. Moore, Rev. J. M. G. Owen, Rev. A. J. Palmer, Mr. Albert Pickard, C.C., Rev. J. Pickett, Mr. E. C. Rawlings, Rev. T. M. Rees (president Methrsdist New Connexion), Rev. G. A. Johnstone Ross, M.A., Rev. Dr. Alfred Rowland, 8.A., L.L.8., Rev. V. B. Selbie, ALA., Rev. J. H. Shakespeare, M.A., Rev. J. Bamford Slack, M.P., Rev, H. T. Smart, Mr. Aldermad Snape, J.P., Rev. Bernard J. Snell, M.A., B.Sc. Rev. W. Hodgson Smith, Rev. T. Stephens, 8.A., Rev. Bowman Stephenson, Rev. J. G. Stephenson, Rev. Dr. Townsend, Rev. J. Travis, Rev. John Thomas, M.A., Rev. Henry Varley, 8.A., Rev. J. E. Wakerley, Rev. T. J. Wheldon. 8.A., Mr. Alderman George White, M.P., Mr. Robert Whyte, Rev. Walter Wynn. The visit to Manchester is being looked forward to locally with great enthusiasm. The delegates will be welcomed by a reception at the Town Hall .on Monday evening by the Right Hon. The Lord Mayor of Man- chester. _ _</p>
        <p>From this outline prosramme it will be seen that the Manchester Council is likely to prove a record gather- ing both in importance and interest. The local Councils are already appointing their representatives.</p>
      </div>
