        <head>ONE NIGHT TREATMENT.</head>
        <p>ONE NIGHT TREATMENT.</p>
        <p>Soak the hands or feet on retiring in a strong, hot, creamy lather of Cuticura Soap. Dry and anoint freely with Cuti- cura Ointment, the great skin cure and purest of emollients. Wear on the hands during the night old, loose gloves, and bandage the feet lightly in old, soft cotton or linen. For red, rough and chapped hands, dry, fissured, itching, feverish palms, with brittle, shapeless nails and painful finger ends, or sore, tender, inflamed or itching feet, this treatment is simply wonderful, fre- quently curing in a single application. Complete local and constitutional treatment for every humour of the skin, scalp and blood, with loss of hair, may now be had for 4s. 9d. Bathe with hot water and Cuticura Soap, to cleanse the surface of crusts and scales, and soften the thickened cuticle. Dry, without hard rubbing, and apply Cuti- cura Ointment freely, to allay itching, Irritation and inflammation, and soothe and heal, and lastly, take the Cuticura Resolvent Pills to cool and cleanse the blood. This treatment affords instant relief, permits rest and sleep in the severest forms of Eczema and other itching, burning and scaly humours, and points to a speedy, permanent and economical cure of torturing, disfigur- ing humours, from pimples to scrofula, from infancy to age, when all other remedies and the best physicians fail. Sold throughout the world. Cuticurs Resolvent, 21. 6d. (in form of Chocolate Coated Pills, Is. lfd. per vial of 60), Ointment, 2s. Goi., Soap, is. Depots: London, 27 Charter. house Sq.; Paris, S Rue de is Paix; Boston. 177 Colum. bus Aye. Potter Drug St Chem. Corp.. Sole Proprietors. airs•nd for "How to Cure Every Humour."</p>
        <p>matter and that the Board should be allowed to dignify those gentlemen representing Roman Catholic and Non-. conformist bodies with the names of chaplain. (Hear hear.) Mr. Hallows: As a Protestant I think that if a Roman Catholic chaplin is appointed it is only right that a Wesleyan chaplain should be appointed. In answer to Mr. J. L. Jones it was explained that the services at the Workhouse would continue to be held under the present system.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>-THE PURCHASING OF A BOILER.</head>
        <p>-THE PURCHASING OF A BOILER.</p>
        <p>The Clerk stated that the Local Government Board had approved the plans of the new boiler which the guardians proposed to erect at the Workhouse. The Board consented to the Guardians expending a sum not exceeding £172 on the work. Tenders were opened for the erection of the boiler. A considerable discussion ensued and ultimately on the motion of Mr. Guest it was decided to advertise for tenders for the supply of a second-hand boiler.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>A Seasonable Meeting.</p>
        <p>' A meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday, Mr. A. Brooks presiding. There were also present Wilkinson, the Rev. Dr. Mercer, Messrs. A. J.Jolley, T. H. Ash- croft, J. Billinge, M. W. Seton, W. Houghton, G. Thompson, J. Turton, W. Flannagan,J. C. Sutton(clerk), J. F. Awbery (master), and A. Wright (superintendent of Padgate schools.)</p>
        <p>DESTITITTLON IN THE NEWTON DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>In the list of cheques read out by the Clerk appeared .a rather larger item than usual in respect of out relief granted by the Newton Committee. Mr. Billinge ex- plained that they were suffering in the neighbourhood from the lack of employment among able-bodied people. The relief was most rigidly administered, but the dis- tress was keen, many families being in a state of destitution, and the committee had been obliged so give them temporary assistance in the shape of food.</p>
        <p>THE CHRISTMAS FESTEITIES.</p>
        <p>The MASTER reported that the Christmas festivities passed off satisfactorily on Saturday, and that the inmates desired him to convey to the Board their warm thanks and appreciation. The following guardians were present at different intervals during the day :—Messrs. Brooks Jolley, Payton, Hickman, Ashcroft, and the Rev. dr. Mercer, gifts of evergreens, had been sent by Mrs. P. Rylands, Mr. F. Monks,Mrs. Avery, Miss Broad- bent, Mr. J. C. Parr, Mrs. Frost, Mr. H. Lyon, and Mr Mather, Mr. S. Wakefield, tenant of the Midland Hotel, sent an ounce of tobacco for every smoker in the House The officers also desired him to express in their name their thanks for the liberal provision once more made for them by the Guardians—an expression with which he desired to associate himself.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>PADGATE SCHOOL CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>THE PADGATE SCHOOL CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>The SUPERINTENDENT reported that the Christmas treat as directed by the Gum diens, was given to the children on December 24th. The officers of the Work- ho use had offered to give an entertainment to the chil- dren at the schools on Saturday, January 7th. If per- mission were granted by the chools committee, the children's concert would be repeated at the Workhouse. Miss Broadbent had offered to pay for conveyances on that occasion.</p>
        <p>HOUSE COMMITTEE,</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the House Committee held on December 16th the lady guardians reported as requested upon the poor condition of the pillows in the hospital, and recommcnded that they be filled with white flock, provided the cost did not exceed £lO. The Master was authorised to purchase twenty additional bedsteads. The master was instructed to spend £2 lOs out of petty cash for toys for the children in the House. The minutes were confirmed on the proposition of Mr. Jolley seconded by Mr. Billinge.</p>
      </div>
