      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>a ;7- r</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Business of Next Week's Meeting.</head>
        <p>Business of Next Week's Meeting.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Town Conncil will be held on Tuesday at 6 p.m. when the fnllowing, among other business, will come up for consideration.</p>
        <p>SAFETY OF THE MARKETS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Watch Committee the Town Clerk reported that the Markets Committee had agreed to pay the wages of a constable for watching the markets at night. THE EITILDG INSPECTOR. The Chairman of the Street Improvement Committee and Borough Surveyor reported that they had inter- viewed Mr. Sugars (the building inspector), and that he had agreed to withdraw from the appointment which he had secured at Birkenhead, providing this Committee would increas3 his salary to the same as that of Birken- head, viz., £l3O per annum, to be increased by annual increments of £8 to a maximum of £lso.—Resolved, that the above terms be agreed to, and that Mr. Sugars's salary be increased accordingly. The Lighting Sub-Committee has made a number of recommendations for the provision of extra lamps in different parts of the town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>HEALTH COMMITTEE._</head>
        <p>HEALTH COMMITTEE._</p>
        <p>This Committee recommends that the salary of Miss Stott be raised from £1 to 255. per week. Resolved also, that the following tenders for the supply of provisions, &amp;c., required at the Hospitals for the ensuing six months,l according to specification and schedule of prices, be accepted :—Messrs. Henry Milling and Co. Ltd., groceries, provisions and cleaning materials; Messrs. W. Pierpoint and Co., bread and flour; Mr. H. Singleton, butchers' meat,- Mr. John Buck, milk; Messrs. W. Whittaker and Sons,iiltry and fish. HEAVY MOTOR TRAFFIC. 4Freg The Paving and Sewerage Committee has resolved that the Town Clerk be instructed to inform Mess re. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Limited, and the Warrington and District Threshing ' Machine Company, that numerous complaints had been received by the Com- mittee of the vibration caused by their respective heavy locomotives passing along the street at an excessive speed, and to request that in future the locomotives be not allowed to travel through the town at a greater speed than two miles an hour as provided by law.</p>
        <p>SCHOOLS AND STREET NOISES.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Rev. T. A. J. Ridpath, suggesting that the setts opposite the National schools, Church-street, should be taken up and wood blocks sub- stituted. Resolved that the suggestion be not enter- tained, but that the Borough Surveyor be instructed to have the joints of the exisiting setts (half the width of the road) opposite the school run in with asphalte</p>
        <p>THE TRAMWAYS. MORE COVERED CARS. At a meeting of the Electricity and Tramways Com- mittee. Mr. Preace attended and explained the delay in the construction of Light Railway No. 1, which was owing to the difficulty in obtaining information from. the Manchester Ship Canal Co. Mr.Preece called atten- tion to the probable damage to the pavement of the bridge by the heavy traction engine employed by Messrs. J. Crosfield and Sons, Ltd. The Town Clerk called attention to the proposed ap- plication for an order to construct a Light Railway between Warrington and Knutsford and to the powers sought to be obtained to construct Light Railway No. 2 authOrised by the Warrington Light Railway Order, 1903. . _</p>
        <p>The Chairman reported that the new Tram-car covers had given great satisfaction and as there was sufficient money in hand he recommended the purchase of four additional covers. Tenders for 'same are invited.</p>
        <p>Resolved that the Manager be requested to furnish the Borough Surveyor with a list of all defects in the pavement of the permanent way and give him instruc- tions from time to time for such repairs as are needed. It has also been resolved that an extra id. fare be charged from Stafford-road to Victoria-square, Wilders- pool, but that children be charged only one penny for the whole distance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The above Committee has resolved that on the recom- mendation of the Museum Committee a picture of "Old Warrington," by the late Mr. George Sheffield, be pur- chased for the Art Gallery. Two letters were read from Messrs. James Airelough and Sons, Limited, with respect to the difficulty of navigating their vessels under Warrington Bridge. Resolved, that the Town Clerk negotiate for the pur- chase of a School Site in the Orford Ward.</p>
        <p>Resolved, th At an account submitted by Messrs. John Walker S.: Co., of £22 for the printing of 150 topies of the Non-Trading Commissioners' Report, be not paid. THE WATER COMMITTEE-£20,510 STILL WANTED.</p>
        <p>Aldermzin Francomb will move: That the Town Clerk be instructed to apply to the Local Government Board for their sanction to the borrowing of the sum of £20,510 for waterworks purposes.</p>
        <p>The problem of the THE UNE APLOYED. unemployed is dis-</p>
        <p>cussed with great zeal, but without any notable increase of enlighten- ment. A report issued by the Trade Unions describes very justly the methods hitherto applied to an economic evil which has been inherent in society ever since industrial organisation began, "In varying degrees of proportion they are ineffec- tive, and more or less demoralising, inasmuch as they weaken the sense of individual and collective responsibility for existing conditions, and not- withstanding generations of experiments, they have lamentably failed to furnish even a temporary solution to the problem." This condemnation covers the "employment agent:ies," and the administration of poor relief and private charity. But it might be extended to every device which has yet been heard of, political or philanthropic. The Trad., Unions have a scheme which cannot be regarded with much confidence. One of the proposals is that public bodies shall "acquire land to which the unemployed may be drafted, with a view to the workers and their families eventually becoming self-supporting through a system of co-operative farming." This looks very well on paper; but we are afraid it will not inspire enthusiasm among • the people who are practically acquainted with the conditions of agriculture in this country. No public bodies are likely to go to the expense of acquirilig land on these terms. The Trade Unions very reasonably urge upon the Govern- ment that "the works of public utility relating to the Port and docks of London and the improvement of the Thames should be carried out with the least possible delay." Here is a definite purpose which everybody can under- stand and approve; but we are transported back into the region of vague aspiration by the suggestion of "coast protection against the encroachment of the sea, and general reclamation of the land saved from such encroachment." A Government which has at last been driven by public opinion to the necessary expenditure in- volved in the provision of adequate artillery fo the British Army cannot be expected to undertake vast and expensive schemes for rescuing undesig- nated land from the ocean.</p>
        <p>In all these far-reaching plans the benefits may be visionary, but the expenditure is sure. Anybody who watches the temper of the public can easily see that there is very small disposition to counten- ance a vast outlay at a time when the finances of the country are so badly in need of retrenchment. There is a natural reluctance, moreover, to admit that it is the duty of the State to find work for the unemployed ; for if this principle were admitted the number of unemployed would prob- ably grow in proportion to the national obliga- tion, and, so far from . curing the economic evil we should merely intensify it. Many people feel this very strongly in connection with the demand that hungry school-children should be fed at the public expense. This benevolence would be a direct encouragement to a certain class of parents to evade all responsibility for the sustenance of their children. Sir JOHN GOHST seems to hold that,</p>
        <p>because we have made education free under certain conditions, we are bound to rve free meals for the children who are stltrvillg. it might as well be contended that free e ;tication carries with it the right of parents to demand that not only the feeding, but also the clothing of their offspring, shall be undertaken by the rate- payers. Many an improvident or drunken father would be glad to get rid of his duties by the rigid application of Sir JOHN GORST'S logic. But the need for discrimination in the use of public money is as urgent as the same need in the application of , private charity ; and it is likely to be insisted upon with much greater energy.</p>
        <p>The Town Council will meet, as usual, on Tues- day evening next, it having been decided that no postponement is necessary,though there has been a general impression in the town that the Council would not assemble until the week following. There is the usual agenda of business, except that most of the matters to come before Tuesday's meeting are of that relatively unimportant character of correctly described by the phrase: " parish pump affairs." We observe, however, that the General Purposes Committee has declined to pass the account for the printing of the Non-Trading Commissioners' Report. This seems a somewhat ungracious act, seeing that the Commissioners were pressed none too gently by the Council to issue the document at the earliest possible moment, convenient or otherwise; and seeing also that the rules adopted by the Council to govern the inquiry, explicitly !state that the costs shall be borne by the Corporation. This parr- ticular report was only issued by dint of arduous and all-night labour by both Commis- sioners and printers, and it is not seemly that the Corporation should now jib at the expense necessarily entailed. Particulars of the circum- stances connected with the printing of the Report are given in another column, and we ima.zine, when the Council has all the facts of the cast before it, that the account will be no longe2 demurred to.</p>
        <p>Among other items likely to provoke discussion there is the renewed proposal by the, Chairman of the Water Committee to borrow £20,510 for waterworks purposes. Only a month ago the Council refused to sanction the expenditure,though Alderman Francomb made a strong plea on behalf of the Water Committee. His explana- tion of the requirements of the water department did not in all probability satisfy one-tenth of the members of the Council, and as we intimated at the time, Alderman Francomb must take the public more into his confidence. Surely if £20;510 is really required, there should be no difficulty in proving the necessities of the case. Another matter is the prospective fare on the extended Wilderspool tramway. The committee proposes to charge an extra halfpenny for the section which crosses the Canal and runs to Victoria square. Stockton Heath r3BideotB argue with some reason that the total distance does. not exceed that of the Latchford route, and therefore the fare should remain the same. The fact that the extension has been a very ex- pensive one, and that it passes the borough bound- ary, are the best arguments on behalf of the Com- mittee's contention that the fare should be increased. We are glad to note, in further connection with the tramways, that the covered cars hake given great satisfaction not only to the public biu, to the committee, and four others are recommended. It would please many people if some of our local tradesmen were successful on this occasion, in tendering for the work.</p>
        <p>We notice, under the minutes of the Electricity and Tramways Committee a resolution to attend, by co-operation with the Borough Surveyor, to all defects in the pavement of the permanent way. This is an admirable resolution to come to, especi- ally on the eve of a new year. The " pernianent way in many placea is showing signs of very rapid dissolution. There are innumerable spots along the tram lines which are known as death- traps by cyclists and motorists who travel over them. Take Warrington Bridge for an example. The permanent way here is in a disgraceful con- dition. In several places the setts have sunk a con- siderable distance below the level of the iron rails, which thus present a most dangerous obstruction to all wheeled traffic. The marvel is that accidents of a serious nature here are not more common. Only the agility of the average cyclist has on many occasions saved him from a smash for which the Corporation would probably be held responsibledt is high time both the Tramways Committee and the Surveyor's Department awoke to the realisation of the state of the permanent way. Up to now it has been neglected in a fasnion which has brought forth the strong condemnation of hundreds of vehicle-users.</p>
        <p>By the removal from our midst, swiftly and unexpectedly, of Mr. T. J. Down, Warrington suffers yet another loss to be regretfully added to the long record of the year now rapidly drawing to its close. Although in but a very limited sense a public man the gap left by Mr. Down's demise will be deeply felt by a large number of our towns- men. intimately connected as he was with a vast business which, for obvious reasons we must regard as loaded with great responsibilities, one cannot but admire the keeness of the business acumen which, on Mr. Down's part, largely rendered possible ito great success as a commercial concern. In business as in the few social matters in which he interested himself Mr. Down was known to be moved at all times by a high sense of duty. Under a somewhat formal, even austere manner, he hid a genial nature capable of and often moved by the most generous impulses, winning thereby esteem, high just in proportion as one knew him the more intimately. In public he appeared, if we remember rightly, in but two capacities both closely associated with the twin arts which largely occupied his leisure. As the first and long reigning presi- dent of the Warrington Photograhic Society an earlier generation saw the social side of his character disclosed on many occasions when, in years past, he preluded its annual lantein exhibi- tion with lucid remark and apt anecdote. But that larger portion of his time and means which Mr. Down devoted to music and the culture of it by amateur instrumentalists of Warrington will have more deeply impressed the public—a line of work and recreation combined which is deserving of all praise. For not only must the practice and public performances of his orchestral and military bands be regarded as largely educational in their effects on those concerned; they have doubtless had far-reaching and not less adeiirable effects in the steadying infience they have exercised upon the characters of many young men whose careers might have otherwise been lacking in prudent ballast. Let us hope that this good work at least may be continued by a worthy and highly respected son.</p>
        <p>The lot of the motorist is not always a happy one, though he has the privilege of travelling along the road at a greater speed than anyone else. There are plenty of laws and restrictions to hamper him, and he may also be the victim of circumstances to a degree hardly conceivable by other users of the high road. Only this week a well-known local motorist, Mr. W. H. Bellian, has been distinctly unfortunate. Though one of the most careful and expert drivers, he has been the cause of death to a poor pedestrian on the Liver- pool Road. Mr. Bellian was returning from S. Helens on Monday evening at a moderate pace owing to the darkness of the night, when he ran over the deceased, who was apparently lying full length across the road. None of the occupants of the car had the slioliftest idea that the, man was there until the wheels bumped over him, and their concern at the dis- covery can be better imagined than described. Of course, no blame or charge of negligence could belaid atlMr. Bellian's door, and the Coroner's jury were unanimous in acquitting him of all responsibility for the fatialty. It was one of those things that might have happened to any motorist, and it is a fortunate circumstance f that Mr. Bellian had not only an excellent reputation but a number of reliable witnesses. As the Coroner pointed out, pedestrians</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>f-)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>' ---- ,€_. 'itr " • '• .. --,:-- -- TR" ix--... • -,3/4, -.,?.- *-,- ', . 4.1-.-.:„. •:- r:-.,:_, ‘,;',..,'"C 2' IIPI IR nitr.lMffg, .7 _ , – 74'. * Ihil. I. 4 '.</head>
        <p>' ---- ,€_. 'itr " • '• .. --,:-- -- TR" ix--... • -,3/4, -.,?.- *-,- ', . 4.1-.-.:„. •:- r:-.,:_, ‘,;',..,'"C 2' IIPI IR nitr.lMffg, .7 _ , - 74'. * Ihil. I. 4 '.</p>
        <p>have a regrettaNe habit of betaking themselves to the centre of the road in preference to the footpath on a dark night. They have, of course, the tirst right to the road, but they should take reas Inable precautions for their own safety. Monday's fatality should act as a warding to road- ramblers at nigeit-time. All the skill and care in the world on the pin of a motorist wU wit save them if they wilfully or carelessly ignore ordinary precautions.</p>
        <p>Mr. Austen Chamber ain's so -ithiug worth rabont the Income Tax demand note will be like vinegar and gall for the collectors. Tnis is undoebta fly a time of strain, and there are Many people, whuse incomes are apparent rather than real, who will feel a difficulty in meeting the tax oe the rreseut basis. Th,re is less money going now, but it is ,not easy to cut one's expenses occordiug' y; and the Income Tax payer, having had his ertimb!e at the early demand, will be less inclined than ever to gather together the shek-31s. is one •of the very extraordinary things that the Szot, who in most matters drives a hard bargain, has for years past given himself up as a victim to the need ,of the Exchequer for soul:, ready cash. Questions have been asked in Parliament,but they have made no diflerence. North of the Tweed nearly all the leeeme Tax is got in very shortly after the N.3w Year has begun. In England something' lik a quarter's grace has been given, and is still probable—though the authorities du not formally ,concede it. On the gross sum there is a neat little saving of interest te the English commercial inagnatea, who have their money to turn over so much longer. Mr. °clamber- lain's reason why the demands were issued on our side so early this year seems iecouseqeential. They used to be delivered by liaed, and now they are sent out by post. The old course involved delay ; the new does not. But, surely, once the past is decided upon as the medium, its stroke is so prompt and simultaneous the, there was still less rtaason for haste. It looks as if the Treasury officials thought thy would try the screw, and are now unwinding it because the turn has so irritated.</p>
        <p>Of Christmas and its good cheer only pleasant memories remain—and pleasant indeed they are! Even the habitual grumbler at the weather was robbed of his favourite theme, and if the conditions were not exactly "old fashioned" there was a Christmassy touch in the atmosphere which made the enjoyment of the festival the more complete. One of the few advantages of Christmas Day falling on Sunday was that most people in War- rington and diatri3t had the unaccustomed privi- lege of three consecutive days' holiday. The churches, too, were able to make the most of the glad d ay, although the home fireside had perhaps the strongest attraction in the evening. Right heartily were the rejoicings entered into on Monday, and it there be any persons in Warrington who have thought that fitting Yale-tide observance is doomed, they should feel re-assured. Particularly pleasing was it to know that the spirit of charity and goodwill was truly abroad, and few, even of the very poor, were denied the luxury of Christmas fare, however humble. Santa Clans, too, went his rounds in some of the poorer neighbourhoods, and many a childish heart was gladdened by his bounty.</p>
        <p>Somehow the cry of "Fire" ! is always a supreme attraction, and when the wierd sounds of Messrs. Crosfields' hooter broke the night stillness at 2.45 a.m. on Thursday, not a few people left their homes and hurried to the works. They were disappointed, for although there was a smart turnout of the fire brigade, t e excitement ended there. Once more, it was a case of " false alarm " ! This time, however, the prastical joker was not respon- sible. We believe that some unaccountable flaw in the electrical apparatus was the cause of the unnecessary ringing of it bell which is recognised as the signal for the sounding of the "buzzer." Such unlooked-for incidents should, at any rate, have the effect of keeping the brigade on the alert, but perhaps public appreciation of the diversion will not he in equal measure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Index to Inside Pages.</head>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages.</p>
        <p>PAGE. 2 Women's Chat and Fashions. 3 The Story of the Year. Motor Car Fatality near Warrington. Football. National Free Church Council. Warrington and Runcorn Boards of Guardians. 6 Thi " Bishop's Carol," by Fergus Hume. Crosfield's Fire Brigade Dinner. Our Local Churches. 7 Runcorn Rural Council. Remarkable Burning Case. Billiards. The Free Churches.</p>
        <p>P LIBLICII BEFIT. UNDER THE CLOCK .EOR BEST BOOTS IN LA.NC ASHIRE.—AdVt. Parsonal. Mr. Thomas H. Whitfield, postmaster at Middle- wieh, is shortly to be transferred to a more important app)intment at Carrick-on-Shannon (Ireland). As a resident in the district Mr. Whitfield was very popular. Mr. Whitfield took a prominent part in the formation of the Middlesvich Volunteer Corpi, and was for 24 years a member of the Ist Prince of Wales Volunteers (Warrington) South Lancashire Regiment. H. 31. AsurroN's LIFE' TONIC is the KING ToNics.-33, SANKEY STREET, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Social. • The members of the Legh-street Young Hen's Bible Class., together with their friends held a social gather- ing on Holiday evening in the schoolroom. A good number sat down to an excellent tea, after which an entertainment was given. During an interval in the programme the Rev. C. L. Stowe gave an andress to the young people. Various games were indulged in, Mr. G. Jones conducting.</p>
        <p>Local Prize Distribution. The Ven. Archdeacon Maitland Wood prevented the books and certificates won by the students at Beacon- field school, Weston, at an "at home" which was held on the 20th inst. He also presented the Principal of the school with a certificate of the Teachers' Registra- tion Council of the Board of Education.—During the proceedings a number of vocal and intuuniental items were giveu by the students.</p>
        <p>The Accident at Gorsey-lane Bridge. At 7.15 on Friday evening of last week, a Grimsby goods train was on its way to Godley, and when near the siding at Gorsey-lane, Warrington, the engine jumped the metals, and together with four waggons was overturned. Luckily no one was injured. The driver and stoker jumping off in time. Air. Darby- shire, the stationmaster at Warrington, at once tele- graphed for a break-down gang from Liverpool and Gorton, and at three o'clock on Saturday morning the up main line, which had been blocked, was used as usual for traffic. The engine which had fallen part of the way down the embankment, was got on the metals again at about three o'clock on Saturday after- noon.</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OF TONICS. I 63. SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt.l Warrington Soldier's Death. News has been received at Hightown Barracks, Wrexham; the headquarters of the Royal Welsh Fusiliers, of the death of Colour-Sergeant Galloway, at the Drill Hall, Gosford. The deceased, who had served for twenty years in the Welsh Fusiliers, had served in Egypt, Crete and Malta, and for al4out tw ) years had acted sergeant-instructor to Gresford and Chirk half-companies of the Ist Volunteer Battalion Royal Welsh Fusiliers. He was a native of Warring- ton. He was 3S years of age and had been suffering for some months from aneurism of the heart, for which he had undergone two operations in the Liverpool Royal Infirmary. He leaves &amp;widow and two young sons. NEW YEAR'S PRESENTS.—Try J. S. GARLAND'S, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington. Splendid selection.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Y.M.A. Harriers Club The above club held their annual Christmas handi- cap on Saturday last, when about fifteen runner, turned out. !The day was an ideal one for harriers and the race,which was from Headqaarters to Newton Reformatory and back to Longford, about seven miles, proved both interesting and exciting, and reflected great credit on the handicappers, as the first six men, finished within 11 minutes of the winner, whose time was 43 minutes. The first to arrive home were H. Dale (scratch) 1 • C. Winstanley (21mins.), 2 ; A. Sinclair (smins.), 3 ; J. Crooks (Imin.), 4; D. Forster (limin.), 5 • and D. Worsley (1 min.) 6, the remainder being very close behind. Next Saturday an inter-club run with Warrington Harriers will be held from Bridge Foot. NEW YEAR'S PRESENTS.—Try J. S. GARLAND'S, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington. Splendid election.—Advt. Death of Mr. R. W. Murray, J.P. Many Warrington people will hear with regret of the death, which occurred at Belfast on Saturday, of Mr. Robert Wallace 3turray, J.P., chairman and formerly managing director of the Whitecross Company, Limited, and head of the tobacco firm of Messrs. Murray, Son and Company. To the older generation of the town Mr. Murray was well known, as prior to 1880, the -year in which he left Warrington to take up his residence at Belfast, he i took a prominent part n local political, religious aud social life, being actively connected with Wycliffe Congregational Church, and displaying a great interest in its welfare. He was promineltly identified with the work of therunday School. In politics he was a Liberal, though latterly he became a Liberal Unionist, and unsuccessfully sought /Parliamentary honours for Belfast.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>BEST BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER THE CLOCK.—Advt.</head>
        <p>BEST BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER THE CLOCK.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Concert at Latchford. A concert, organised by the Misses Broadbent, was held in the Parish Room, Latchford, on Tuesday even- ing. There was a large attendance and as a result a substantial balance will be handed over to the Christ Church Decoration Fund. Miss Willcocks gave a couple of songs and Miss M. Fairclough, who was the accompanist, gave two pianoforte solos, the efforts of both being much enjoyed. In a coon song and dance Mrs. Coghill and Mrs. H. Broadbent acted and sang well, whilst another dance, by three country girls, was successfully gone through. Mr. R. W. Wilde with two songs earned hearty applause, while Mrs. Coghill dis- played remarkable ability with her banjo songs. Mrs. Coghill also contributed a pianoforte solo. Mrs. H. Broadbent, sang " Josie." A couple of humorous sketches entitled "A. Fair Encounter" and "The Mouse Trap" followed. The former was given by the Misses M. and S. Broadbent and the latter by Miss Broabent and Mr. Tom Broadbent. As for PII_N.NDIGHAM'S HIGH-CLASh MINERAL</p>
        <p>Seasonable Entertainment at the Parr Hall. By no means the least attractive of the Christmas entertainments has proved to be the appearance at the Parr Hall of Mr. Tomkin§on's ROyal Gipsy Chil- dren, who have nightly attracted large audiences to that building. The performanca by the company is much of the character generally associated with gipsy choir productions, and is characterized by a brightness which makes it particularlyienjoyable. Both collec- tively and individually the "gipsy children" prove themselves no mean vocalists and dancers, their con- ributions called forth the hearty appreciation of their auditors. The second half of the programme is given over to specialities, the opening item being a clever exhibition of tight wire dancing by Miss Rubina. Other prominent participants were "The musical Keiths' who proved themselves accomplished enter- tainers, the Lizzette troupe of lady acrobats—in their particular line one of the most expert troupes. touring the country; picturesque coon dances by the Florida troupe, and humorous selections by Fred McLean and the Mister Gee Party which created considerable amusement. On the whole the entertainment is of an eiljoyable character and one which is well worth a I visit.</p>
        <p>Astc for CUNNINGIaId'S Hlthi-CLASS MINERAL WT ERS.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
