      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, December 31, 1904„ "NON-TRADERS' METHODS." WARRINGTON IN 1904.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 31, 1904„ "NON-TRADERS' METHODS." WARRINGTON IN 1904.</p>
        <p>Education have consulted the Education Committee who have now submitted to the Board the conditions under whica they will take over financial responsibility in respect of the school. Their terms include adequate representation on the governing body, a reduction of fees, and the power to appoint and dismiss the staff, Sce. The necessity of coping with the overcrowding of the elemendary schools has repeatedly been urged upon the committee, and two new schools are to be erected at Latchford and in Orford Avenue. Much comment has been aroused latterly by a building scheme which the Board of Guardians contemplate. At the July meeting the question of the ovecrowding of the Workhouse was fully debated, and, notwithstand- ing an element of opposition,the Board decided to erect a building intermediate in character between the In- firmary and the Workhouse proper, for the accommoda- tion of aged and infirm inmates. Since then plans have been approved, but the projected heavy expenditure is strongly objected to in many quarters, and protests have , been forthcoming from the Property Owners' Associa- tion and a public meeting promoted by that body. In religious circles there have been several events of interest. On February 27th a handsome new chapel erected by the congregation of the Workingmen's Mission, Qank Quay, was opened by Mrs. Crosdeld. It wilt accommodate 320 persons. During the week com- mencing May Bth, Warrington was honoured with a visit from a large number of Wesleyan miuisters on the occa- sionjof the Li verpooliDistrict Wesleyan Synod. The meet- ings were well attended, several eminent speakers appearing on theplatforms. On January 2nd the Rev. H. B. Firth, M.A. was instituted and inductedto the living of St. James', Latchford. The Rev. Father Ryan, priest in charge of the Church of the Sacred Heart,Liank Quay, this year completed the silver jubilee of his priesthood, and the happy occasion was celebrated on May 30th, when the congregation presented hini with a purse con- taining £33 in gold. The foundation stone of a new Free Church 11:11 at Winwick was laid on December Ist by Mrs. C. E. Parker. Tne attainment by the Rev. John Yonge of the twenty-eighth anniversary of his I pastorate at Wycliffe Church was fittingly marked at the conclusion of the ev.2ning service on November 6th, the rev, gentleman being very heartily congratulated, One of Warrington's most honoured institutions— the Clergy Daughter's School—has during the year been removed to more picturesque surroundings at Da.rley Dale. The last anniversary at Warrington was kept on Wednesday, July 27th, when the speakers included the Bishops of Liverpool and Chester. . .</p>
        <p>Twelve new Wariington jusEicas of the peace have been added to the Borough Bench, the names of the following gentlemen being placed on the Commission of tne Peace on June 15th :—The Mayor, Alderman A. Bennett, Mr. C. S. Burgess, Mr. C. Jackson Holmes, Mr. A. J. Jolley, Mr. G. Pilling, Mr. L. W. Reynold; Coun- cillor D. Tinnion, Councilior J. Wilkinson, Mr. Edwin Woods, Mr. W. Ricuardson and Mr. W. Roberts. A social event in which 'Warrington was interested took place on June 7th, when Miss Dorothy Lyon, only child of Mr. T. H. Lyon, Applet in Hall, was married to Lord Acton, secretary in his Majesty's diplomatic service. The marriage took place at Brompton Oratory, in the presence of a fashionable congregation. When Lord and Lady Acton visited Appleton gall after the honeymoon, they had a magnificent reception, and the ' bride was presented witn numerous gifts from the tenantry and other well-wishers. Warrington did honour to the memory of Joseph Priestley on Saturday, February 6th, when in com- memoration of the centenary of the death of the great scientist, a tablet was unveiled on his former residence in Academy street, which is now used for office pur- poses by tne Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Company, Limited. Dr T. E. Thorpe, head of the Government laboratories in London, performed the unveiling cere- mony and read a most interesting paper. Another centenary of a different character—namely that of the Bible Society—was celebrated at the Parr Hall on March 21st. An enthusiastic meeting was addressed by the Bishop of Liverpoel and the Rev. Dr. Watson ("lan Maclaren.") The veteran Leader of the Salvation Army, Geneaal Booth, visited Warrington on August 13th and received a hearty welcome, the respect of townspeople being appropriately voiced by the Mayor and the Rev. John "Yong°. The inhabitants of Warrington were delighted to hear that on October Ist the members of Messrs. Crosfield's band cacried off the silver cup in the national junior competition at the Crystal Palaceafany Warringtonians figured in the Cheshire Lines band which secured the shield in the preliminary contest. Another success which gave much gratification was the award to the Warrington Cheshire Lines ambulance team of the third prize in the inter-railway ambulance competition in London on May 7th. The local Volunte3r battalion is in a flourishing state and the beginning of the present month witnessed the opening of the new quarters at the Drill Hall. Addi- tional warehouse accommodation az well as recreative facilities are provided. Warrington was favoured with a visit from upwards of 60 members of the Associated Chambers of Commerce on September 30th, the delegates inspecting Messrs. i Crosfleld's up-to-date ssvorks with considerable interest and pleasure. A new organizationwas formed on October 12th in the shape of a Social Service League ,which is largely the outcome of the efforts of the Rev. H. Dunnico. Its object is summoned up in "the readjustment of the present social order," and the league seeks to "educate, a;itate, and op3rate." Weekly debatesoon topics of the day have been held, It is a matter for deep regret to have to record the death ofsmany well-known townspeople. On February 9th there passed away Mr. Joseph Pemberton, an earnest advocate of Spiritualism' Mr. Charles H. Pigot, music dealer, Bridge-street, and Mr4Joseph Albinson, a stalwart Libertil,of Stockton Heath. The town was shocksi on February 16th to hear of the painfully sudden death of Mr. Thomas Tunstall, head and founder of the firm of Messrs. T. and J. L. Masten and Co., and actuary at the Savings' Bank. Mr. Robert Anderton, licencee of the Cross Keys' Buttermarket-street, and an ex-member of the band of the Royal Dragoon Guards, passel away on March 12th, while on March 26th the town sustained a further loss by the death of Mr. John Harrison, *ho for twenty years acted as elective auditor and was a consistent Liberal. Mr. James Tinsley, J.P. of Daresbary, a trustee of the Blue Coat School and Warrington Savings Bank, died on rtpril 3rd, to the great regret of a large circle. During May we had the painful duty of announcing the death of Police Sergeant Henry Cheney, one of the oldest and best known mem- ber of the Borough Force; Mr. S. G. Davey, a highly respected tradesman, of Padgate lane; Mr. E. A. Wallington,of Sutton, for twenty years a member of the Runcorn Board of Guardians and Rural District Council ; and Mr. William Dillorym Erc-fo3tballar, and licencee of the Raven Hotel. An old a,nd'respected mem- ber of the Town Council passed away on June 7th,in the , person of Alderman Heaton, who entered the Council in 1883. The sudden death of Mr. S. B. Stringer, a pro- minent figure in local Wesleyan circles, created apainfnl sensation on July 12th. Mr. Edwin Richardson, of Paddington House, with possibly one exception the loldest Warrington tradesman, died on August 23rd after A long illness. Other townsmen who have recently been removed by death are: Mr. James Bolton, Mr. Edward Pickavance, Mr. William Bellian, and Mr. John Hedgcock. This week the lown mourns the loss *of Mr. T. J. Down, of whose efforts to cultivate a love of music among all classes it is impossible to speak too highly. Without a brief reference to the state of trade this review would be incomplete. In 4 the earlier part of the year depression in the iron industry caused acute distress in the Bewsey district, but the conditions generally were not such as to call for a public appeal. 'Since then there has, fortunately, been a welcome improvement, and we are hopeful that the coming year will be one of increased prosperity to the town and to the workers in its many and varied industries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>"A DISPUTED PRINTING ACCOUNT.'</head>
        <p>"A DISPUTED PRINTING ACCOUNT.'</p>
        <p>The" Warrington Observer " of last Saturday con- tained, under the above head-lines, a reference to the account from John Walker and Co. for the printing of the Non-Trading Commissioners' Report, which did not state the facts correctly. We have therefore addressed the following letter to the editor of the " Observer " explaining our position in the matter as printers of the Report.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>[COP Yl.</head>
        <p>[COP Yl.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Under the above headings you last week Published what purported to be a report of the proceed- ings at a meeting of the General Purposes Committee of the Corporation when our account for the printing of the Non-Trading Commissioners' report was considered. Whether your version of what took place at this private meeting is correct, is a point about which we are not much concerned. Several of the statements you give publicity to are, however, contrary to fact, and at the same time reflect unfavourably upon us as printers. Before publishing what is obviously a coloured version of the circumstances relating to the disputed account you ought, we venture to think, to have made some endeavour to get at the facts of the case, which are, shortly these: The order for printing the Non-Trading Commis- sioners' report was given to us,in the first place, by the Commissioners themselves, and not by Mr. James Dodds , as you state. Subsequently the Deputy Town Clerk ' intervened, and, before the work was completed, took over the responsibility for it, at the same time ordering 100 copies of the report and agreeing to pay also for 50 copies for the Non-Trading Commissioners. Our account therefore against the Corporation was for the first 150 copies printed. The work was done under cir- cumstances of extreme urgency and involved all-night labour. Our charge was consequently proportionately high. When the Town Clerk pressed for a reduction we met him to the fullest extent we were able by an abate- ment of £1 10s., and not £2 as you erroneously state. The concluding paragraph of your report makes a most unjustifiable and entirely misleading comparison between our work and that of some other printers. What the Corporation paid 4s. 100. per page for was work of an entirely different character done under corn- petition at a contract price, and at comparative leisure. You are also totally wrong in stating that "the charge made by Messrs. Mackie and Co.,' Guardian Office' for printing 500 copies of the same report was £9 75." What Messrs. Mackie and Co. did was to re- print the report from our printed copy in a size little , more than half the size we issued it, and under cir- cumstances vastly different. By giving a separate order for reprinting the report to Messrs..Nfackie and Co., we contend that the Corpora- tion involved themselves in a needless expenditure. Exactly the same type that we employed could have been used in producing the report in the style required by the Corporation at a cost of about one-third of the sum (29 75.) paid to Messrs. Mackie and Ca., , for a separate reprint. We trust you will give this explanation the same publicity as was accorded the garbled report published in your issue of Saturday last, and we bag to remain, yours faithfully, JOHN WALKER &amp; CO., Bewsey-st., Dec. 30th 1901. The Editor "Observer."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Letter from Mr. Dodds.</head>
        <p>Letter from Mr. Dodds.</p>
        <p>Mr. James Dodds has written as follows to the Editor of the "Observer." [copy]. To the Editor of the "Observer." Sir,—As the statement in your last issue that I gave the order to Messrs. John Walker and Co., for the print- ing of the Non-Trading Commissioners' Report on the Inquiry is absolutely untrue, I mast call upon you to give your authority for so barefaced and ridiculous an invention. I never gave such order, and no one is more willing than I am that the full "facts" should be made public. So far as lam concerned, they are very simple. The Deputy Town Clerk who had on several occasions asked me, on behalf of the Mayor, when the Non- Trading Commissioners' Report would be presented, rang me up on the telephone on the 20th October, when in answer to his inquiries I told him the Commissioners, whom I had seen early that morning, had, the night before, decided to have their report printed in foolscap size, to hand the Mayor a printed copy; and to have 200 copies printed for them- selves, also that they hadthemselves seen and instrucited Messrs. Walker and Co. to do the work. The Dep7rEy Town Clerk then said, and most clearly, that the Cor- porationwould have preferred a different size; that they were responsible and would haveto pay for the printing of the Report, and of a reasonable number of copies for the Non-Trading Commissioners (he mentioned 50 copies), but that for any further copies they required, they must pay. I thereupon suggested to the Deputy Town Clerk that he should see the printers and instruct them accordingly. This, I understand, he at once did.— Yours &amp;c., JAMES DoDDS. December 30th, 1904.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Letter From the Non-Trading Commissioners.</head>
        <p>Letter From the Non-Trading Commissioners.</p>
        <p>[To THE EDITOR OF THE " EXAMINERI.</p>
        <p>Sir,—We notice, with regret, the action of certain members of the Warrington Town Council who seem never to tire of making mountains out of molehills if only. they can strike a blow at the recent Trading Inquiry or the Non-Trading Commissioners. The feel- ing seems to amount to positive spleen and venom. The latest sample of it is found in a statement which appeared in a Warrington newspaper of Saturday last, and which had reference to the cost of printing the Report of the three Commissioners. This newspaper report speaks of an "extraordinary proceeding " and alludes to " Non-Traders' Methods, "Revelations," and so forth. It seems to us that the whole affair was straightforward and easily explainable. It must be remembered that we were greatly pressed for time in getting out our Report. Mr. Henry Roberts had actually, in a placard he placed on the walls, sug- gested that we were purposely putting off its issue. As a matter of fact, it was a case of working night and day to get it completed. Seeing that our original Coun- cil colleagues had the assistance of the officials,and had issued their Report and sent it at once to the Press, we decided that we must ourselves see to the printing of our Report. We gave the order for its printing. We knew it would be comparatively costly, as the document was so long. and the time allowed for its printing was so brief. While we were in the midst of the work, the,Deputy Town Clerktelephoned to Mr. Dodds, who had been the ' the convenient medium of communication between ourselves and the Corporation, although he had then ceased to be a Councillor (be resigned on the 6th , October). The Deputy Town Clerk asked Mr. Dodds on behalf of the Mayor when our Report would be ; ; received and was told that we (the Non-Trading Com- missioners) had decided that it would be most expediti- ous and satisfactory to have the Report printed and I I that in fact the work was then in hand. The Deputy Town Clerk then, and quite voluntarily and distinctly, as Mr. Dodds affirms, stated that the Corporation were responsible and would pay fer the printing of the Report for themselves and fifty copies for us but that for any further copies we required we should have to pay. We ordered and received one hundred and fifty extra copies, for which we subsequently paid. A calm and dispassionate consideration of the case will show that the Councillors who have raised this question have discovered a mare's nest. Our action was perfectly straightforward and above board. We were taunted with delaying the report. We did all we could to get it out in time, at any cost. We succeeded. And now these gentlemen are comparing the cost of a document printed on good paper, foolscap size and under tremendous pressure of time, with the cost of another document leisurely printed, on relatively, poor paper, small size and small type. The comparison is, we venture to think, absurd. At any rate we consider that we adopted the best course that was available under very peculiar and trying circumstances,—Yours &amp;., ADAM HORROCKS. JOHN PEMBERTON. S. NORBURY WILLIAMS. 29th December, 1904. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>St. Hilda's Mission.</head>
        <p>St. Hilda's Mission.</p>
        <p>St. Hilda's Mission Church, off Knutsford-road, which through unavoidable circumstances, has been closed fcr the past two years, is to be re-opened on Thursday next, January Zsth, at 7.30 p.m., when the sermon will be preached by the Rev, H. V. Pigot , M.A., vicar of Stockton Heath. The Mission Church in the centre of a thickly-populated distriqt, and it is hoped that those dwelling near will IA regular in attendance at the services. For the present, evening service and sermon will be held only, on every Sunday at 6.30 p.m.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>HEATHER</p>
        <p>SHORTBREAD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>YEAR OF LOCAL HISTORY.</head>
        <p>A YEAR OF LOCAL HISTORY.</p>
        <p>As we stand on the threshold of .a new year, it is</p>
        <p>interesting,may be profitable, to review the events of the past twelve months. While there have been no very memorable happenings locally,the year 1901 has witnessed much that is worthy of note in writing Warrington history. In the first place the year has been one of exceptional political activity. The great fiscal question and other important issues before the oountry have been very fully debated, and the Liberal party has performed a most useful educational work in the constituency. The workers have had the advantage of the services of Mr. Joseph Howes who has addressed several series of enthusiastic meetings. The Liberal Association on June 6th unanimously invited Mr. Arthur H. Crosfield to contest Warrington in the progressive interest at the next election, and Mr. Crosfield wrote intimating that when the time came he should accept without hesitation their handsome invitation. It is unnecessary to enlarge upon the energy and ability which Mr. Crosfield has brought to bear in seeking to enlighten the electorate, suffice it to say that ' his well-reasoned addresses have always been appreciated and he is assured of a hearty backing when the trial of strength comes. Open air meetings were much in vogue during the summer months, and attracted large and attentive gatherings. The efforts of the Labour Representation Committee to secure a candidate have up to the pre- sent proved ineffectual. On February 25th it became known that the Executive Committee of the Associ- ated Shipwrights' Society had declined to allow Mr Alexander Wilkie of Newcastle-on-Tyne, to contest Warrington. An offer by the Liberal Association to arrange a compromise was declined, and other prob- able Labour candidates have been approached, but without success. The most snthusiastic political demonstration of the year was that organized by the Liberal Association and held in the Parr Hall on March 15th, while further unmistakable evidenoe of Liberal vitality was afforded by the large audience which assembled in the same building on November 25th, to hear Mr. Henry Broadhurst, M.P. The ladies too have played their part in the year's work, and a suc- cessful scheme to raise funds for party purposes took the form of a picturesque "British Isles Bazaar," which was opened by the Hon. Mrs. Emmett Barlow, M.P. It is gr atifying to be able to record that a Liberal-Labour alliance has been arrived at, for on November 28th, the Progressive Labour League, with Mr. J. Howes as the first president, came into being. Already the membership of working men is large and the organization promises to "become an important factor milocal politics. The municipal elections in November were most encouraging to Liberals, a notable victory being achieved in Howley Ward. From the point of view of municipal work, the year his been an important one. When 1904 dawned the oft-demanded inquiry into the trading transactions of the Corporation had commenced, and during the succeeding months a great deal of evidence was heard. 01 Thursday July 21st, in the absence of Mr. Norbury Williams, one of the Non-Trading Com- missioners, opportunity was taken to bring the in- vestigation to an abrupt close, and the Council Com- missioners (Messrs. L. Greening, T. Birchall, and A. J. JolleY) issued a report in which they stated that there was nothing to support the allegations of cor- ruption. The Non-Traders felt aggrieved at what they considered the premature closing of the inquiry, and held that further examination of the books was necessary. At their next meeting the Council decided that the inquiry must proceed, but Messrs. Greening, Birchall, and Jolley declined to act as Council Gommissioners, stating that the allegations ,made " rested on nothing more than frivolous gossip." Ultimately Councillors Wilkinson, A. Peacock,and Evans agreed to represent the Council on the Commission and the inquiry was resumed. On October 22nd the Commissioners pub- lished their respective reports which were singalarly contradictory. The Council Commissioners found that "there was no evidence of anything to be considered of an illegal or immoral character, or to prove that any member or official of the Corporation who •was a shareholder in a company trading with the Corporation attempted. to use his position in an improper manner." The Non-Traders' report was much more voluminous, and the Commissioners came to the conclusion that "the trading, direct or indirect, alleged at/the public meet- ing was amply proved. " Instances of questionable transactions were detailed. A fact significant of public opinion was that the Non-Traders achieved a striking victory in the ensuing bye-election in Town Hall Ward, Mr. W. Benson defeating Mr. A. J. Jolley by, 41 votes. The long-deferred widening of Warring on Bridge gave rise to several discussions in the Council Chamber. At the January meeting it was decided to widen the bridge by means of cantilevers on either side, but to this proposal there has been strong opposi- tion from Messrs. James Fairclough and Sons, who are large traders on the river and contend that the I projected alteration would impede navigation. The 1 firm offered to contribute £1,009 towards the cost of a scheme which they believe(' would be more suitable, and the General Purposes Committee decided that, as opposition was said to be threatened from various quarters,it would be desirable to defer the matter. At the December meeting of the Council, however, an amendment that the* widening by cantilevers be pro ceeded with was carried. Up to the present, howevers nothing has been done. Incidentally, it i interesting to recall that the Council at their Allarch meeting approved a motion by Councillor Magih to the effect that it was desirable that a bridge be con- structed across the Mersey at Howley, and that plans specifications, and estimates for a single-span iron girder bridge be prepared. No doubt in considera- tion of the additional heavy outlay which the improvement would involve, the subject has never been re-introduced.</p>
        <p>The litigation which had centred round the question of the dredging of the river by the Manchester Ship Canal Company took a rather alarming turn in March. The Company went to the Court of Appeal 'with the • object of obtaining a reversal of the judgment of Mr. Justice Byrne who held that the Company were bound to carry out their obligations with regard to the dredging of the river at Warring- tor according to the agreement embodied in an Act of Parliament obtained by the Company. The deci- sion of the Court of Appeal laid it down that the Corporation had no ground for action, but were bound by the arbitration clause in the agreement with the Ship Canal Company which stated that all disputes arising should be settled by reference to arbitration. The traders, who constituted the other plaintiffs in the action, were placed in an equally anomalous posiron. While the Judges agreed that they had the right of action, the Court held that they were not in a position to exsrcise that right until the Corporation had proceeded by arbitration. The Corporation and traders decided to appeal to the House of Lords, but happily matters assumed a more favourable complexion on the introduction of the Ship r Canal Bill, the object of which was to enable the company to raise the water level and to carry out various improvement works. The Corporation appeared in opposition to the Bill and as a result practicalty all the points raised in the action were conceded by the company. - - -</p>
        <p>- - The continuous efforts of Councillor Stott culminated in the adoption by the Council of a serious of standing orders making it compulsory that all contractors to the Corporation should pay all persons engaged on the work the trade union rate of wages. It has since been strongly rApresented that susil regulations would be harsh in their operation, and the orders have been suspended, in order that a special committee may go fully into the matter. Warrington's rates have continued their upward tendency, and in April the Council sanctioned an increase of 6d., making a total rate of 7s. 10d. in the Educational work has made considerable progress during the year, but the natural consequence has, of necessity. been increased calls upon the public purse. The estimates passed at the February meeting of the Education Committee required an education rate of 101 d Early inithe year the committee took an important step in adopting a complete educatioual scheme. When this is in full force, it will be possible for a child to pass from an infant s' school through the -various grades to the Grammar school or the higher school for girls, and on to the university. The secondary school is doing a useful work, more particularly in the training of young people for the teaching profession. Mr. A. Gow, the first headmaster, obtained a more impor- ' taut appointment at Blackburn, and he has been succeeded by Mr. E. S. A. Robson. It is, of course, eminently desirable that the Grammar school should form an important link in the educational system. At the beginning of June the governors of the institution decided to make application to the Board of Education for the recognition of the school as a secondary school. The effect of such a course would be that the school would be recognized as an educational institution in the town for higher educa- tion, and more advanced subjects than those taught at the secondary school would be taken. The Board of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>STOcKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOcKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>MEETING OF THE PAROCHIAL CONIMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee was held in the schools, Stockton Heath, on Thursday, when there were present—Messrs. Piling (in the chair), Mounfield, Whitley, Williams, Clarke, Fairhurst, Bouehier, Mclntyre, H. J. Westbrook, T. R. Westbrok, Welsby, Hallows, Warburton, Ashton (clerk), Swinton (surveyor),. and Farrington (inspector).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>A. PETITION.</head>
        <p>A. PETITION.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read an fiXtf,asively-signed petition from property owners and reiidente in Fairfielt-rd,asking th 3 Committee to reject plans for the erection of property fronting on the new road about to be made from Fairfield-road to Mill-lane. This property, they con- tended, if constructed as shown on the plans would result in the transformation of the twenty-foot passage in the rear of Fairfield-road into a huge cul-de-sac, which they urged would be an enormous nuisance and inconvenience, and would result in a deterioration in the value of the property in Fairfield-road. Mr. aallowspointed out that the plans did not show that the passage would be blocked at one end,and it was decided to take no action in the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>PAVING OF FOOTPATHS.</head>
        <p>THE PAVING OF FOOTPATHS.</p>
        <p>A longldiscussion ensued as to whether the footpaths in back streets should be paved with flags or tiles. The matter was ultimately ajourned. _ _ _</p>
        <p>A GOLDEN JUBILEE.—Messrs. Leo and Nightingale, the well-known firm of Advertising Contractors, News.- paper Correspondents, Shorthand Writers, Printers, and Lithographers, of Liverpool, whose name is familiar in every newspaper office of the country, celebrate this month the Half Century of their establishment. The business was started in a small way by Mr. Thomas Lee and the late Mr. Joseph Henry Nightingale at Swift's Court, Castle Street, in 1854, and as it progressed was removed to larger offices at 16, Castle Street. These premises becoming too small, the firm decided to purchase the entire building of 13 North John Street, to which they removed in 1871. In 1901 it was found necessary to make a further extension owing to the continued growth of the busi- ness and the addition of new departments. Hence there was erected an entirely new building at the rear of the principal office, and here are carried on the printing and lithographing departments under the most modern con- ditions as to machinery and the use of electric power. It is worthy of note that the senior of the firm, Mr. homas Lee, enjoys good health, and continues an, ctive oversight of the business, and that he has the satisfaction of seeing two younger generations, viz., a son and a grandson, also working towards the general weal.</p>
      </div>
