      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Coming Events. At Bewsey:road Wesleyan Church to-morrow the morning service will be taken by the Rev. R. Wilkins Rees who will preach a New Year's sermon. The annual Covenant Service will be held at 2.30, and in Ahe evening Mr. John Upson will preach. Warrington's annual Eisteddfod will be held on Monday, and as the day generally will be observed as a holiday there should be a large attendance through- out the day at the Parr Hall. The Sergeants of the Ist. V. 8., P.W.V., South Lancashire Regiment, will hold ttieir annual ball in the new quarters at the Drill Hall on Monday week. The members of the Warrington Butchers' Associa- tion will hold their annual ball on Thursday January 26th. The annual treat to the aged poor of the town will be given in the Parr Hall on Thursday next. At noon the old men will be entertained to dinner, whilst in the evening the aged dames will be provided with a tea and humorous entertainment. A watch-night service will be held at Wycliffe Church to-night at 11-15, which will be conducted by Mr. A. Corney—At Wyclifffe Church to-morrow morning the annual distribution of Bibles to the Catechumen 'Class will be made. A watch-night service will also be held at St. Paul's Church.</p>
        <p>NEVV. YEAR'S PRESENTS.-Try J. S. GARLAND'S, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington. Splendid selection.--Advt. .</p>
        <p>Musical Successes.</p>
        <p>The following pupils of Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bac., have been successful in passing the following examinations in music'recently held by Trinity College London, in Liverpool. Intermediate Pianoforte—Miss Alice Griffiths. Senior Piapoforte—Miss Jessie Farrar and Miss Gladys Atherton.</p>
        <p>LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Fatal Fall Downstairs. Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, held an inquest at Warrington yesterday on the body of Peter Tickle, (63) of 38, Hoyle street, who died on Wednesday from injuries received on Christmas Day. The evidence showed that the deceased, who was very feeble, was coming downstairs on Sunday afternoon when he slipped and fell to the bottom. Dr. Smith was sent for and on arrival found deceased had fractured one of his ribs. Pneumonia supervened, death ocenrring on Wednesday.—A verdict of accidental death was returned. UNDER THE CLOCK for Good Boon.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. 1. J. DOWN.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. 1. J. DOWN.</p>
        <p>The deep regret which we feel in announcing the death of Mr. Thomas J. Down, managing director and secretary of the firm of Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., Wilders. pool Brewery, will be shared by the inhabitants of Warrington in general. Not only has a familiar figure been removed, but the town has suffered the loss of one whose chief deltlght it was to encourage a love of music among all classes and to provide refined and elevating pleasure for his fellow townsmen. How well he succeeded in this commendable aim was shown by the wide and genuine esteem in which he was held. For many years Warringtonians have enjoyed the privilege of listening to frequent per- formances by his efficient orchestral and military hands, and Mr. Down, whose interest in the organiza- tions which he had founded and maintained never flagged, invariably figured as conductor. Of late the deceased gentleman had been in failing health, but not until last week was it anticipated that the end was so near. About seven \ weeks ago .he underwent an operation for a spinal affection in a Manchester nursing institution, and appeared to be progressing satisfactorily. Just when it was thought that he might with safety be removed to his residence at Underclyffe, Appleton, his condition became much worse. He was allowed to remain in the hospital and passed peacefully away at nine o'clock on Monday morning at the age of 63 years.</p>
        <p>I Not only as an enthusiastic and accomplished I musician but as a successful business man did Mr. IDown command the respect of a large circle. The son of the late Mr. Thomas Down, of Wilderspool, who held the post of farm bailiff for the late Sir Gilbert Greenall, he received his early education at Miss Goulden's school, Stockton Heath, and afterwards at Mr. Brindle's school, Grappenhall,and at Mr. Savage's, Warrington. On leaving the school he became articled to Mr. Sanders, miller, Northwich, but after the lapse of two years entered the service of Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co. His capabilities soon attracted the attention of the management with the result that his promotion was rapid. He worked his way up to the position of head brewer and evinced so sound a grasp of the workings of the concern that he ultimately received the more important appointment of managing director and secretary which he filled with credit up to the time of his death. It was, however, by reason of his musical work that Mr. Dawn was more widely known. He began his musical studies at the age of ten, his first tutor being the late Mr. Thomas Eaude, who was for many years organist at the Warrington Parish church His ability was not confined to any single instilment; 'for at an early age he learned to play on the piano, organ, violin, and accordion. For six years he acted as organist at the Bold-street Wesleyan Church, and during that period he formed a friendship with the late Mr. William Oakden, of the Royal Dragoons,fi om whom he derived much of his knowledge of theory and harmony. From an appreciative article written by Mr. H. P. Hughes, we learn that it was in 1883 that Mr. Down's string band was established, and so great a success did it prove, that the military band was founded three years later. Under Mr. Down's personal guidance each organization flourished, and -the combined member- ship now numbers 85. Mr. Dawn brought a love of music and a marked business aptitude to bear in his superintendence of the ventures to which so much of his time was willingly devoted, and his efforts hive been ably seconded by his son Mr. Arthur Down who has acted as his deputy when occasion has arisen. The whole of the instrumentalists have rallied round their conductor with the utmost en,'': and the result has been that the bands are unsurpassed' locally in e.fic:ency, and indeed, will compare most f ,voarably with similar organizations throughout the country. Some idea of the extent of the work- nay bg glined from the fact that the combined libra -s of thc: bands total over 1,000 pieces of the tuo- -.red description. Although Mr. Down paid due attantiou to ..:iassi.43.l works, he endeavoured to en t .-ntain all classewho listened to the band performaa,..vs, and many light and popular selections found places in , tha.. repettbire. The first public appearance of the string orchestra was on 'December 10th. 1884, at Stockton Heath, in aid of St. Thomas's Chore' h choir fund, while' the military band was introduced to a Warrington audience in April, 1887' at the Grand International Jubilee Bazaar herd In the Drill Hall in aid of the Hamilton-,trect clellools. The military band had the honour or- giving the fit perLrmance on the opening of the now baud staud iu ;1,, park on October Bth, 1892. It is almost unnecessary to remind Warring- tonians of the numerous entertainments which have been given for their enjoyment in the Park, or to emphasise the general appreciation of Mr. Down's thoughtfulness for those to whom music is so refresh- ing after a day's toil The help of the orchestra has been readily accorded at the annual concerts in aid of ' the Infirmary, and it is safe to say that their selections have greatly enhanced the success of those functions. Up to the end of 1903 the string band had given 32 public performances, and the military band 30—mak- ing the gratifying total of 71. It is a testimony to Mr. Down's charm of manner that for twenty years he was I able to keep two purely amateur organizations together, and it has always been pleasing to note the esftrit de corps existing among the members. As President of the Association of Bands since its in- ception in May, 1892, Mr. Down has taken a deep , interest in its progress, and to his conscientious work ' the success achieved is in a great measure due. In June 1887, Mr. Down was the recipient of an illumin- ated address and photograph from the military band, and his remarks on that occasion will now be read with interest as possessing a touching significance. Mr. Down said . "As far as lam concerned, I have done my best for , the band. I bave worked hard for it, and conscien- tiously. I have tried to do my work well, as far as I have known it; and it' is my intention—if my life is spared—to go on as energetically as I have in the past. • • . . I have long since learned that 'life is real, Ilife is earnest,' and if we are to succeed we must work with might and energy, and not allow our enthusiasm to drop at all. It has always been my desire, and it will be my endeavour, to make these meetings as plea- sant and agreeable to you as they are to myself. It is my heartfelt desire to so live amongst you, and my other friends, that I may continue to deserve the kind- ness and affection with which you have honoured me, i up to the present moment. It s my desire to so live that when I have finished my course, you will recollect me kindly, and that you will, perhaps, sometimes long for</p>
        <p>The touch of a vanish'd hand. And the sound of a voice that i still."</p>
        <p>Mr. Down leaves a widow and one son, Mr. Arthur Down, to whom the sympathy of all will be extended in their hour of bereavement. THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>In the quiet churchyard of St. Thomas's, Stockton Heath, the remains of the late Mr. Down were laid to rest at noon on Thursday. The whole village was in mourning, and the large attendance to witness the last rites was perhaps the most eloquent testimony to the respect in which the deceased gentleman was held. The pretty church, bright with decorations in honour of the Christmas festival, was filled, the congrega- tion including the Wilderspool employ&amp; of Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co. The cortege left Under- clyffe at twelve o'clock, headed by the orchestral and military bands, several members acting as bearers, At the church the choir was in attend- ance and met the mourners at the entrance. The service was conducted by the Rev. H. V. Pigot (vicar) assisted by the Rev. C. A. M. Evans. Psalm xc. was chanted, and the congregation joined in singing the illymn "Thy way not mine, 0 Lord." At the conclusion of the first part of the service, Mr. F. Rose, who pre- sided at the organ, played the Dead March in "Saul." The chief mourners were: Mr. Arthur Down (sou),Mr. T. J. Down (nephew),Messrs.E. J. Guest,John Guest,and George Guest (brothers-in-law), and Dr. Gornall (nephew). Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co. were represented by Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bar.., Messrs. R.I. Wynne Jones, John J. Whitley, and Samuel Robinson (directors), Messrs. Francis Maddock (New Brighton), Joseph H. Clarke, William B. Roberts, Frederick Howard, Walter Greenhouse, John Henry (Chester agent), James Evans (North Wales agent), William Butterworth (Crewe agent), Frederick Pearson, R. N. Foster, C. G. Stone, William Howard, W. A. Timperley, Frederick Broady Albert Broady, D. E. Watson, John Burrows, George 'Clarke, and Walter Howard; Messrs. Arthur Robinson, William Leather, John Turton, and C. H. Jolliffe (St. Helens office) ; Messrs. H. Lloyd Carter (solicitor, Carnarvon), W. T. Marshall (Northgate Brewery. Chester), E. R. Parry (Garston), B. N. White (Prestbury), T. E. Mullan (Manchester), William Harrison (Maccles- field). The representatives of the Warrington and District Licensed Victuallers's Association included : Messrs. J. Sampson Peers Brimelow, Ft. Shacklady, W. Chalmers, J. Lee: J. W. Plinston; J.'Shillcock, A. W. Paul, S. Wakefield, J. Rushworth, G. Lang, W. Teece,G. Wright, J. McClinton, G. Cooper, G. Simcock and W. Cherry; Wine and Beer Sellers' Association, Messrs. W. J. Shavv, .T. Wrench, B. Kenyon, R. Barlow, A. Whittaker, J. Roberts and W. Boothroyd. There were also representatives of the'Northwich and St. Helens Licensed Victuallers' Associations. Councillor J. Fulton Smith and Mr. W. Holbrook were present on behalf of the C nservative Association, and :Aldermen Francomb and</p>
        <p>Bennett and Mr. George Piling on behalf of the Con.. servative Club. Alderman Smethurst represented the Town Hall, Parks, and Baths Committee. Members of the Association of Bands in attendance included repre- sentatives of the Volunteer, Pioneer, League of the Cross, Borough, Crosfield's, Walker's, and Legh-street bands, with Mr. B. Wadsworth (secretary.) a The following past and present members of the ' Orchestral and Military Bands attended :—Mr. P. Cald- , well (for some years leader of the orchestra), F. Pendle- • bury (for some years leader of the military band, ' and at the same time a member of the orchestra) T. Hughes, J. Rudd, T. Smith, P. Brimelow, J. B. Cald- well, J. Taylor, A. Hampson, J. Whitham, H. P. Hughes, J. Burrows, W. Parkinson, R. Whitfield, J. Riches, W. Hipkiss (leader of the military band), J. Smith, F. Molyneux, P. Walsh, J. W. Plinston, R. Lowe, W. Cummins, J. Scott, F. Pearson, T. Meredith, J. Board- man, S. Appleton, A. Grounds, W. E. Ray, W. H. Pod- more, E. Lawton, W. Greenhouse, W. Knight, Buckard, E. Yorke, Ray, N. Taylor, Gornall, Peplow,lß.N.Forster (leader of the orchestra), T. Jackson, E. Balmford, A. Sutton, J. Knight, jun., Scott, jun., T. Clarke, G.Clarke, F. Warburton, T. Gilligan, T. Croughton, J. Raybould, Davenport, W. Hatton, W. Brough, H. Roberts, A. Bennett, Gorst, J. Shaw, J:ane, Combrery, J. Rudd, jun., R. E. Warburton, R. McEvoy, Kirkham, Isherwood, Brownhill and Kirkpatrick. Mr. Arthur Browne andiMr. Stanley Wright represented the Warrington Dramatic Society and Mr. P. Flannery the Northwich and Dis- rict Workingmen's Hospital Saturday Committee. Among others present were : The Hon. the Rev. C. F. Cross, Revs. Richard Greenall, and J. S. Hughes, Councillor Craik, Captain Waring, Dr. Parker, Messrs. John White, Henry Greenall, H. Beresford Melville, William. Owen, Segar Owen, Vero B. Davies, J. Burgess F. Monks, F. W. Monks, J. Monro, James Ilepherd, W. Richardson, David Garnett, W. Roberts, J. Moore Murray, J. Hesketh, W. Mclntyre, W. SP-cock, W. H. Bainbridge, E. Caldwell, A. J. \Valet, R. Garnett, W. H. Payton, Percy Randall, R. H. Pearson, F. Hickman, J. C, Thompson, C. W. Phillips, H. Ferguson, F'. Timperley, H. Isherwood, W. Parkinson, J, A.McGrego r, T. J. Lee, T. B irtles, E. Isherwood, J. Woods, G. Holford, H., F, Greenough, G. S. Pendlebury, J. Potter, J. Watson, J. Hallows, John Smith, J. Dixon, E. Bradshaw, I. Mills, S. W. Dallow, T. Stringer, J. Swinton, Joseph Taylor,J. Harding,E. Caldwell,Poter Caldwell,.l. Cooper' C. McDonald, R. Isherwood, W. Brereton, Joseph Dolan" and Thos. Armstrong. The only wreath on the coffin was one from Mrs. Down and Mr. Arthur Down. Other floral tributes were forwarded by: Lady Greenall, the office staff at Wilderspool Brewery, office staff at St. Helens Brewery, workmen at Wilderspool Brewery, indoor and outdoor servants at Underclyffe,Warrington Licensed Victuallers Association, Wine and Beer Sellers' Association, St. Helens and Widnes Licensed Victuallers' Association Warrington Association of Bands. Mr.Down's orchestra' the military band, Dr. and Mrs. Guest Gornall, Mr. and Mrs. Cyril Greenall, Mr. and Mrs. H. I. Wynne Jones, Mr. and Mrs. William Down, Mr. T. J. Down (nephew), Mr. J. J. Whitley, Mr. and Mrs.W. Owen, Mr. and Mrs. Sermr Owen, Mr. J. H. Clarke, Mr. and Mrs. W. B. Roberts, Mr. and Mrs. Edward Griffith, Mr. and Mrs. W. Richardson, Mrs. Voisey and Mrs. Geary, Mr. and Mrs. J. W. Plinston, Mr. and Mrs. Helsby, and Mrs. Riches, Mr. and Mrs. B. Kenyon, Mr. and Mrs. W. Greenhouse, J. Melbourne, Ltd., Mr. C, H. Jollifft, Rev. T. S. and Miss Tofts, Mr. C. G. Stone, Mr. Joseph Robinson, Mrs. Bennett and the Misses Gornall, Mr, S. Robinson, Mr. and Mrs. Louis Voisey, Mr. and Mrs.PeterChrimes, Mr. R. Garnett, Mrs. and Misses Westbrook. Mr. and Mrs. J. Hepherd, Mr. and Mrs. S. Wakefield, Mr. and Mrs. Hugh Fergason, Mr. E. R. Parry, Mr. J. Milnthorp, Mr. B. E. Ross and :qr. B. N. White (Liverpool Malt Company), Mr. T. E. Malian. The funeral arrangements were in the hands of Messrs. Robert Garnett and Son.</p>
        <p>CONSERVATIVE CLUB MENIBERS' TRIBUTE. At the annual meeting of the members of the Conservative Club on Thursday evening, the chairman (Alderman Francomb) feelingly alluded to the death of Mr. Down. There perhaps never was a kinder man in Warrington or even outside the town than Mr. Down. He had done an enormous amount of good in a very' quiet way. His one great hobly vi Ls music and he made use of that hobby for the benefit of any good institution. They knew what crowded audiences his band had attracted at the Parr Hall and Years before at the Public flail, when it performed at concerts in aid of the Infirmary. Without selecting any special religious demonstration, the services of Mr. Down and hi a band were always at the disposal of any class of people if their object was a right one. Mr. Down had been the means of helping so many charities that it would indeed be difficult to find another means of gaining that assist- ance. Alderman Francomb concluded by moving: The members of the Conservative Club desire to express their deep sense of the loss they have sustained in the death of Mr. T. J. Down, and to convey to his widow and son their sincere sympathy in their sad bereave- ment.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. G. IcDOwELL seconded. A man of greater integrity than Mr. Down it would have been impossible to find, and the charities of the town wotild miss him I very much. councillor JOHN Itomars supparted and alluded more particularly to the willingness with which Mr. Down and his band gave their services at concerts organized by the Workingmen's Committee in aid of the Infirmary. In addition, Mr. Down always gave them a donation. The resolution was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>Rev. J. Lowe and Mr. Wm,floughton•</p>
        <p>Sir,—The" Warrington Guardian" of Decem6er 17th 1901 has just c3me to my notice. Had I been in health Ishould no doubt have become sooner acquainted with the following. lam surprised to read that at a meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians, Mr. William Houghton, in the discussion concerning the instruction of the children at Cottage Homes, Padgate, has made an insinuation about me as contemptible as it is cowardly. It is reported that "Mr. Houghton seemed to have something very delicate to say on the question (that is, concerning my appointment as Catholic instructor) and did not wish to say it publicly. He told Mr. Mather that if the question were opened he should say some- thing which would cause them to modify their decision, and in going about the matter that way be was trying to avoid friction. He could show reasons for modyifying the Committee's decision." Why, then, prolong the agony ? Were ladies' ears too delicate for his disclo- sures, or gentlemen's brains too dense to qnderstand ? The very thing I desire is that what' you (Mr. Houghton) have to say you will say publicly. What startling revelation is there in store? It is awaited with calmness, and the sooner I hear it,the sooner I shall be glad to have the power to act. To-day I visited the Padgate schools and the neighbourhood for an elucidation of the mystery. I can find none. For more than ten years I have been gratuitously attending the Padgate Cottage Homes, and I throw•down openly the challenge that any act or word of mine has been other than will bear the closest investigatien, and re- ceive the commendation of every right-minded person. Has bigotry been aroused becausel have attempted to give my co-religionists a recognised standing? No worthy member of the Church of England could or would blame me for so acting. Glad am Ito say that 1 I have the esteem, and even the friendship of ivany in the neighbourhood who ate not of my religion tend consequently am able to speak with confidence. What then did arouse your ire, Mr. William Houghton, and cause you to make- an insinuation against my fair name, precious Ito me not merely as a gentleman, but also as .a priest? ' You will have no need to whisper any base Insinuation or accusation the next time the representatives of the people meet. No, sir, as we gladly expect, you will boldly stand forth as the advocate of law and morality, expose the wicked, and hurl the thunders of your eloquence against the transgressor.-1 am, sir, yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>(REV.) J. LOWE.</p>
        <p>St. Peter's Woolston, December 24th, 1904.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>MINISTER AND POLITICAL CLUBS. --- Alderman Francomb Replies to Criticisms. \</head>
        <p>A MINISTER AND POLITICAL CLUBS. --- Alderman Francomb Replies to Criticisms. \</p>
        <p>Alderman Francomb presided over the annual meet- ing of members of the Warrington Conservative`Associa- tion which was held at the club in Sankey-street on Thursday evening. The attendance included Mr. G. Pilling (hon. secretary), Mr. William Owen (hon. treasurer), Councillor John Roberts and Mr. NV. Holbrook (Conservative agent.) Mr. OWEN reported that although the past year had been a trying one for clubs they had weathered it very well, and they still had money in hand. The statement was accepted. The CHAIRMAN said the success of the year had realized their anticipation, and the new manager and manageress had given every satisfaction. One of :heir local clergymenjwas recently speaking very severely aboutclubs. He (Alderman Francomb) did not know much about other clubs of the town, but he knew that the rev, gentleman could walk into that club at any time, and he did not think he would lib ashamed of any- thing he saw going on. (Hear, hear.) He (Aldezman Francomb) never saw any gambling there, and he was sure the club was one of the most temperate places he knew where such a large body of people assembled. He would rather like that minister to come and havea look round without giving them notice. He was sure he would then apologise and say that the Conservative Club, at any rate, ought not to share any abuse from him or anyone else. Mr. GEORGE PILLING resigned from the committee and Mr. Davenport was elected in his place. The other members of the committee are: Alderman Francomb, Councillor Roberts, Messrs. W. Owen, D. Cooper, R. Chadwick, W. H. Bellian, C. G. McDowell, C. Lee, J. Thornton and J. Streetle. Mr. G. Pilling resigning,Mr. Henry Collin was elected secretary., Mr. Owen was elected treasurer, , Votes of thanks were accorded to Mr. Pilling and Mr, Owen for their past services, and Alderman F:ancomi was thanked for presiding.</p>
      </div>
