      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>• ASSAULT ON WORKHOUSE OFFICIALS. At the Warrington Police Court, on Tuesday, before Alderman Hutchinson (presiding), Dr. Fox, and Mr. C. Jackson Holmes. Ernest William Holdin, last from the Workhouse, was charged with having been drunk i and disorderly n the Workhouse on Monday evening, and also with assaulting William Follows, labour master, and John Constable, porter. Constable said the prisoner came in shortly after seven o'clock in a very drunken condition and could not stand. He acted very violently and wanted to fight any officer in the place. Witness -tried to persuade him to be quiet, but he kicked witness and also the labour master. He had been in the workhouse for several weeks. William Follows also gave evidence as to seeing the prisoner in a very drunken state. He acted like a mailman, kicking out right and left. He was not allowed to mix with the other inmates.—Prisoner was sent to gaol for one month with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>CROSFIELD'S FIRE BRIGADE.</head>
        <p>CROSFIELD'S FIRE BRIGADE.</p>
        <p>Annual Dinner.</p>
        <p>The members of the fire brigade connected with Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., held their annual dinner at the Lion Hotel on Monday evening, when they spent a very enjoyable evening. There was a full attendance of the men who did full justice to the repast which was served by Mr. Lee. Over the subsequent proceedings Mr. Gallon, superintendent of the brigade, presided, being support- ed by deputy superintendent Mr. J. Flood, third officer Mr. R. Whitley, and secretary Mr. J. Houlden. The usual loyal toasts were heartily drunk. SECRETARY'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY presented a report which showed that the numerical strength of the Brigade was 50 officers and men, whose appliances consisted of two steam fire engines, three hose carts, two miles of hose, a fire escape and pompier ladders and life belts. During the past year the Brigade had hela 120 drills and had assisted at fires at thO works of Messrs. Pochin's and Messrs. Monks Hall and Co., at both of which the men responded cheerfully to the call. THE FIRM.</p>
        <p>The toast of tb e firm was proposed by Mr. T. KNOW LES, who, referring to the conditions of labour, quoted words used by the Rev. John Wakeford who said :—"The ,coming in of the limited liability company has dehumanized employment, and we must no longer speak of employers as 'heads' and the workmen as 'hands," inferring that employers regarded their workmen as mere human machines. (Applause.) Whatever might be their opinion with regard to that question, he (Mr. Knowles) thought they would all agree upon this, that if all employers of labour were to take the same active interest in the social and educational welfare of their workpeople as did Messrs. Crosfield, they would hear a great deal less of these complicated questions, which were the disturbing elements of labour to-day. (Hear,hear) Employers all over the country were beginning to see that it paid them to look after the social and intellectual interests of their working men. There was placed upon them a moral obligation, which must in the end tend to the success and prosperity of a firm, and so they would agree with him that they were pleased to see their own firm take the initiative. The wishes of the directors were, so far as the workpeople were concerned, falling into line in trying to make the firm a model industry. No section of the work had contributed so largely, so enthusiastically, and so willingly to maintain the stan- dard of excellence which was required as the fire brigade, led by their excellent and most worthy chief, Captain Gallon. (Applause). The toast was heartily drunk. Mr. J. WILLIAMS, in proposing "The Fire Brigade," spoke of the great value of such organizations, and also of the value of a knowledge of ambulance work. Captain GALLON, on rising to respond, was received with hearty applause. He remarked that as a fire brigade they existed to protect the works, and for the purpose of educating the surrounding people with the idea of fire prevention to such an extent that it was impossible to have a conflagration. So far as this brigade was concerned they had done very well in that respect, because twelve months had passed without an outbreak. (Applause.) Alluding to competitions, he said they had a limited body of men to draw from at the works, and it was doubtful whether they could get any person out of the works of quite the right physique to win a cup, but next year they meant to rally round, and to do their best to bring home a few cups. They were fourth at, Leicester, and the people who had the greatest experi- ence told them that their own brigade did the best drill of the evening. (Applause.) They did not get the cup, but he hoped to be able to have some trophy in front of him to show that they had a remarkably good year. (Applause.) They intended to go in for escape drill so that in the event of a person being cut off in a high building owing to a fire they would be able to effect A rescue. In conclusion, he said the brigade was able to turn out very quickly—two minutes any time—and he did not think there were many private brigades in the kingdom that could do better. They had had several flattering notices from some of the fire brigade journals as to their smartness, and they wished to continue in that direction. (Applause.) Second Officer Flciod proposed "The old members," and Mr. George Gleave responded, During the evening other toasts were submitted, in- cluding that of "Our guests," and the health of the Chairman,and songs were given by MessrsiGallon, T. Cleave, R. Morris and Lawless, and concertina solos by the Brothers Brobbin. Mr. Bert Smith was the accom- panist.</p>
        <p>CHAPEL V. PUBLIC HOUSE. The county coroner (Mr. S. Brighouse) held an inquest on Tuesday in a chapel at Marshall's Cross, St. Helens, and commenting on the coldness and discomfort of the place, said the one advantage about meeting in public-house rooms was that they were always warm and comfortable.</p>
        <p>No Breakfast Table complete without</p>
