      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>BISHOP'S CAROL.</head>
        <p>THE BISHOP'S CAROL.</p>
        <p>BY FERGT:TS HUME,</p>
        <p>Author of "The Mystery of a Hansom Cab," "Madame Midas," "Harlequin Opal," "The Nameless City," "The Dwarf's Chamber," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>"Have you finished the Carol, Mr. Horn P "</p>
        <p>"At least a week ago, Miss Austin. It will be sung here to-night. I have told the singers to come round."</p>
        <p>"In two or three hours, I suppose ?" said the girl, glancing at the clock. "How delightful I I love Christmas carols on Christmas Eve."</p>
        <p>"My carol will be a swan-song, Miss Austin. You know that in a day or so I leave England on mission work?"</p>
        <p>Winifred looked down, unable to reply to the questioning expression in his eyes. They asked eloquently if she, above all others, would regret his departure from Englehur.t. village. "I am sorry you are going," she said at length. "The vicar will miss you dreadfully." "And you ?" aiked the young man, his eyes still on her.</p>
        <p>She turned the question with a gay laugh. "I Oh! of course I shall snips you. Life is dull here, and you have done much to brighten mine." "I can pay you back the compliment, replied the curate, earnestly. "Believe me, I carry away a precious memory of your--"</p>
        <p>Before he could complete the sentence Mr. Vaughan entered. Winifred's uncle was a saturnine man, with a hard face and a stern mouth. EV.I3 the influence of the season could not soften his harsh nature. He looked at the couple, and Winifred winced as his cold eyes rested upon her and Michael Hem. Certainly the curate was seated very close to her, and the expression of his face—which Vaughan noted—was that of a lover. With intuitive feeling that her uncle misjudged the situation, Winifred moved hastily to the window and made a careless remark about the beauty of the moonlight on the snow. "Yes," observed Hem, guessing her nervousness. "This is a real, old-fashioned Christmas. Snow and holly, and carols, and—"</p>
        <p>"And sentimentality," finished Mr. Vaughan. "You talk of the Christmas invented by Dickens. As a parson, Hem, you no doubt approve of his sickly rubbish. I don't. Why should we open our hearts and our purses at this particular season to people whom we hate all the rest of the year ? " - -</p>
        <p>"Uncle! Uncle I We should hate no one I" "Without cause, you should add, Winifred. Bah 1 Hatred is a healthy quality when properly exercised."</p>
        <p>"Do you exercise it, Mr. Vaughan ? " asked the curate, mildly. "No, sir. I am absolutely indifferent to my fellow-mortals, with their selfish aims and grasping ambitions. Christmas does not improve their natures; why should it weaken mine ? " "You are wrong, Mr. Vaughan. The solemnity of this blessed season softens many a man. It teaches us to forgive and forget." The scoffer turned his hard gaze on the curate. "Some things cannot be forgiven," he said; "some things cannot be forgotten." Winifred shivered by the fire and spread her hands over the blimp. She impulsively raised her head to reply, but Vaughan's grim look quelled her. The curate, scarcely knowing what answer to make, wisely held his peace. A silence ensued. The gloom of Vaughan's presence darkened the room. It seemed less like Christmas while he stood there.</p>
        <p>Hem's intention was to become a missionary, and while waiting his opportunity he had accepted the curacy of Englehurst parish. Here the meeting with Winifred Austin played havoc with his aspirations. He began to think that if she would marry him, he would be content to settle down in England, and leave the conversion of the heathen to those whose affections were less engaged. The girl, as he knew, had a hard life with an uncle who had small sympathy with her gracious youth. Michael yearned to take her from this dismal house and enshrine her in his heart. But he had never been sure enough of her feelings towards him to put his fortune to the test. And, indeed, she treated him in a most variable manner. At one time she would be kind and encouraging; at another her cold demeanour made him doubt if she even cared for his company. Yet, in spite of this weather-cock behaviour, he felt that she was not averse to his wooing. Long ago he would have put the matter beyond doubt by a frank proposal, but for this intangible something—he knew not what—which held them apart.</p>
        <p>Lately there had been rumours in the parish that Miss Austin had a lover, or, as the gossips put it, that she was keeping company. She had been seen, so ran the report, walking at untoward hours with a stranger and hanging affectionately on his arm. Hem n was discomfited, but doubtful. He had met no one at Vaughan Park who was paying court to Winifred; nor could he see in her manner any sign that she loved and was loved. Therefore he dis- believed the gossip. Yet he still hesitated to declare his love. But on this Christmas Eve, although he knew it not, circumstances were forcing him to decide his future.</p>
        <p>An offer had come from a missionary society which, if accepted, entailed his leaving for the South Seas immediately after Christmas. He gave notice to his vicar, and let it be known in the parish, that he contemplated a Polynesian exile. By this action, he hoped that Winifred would chew if she cared for him sufficiently to prevent his departure. But as she displayed no emotion and expressed no more than conventional regret, he began to think that he had been living in a fool's paradise. To-night he intended to know the worst or the best, and indeed was on the point of learning his fate from her own lips when Mr. Vaughan entered. The conversation relative to Christmas ensued, and after the silence it was the new-comer who spoke. _ _</p>
        <p>"There are some people," said Mr. Vaughan, "who cannot be forgiven. I speak of those who disgrace the name they bear, who are sunken in profligacy and sin. Can such be pardoned, Mr. Hern ?</p>
        <p>"Surely," replied the curate, "for such weak souls came Christ into the world; and at this holy time of His Birth no human being should dare to withhold a pardon that our Master Himself would have granted."</p>
        <p>"I can't conquer my feelings to that extent, Mr. Hem. lam a man, and I act like a man. To me Christmas means nothing—in that way." "Heaven help you, then, Mr. Vaughan."</p>
        <p>The old man looked at the young one with con- temptuous cynicism. and was about to retort sharply. But a glance at the agitated face of Winifred altered his mind. He left the room and the young people together. Immediately the girl addressed Michael Hem. "Oh, thank you for those words," she said, eagerly. "If yon could only induce my uncle to forgive— to pardon ! - "Whom is he to forgive ? " asked Hem, for he was unacquainted with the private circumstances that made Vaughan so bitter.</p>
        <p>Winifred opened her mouth, but shook her head and closed it again with a hopeless gesture. Lean- ing against the window she looked out into the wonderful white world, sleeping so peacefully in the moonlight. riliern saw that she was un- willing to speak, that he had touched upon a tender point. With swift tact he changed the subject. "Would you like to hear my carol ?" he asked, moving to the piano. "No, no," she protested, "that would spoil it. I would rather wait until midnight for the choir. Then your swan-song will be rendered in a way worthy of it." "That is if it be worthy," laughed the curate "but I think it will be well sung. William Barter, our chief alto, is coming to me in an hour to run through it. My swan-song--" He stopped abruptly and looked significantly at Winifred. "Are you sorry, Miss Austin ? " "That you are going away? Indeed am. I lose my best friend in you, Mr. Hem." Carried away by the pathos of her voice and the beauty of her face, the curate no longer hesitated. "Need I go---Winifred ? " "Mr. Hern !"</p>
        <p>A rosy flush made her face still more lovely, and her eyes became lustrous. Yet she did not look unkind ; she did not reprove his eager words with cold looks, lie took his courage in both hands and spoke out.</p>
      </div>
