      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, December 31, 1904.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 31, 1904.</p>
        <p>"Winifred, you must know that I love you. Long, long ago I should-have told you so, but that I thought someone "There is no one else!"</p>
        <p>"Winifred!" he caught her hand. "Then the goss p about you walking with a stranger, and--"</p>
        <p>- ..1 have Walked with no stranger," she said, proudly, but with an uneasy glance. "There is no one else "—her 'voice dropped—" Michael." _</p>
        <p>"My darling. And as she lay in his arms he whispered: "You love me?" - -</p>
        <p>_ "I have always loved you," she murmured, and their lips met.</p>
        <p>The happiest man in Englehurst left the house an hour later. He had been accepted; he had promised to give up his missionary enterprise, and the consent of Mr. Vaughan was to be asked when the Christmas celebrations were ended. Winifred loved him after all, but, as she whispered, she had not been certain if her love was returned. There- fore had she adopted that changeful demeanour which had so puzzled the curate. ,Michael could not understand, so he assured her, how she could have misinterpreted his attentions. "I am but a clumsy lover, dearest, w he said, when they parted; "but now that we know each other's hearts, you must teach me to improve." And he left with her kiss warm on his lips. William Barter waited at Mr. Bern's lodgings and wondered at the joyous expression of his face. The lad was a chubby imp of thirteen with the voice of an angel, and the disposition of a monkey. Here rehearsed the carol, and made inquiries about the other singers. According to William they were all perfect, and proposed to sing the carol first under the windows of Mr. Vaughan's house. "Not that he'll give much, sir," said William, thoughtfully; "but Miss Winifred is always kind."</p>
        <p>"My dear lad, you must not look to earn money for your singing on this sacred night. It is Christ- mas time, William, and we must give freely of what we have—my carol and your voices." William nodded, although he did not approve of tramping the midnight snow for nothing. He and his fellows loved cakes and ale, and for these mainly they sang. However, William was too astute a youth to contradict his ecclesiastical superior, and changea the conversation by detail- ing an adventure. He was at once dramatic, and, at the outset, incoherent. "There was two of 'em," said William; "two of 'em in the churchyard three nights ago when I was going home to mother." "Two what, my boy ? " asked the puzzled curate. William scratched his rough head. "Well, sir, I thought at first as how they were ghosts, being in a graveyard. My hair stood up, and I was as white as the snow. Aye, that I was. My blood freezed."</p>
        <p>"Nonsense, boy' There are no such things as ghosts." • "And they weren't, sir," said the lad, triumphantly. "I was that afraid as I could have run away when they two came past me, but I dropped behind a tall white tombstone, sir. Then I saw they were humans. But why Miss Winifred should-"</p>
        <p>Hem n remembered the rumours, remembered also the denial of his beloved, and angrily contradicted the boy. "You must have been dreaming, my good William," he said, with a forced laugh.</p>
        <p>"No, sir. I was wide awake and much afeared till I saw they was humans. The moon was up, and the snow was white. They passed quite close, Miss Winifred and some'un I never saw before." "What was the man like ? " asked Hem, a sick doubt creeping into his heart. William became still more dramatic.</p>
        <p>"Tall and dark, with awful eyes. He wsts like the picture of Apollyon in the Pilgrim's Progress. Oh, Mr. Hem," said William, in an awed whisper, and a glance around, "could he have been Him I"</p>
        <p>With a good-natured laugh, Michael took the lad by the shoulders and put him outside. "You are a young donkey, William. Mis, Winifred does not meet strangers in the churchyard. No doubt you dreamt all this after a heavy supper. Go and sing my carol."</p>
        <p>But Hem knew that the lad had not been dreaming. The report was circumstantial enough, and fully bore out the rumours of Winifred's secret meetings. In spite of his love, of her denial, he began to feel a certain mistrust. Why had she told him an untruth ? Without hesitation she had said that there was no one else, and she had denied the reported meetings with a stranger. Yet an hour later and an unprejudiced witness contra- dicted her story. "What can I do ? " muttered the curate, with anguish in his soul. "If I ask her a second time it will shew that I mistrust her. Moreover, what she denied once she may deny again. Yet if it is true —if she really does meet this man, she cannot care for-- No," be broke off, thrusting the doubt from him, "I don't believe it. She ]ores me—me only. There must be some explanation." There might have been, but he could think of none. In the first frenzy of what he believed to be his betrayal, he was almost inclined to leave the parish as had been his intention before Wini- fred accepted him. All was arranged. When he arrived in London he was to sail for Australia, en route for the South Seas, within a week. He need never see her fair false face again. Yet on second thoughts he resolved that he would give her every chance of explaining. He would call, on that very Christmas Eve, and repeat the tale of William Barter. If necessary, the lad could be called upon, when the carol was ended, to tell his adventure in her presence. It seemed cruel that he should doubt, but so much was at stake. He must know the truth at all costs. If Winifred loved another; if she met that other secretly—perhaps out of fear of her uncle—then she must go with that other. He would resign her. Thus, in a moment, did Hem's happiness change to sorrow. But he never flinched from his determination to learn the bitter truth.</p>
        <p>Thus resolvea ijo wall —a towards Vaughan Park, retracing the way he had lately trodden so joyfully. It was close upon midnight, but, on account of the carol-singing, Winifred could not have retired. The night was calm, the sky cloudless, and myriad stars scintillated like jewels. As his feet crunched the frozen snow, Hem thought how at an earlier hour those brilliant stars would have turned his medi- tations to that Star of Bethlehem, which close upon two thousand years ago had flamed in the Syrian sky. But these new doubts clogged the wings of his fancy. He forgot the season, he forgot the carol. He could think only of Winifred. Was she false or true? He asked his shadow, black in the moonlight, if she were true or false? Then he laughed at the folly of the fantasy. But there was no mirth in his laughter. The park was a waste of arctic desolation. Snow everywhere, and trees gaunt and black under the hard moon. Oak and ash, sycamore and elm; their naked boughs diapered the gleaming whiteness with queer shadows. The church bells were not yet ringing, nor had the singers arrived. Bern passed through the -tall iron gates, and paused in the shadow of the brick wall. He heard voices—a man's and a woman's. And the woman's voice was that of her be loved.</p>
        <p>With a dry mouth, a fast-beating heart, and his brain on fire, the curate shrank against the wall where the shadow was almost RA black as his own suspicions. He had no compunction in listening. The position was too critical for him to be over nice. •</p>
        <p>"You must leave Englehurst at once, dear," Winifred was saying to the man whose arm she clasped, "for my sake." "Shall I not see you again ?" he asked, softly. "Perhaps, on some other occasion. If Mr. Hera knew----" ".11a. I ha! Poor llern ! I wonder what he would say ?"</p>
        <p>"Say!'" echoed the girl. "lie would leave me. I don't want him to do that, George. He believes that lam all that is good and true. Oh, Heaven help me, should he learn the truth !" Michael shuddered, and with difficulty suppressed a groan. This callous speech chewed bow false was her heart. Unable to bear more he was on the point of revealing himself, when the sound of voices made him pause. They were those of the Englehurst choir, and shortly a number of men and boys passed through the gates on their way to the house. One of the lads—if was William— broke into the carol. The old-fashioned, uncouth words which Hem had set, sounded quaint and sweet:</p>
        <p>"Say, why in the Heav'ns are ringing, Songs celestial, wondrous sieet:? 'Tis triumphant angels .singing, • While the Star of Hope is bringing Wise men three the Babe to greet. God is with us. Sing Noel! " Now is born Emmanuel." As the company trudged over the frozen snow, the listener heard his false love hurriedly address his companion. "I roust go at once, George. Uncle will expect me to hear the carol." "Hern's, carol." laughed the stranger. "Don't</p>
        <p>go yet, Winnie. if sou really love me you will come with me to-night."</p>
        <p>"I do love you. Have I not proved my love? But go with you—where ? "</p>
        <p>"lo Austialia. We can easily catch the mid- night train. I have plenty of money and can buy what you want in London. Come, Winnie." "George, I cannot. You must come down here tl see me again. Be cautious, and no one will suspect our meetings." "Least of all the trusting Hem. Well, let him live in his fool's paradise. Winnie ? " He detained her.</p>
        <p>"No, no ! The carol is beginning. Let me go, George." "Not without a kiss," and he snatched her to his breast to press thwe lips Hem had had thought were all his own. The unhappy man closed his eyes, sick at heart. When he opened them the lovers had parted. Winifred was speeding up the leafless avenue, and the man she called George a moment later passed through the gates. Only Hera remained, leaning against the ivy-covered wall.. And as in a dream he heard the distant music of the carol:</p>
        <p>"Say, Who is this Babe so tender, At Whose feet the wise men fall ? 'Tis the Christ Who doth surrender He,av'n on high, that He may render Earth back to the Lord of All."</p>
        <p>Michael felt his way blindly along the wall, seeking for the gates that he might go forth into the wide world. It was needless to ask her ques- tions now. He knew the truth, and the truth meant the loss of joy, of hope, of faith, of love. Behind, lay happiness; before, awaited him exile and eorrow. The bells of Englehurst rang in the Birth of Christ. Their music mingled with the music of the carol. But there was no music in his heart. Only a dull aching, an everlasting sorrow. "On earth peace, good-will toward men." That was the message of the bells: but to him they foretold unrest and mistrust. With Job he cried in his affliction, and the tiry of his broken heart went up to the midiaight sky. "For the thing which I greatly feared is come upon me, and tiPat which I was afraid of is come unto me."</p>
        <p>The Bishop of the Islands was seated by the fire in a reflective frame of mind. A month befera he had returned from his diocese on the fringe of the Empire after an absence of twenty years. For a few weeks he was staying with his married sister in South Kensington, and Mrs. Allen was bent upon lionising her unwilling brother. On this particular evening she and her husband were going to a dinner-party, but in spite of her persuasions the Bishop refused to join them. "It is Christmas Eve," he said, shaking his head. "I am sure, my dear Eliza, you would not have a Bishop go to an entertainment on Christmas Eve. Even a Colonial Bishop has his limitations." "There is something in what you say, Michael," responded Mrs. Allen, giving in unwillingly. "But you are so melancholy that I only asked you to come in the hope of cheering you." "Would this particular dinner cheer me ? " asked Hem, smiling. Allen the husband laughed and rattled his keys. "It would rather have the opposite effect, Bishop," said he, jovially. "It is merely a day dinner. You are wise to remain at home on this bitter night. I should prefer to stay myself, only Eliza will make me go." "I know what is good for you," said Mrs. Allen, reprovingly. "Pray make yourself at home, Michael. The children have gone to bed, so you will not be bothered. I daresay you find it chilly after the warmth of the South Seas, so keep up a good fire. Ah, my dear Michael, how I wish you had a wife to look after you. Men are so careless." Bishop Hera sighed. Not even Eliza knew why he kept single, and he did not intend to enlighten her now. She had always been curious, and for years had bombarded him with letters. But Michael had invariably turned her questions with a laugh, as he did now. _ _ _</p>
        <p>- " • ' "'Who would take an old man like me ? " he joked. "Old? Why you are not yet fifty—a very young Bishop. However, we must be off. Keep up a good fire, see that you have everything you want, &amp;c."; and Mrs. Allen, after rattling again through her directions, bustled away on the arm of her ponderous husband. The Bishop was not sorry when he heard the carriage drive away. Eliza, he thought, had certainly become a rather domineering person. Then the good Bishop rebuked his uncharitable thoughts when he remembered how warmly Eliza had welcomed him.</p>
        <p>So this was how Michael Hern, formerly curate of Englehurst and now Bishop of the Islands, found himself alone on this Christmas Eve. The room was cheerful, the fire worthy of the season, and he was enjoying a well-earned rest. Yet he was uneasy and unhappy. With a sigh he walked to the window, wondering why he felt so depressed, It was snowing heavily, and the returned exile looked dreamily into the dark' night which the whirling flakes made still darker. Twenty years of absence from wintry lands gave the sight a certain novelty in his eyes. He recalled the brilliant green of the islands, the Pacific seas that swept the long beaches, that thundered on the coral reefs, and saw in his mind's eye the vivid, picturesque life that existed amidst tropical heats, and under eternally blue skies. It was a far cry from that Eden under Capricorn to this wild and stormy island invaded by mists and snows. -</p>
        <p>The sight of tge snowy street, the bitter chill in the air, and the decorations of holly and mistletoe brought back the memory of that terrible Christmas E,v.e when he had staggered broken- hearted through the gates of Vaughan Park. He recalled his feverish anxiety to put leagues and leagues between his false love and himself, the journey to London, the long voyage, the years of labour amongst the heathen, and the good fortune that had attended his efforts. Rung by rung he had mounted the ladder of preferment until at a comparatively early age he found himself back in the motherland, Bishop of the Islands. "Heaven has been very good to me," thought Hun, as he looked out at the falling snow. have prospered beyond my deserts. Yet-" He sighed sadly, for he was a lonely man, and the memory of the past lay heavily upon him this night. The fire crackled, the flakes fell faster, yet the Bishop still stared out into the black night pricked with the yellow flames of the street lamps. Occa- sionally a passer-by would glance up at the white face and whiter hair of the man who watched with such sad eyes. Bishop Hera was prematurely old. He had never recovered the shock of Winifred's falseness. And to-night the memory of that bitter sorrow was peculiarly vivid. Why should this be?</p>
        <p>"No doubt because it is Christmas Eve," said Hem, answering his unspoken thought. "I have heard nothing from Englehurst all these years. Ido not know if she is alive or dead. I have never sought to know. No! No!" He dropped the blind and covered his face. "I must not think of her or of the past. Doubtless she is married to— to—him. And I--" He caught a sight of his wrinkled face in a near mirror and walked slowly back to the tire shaking his white head. Suddenly he stopped. A woman was singing out- side in a high, sweet voice. Probably some street singer trying to earn a few coppers. Out of com- pliment to the season she sang a carol ; and in the cheerful room Michael could hear the words plainly. Ah! did he not know-them only too well: "Say, why in the Heavins are ringing, Songs celestial, wondrous sweet? 'Tis triumphant angels singing, While the Star of Hope is bringing Wise men three the Babe to greet. God is with us. Sing Noel! Now is born Emmanuel."</p>
        <p>-heart almost stopped beating; his mouth was dry, his hands were cold, and he stood where he was as though turned into stone: It was his carol this woman sang. the carol he had last heard at Vaughan Park on the saddest night of his life. How came it that a stranger knew of it? Still singing the woman moved up the street. Her voice was dying away in the distance, and unless he found her, he would never know how she came to sing: that long dead song of his youth. Its every word, its every note, was laden with the sorrows of an unforgettable past. The Bishop was a man of few words and quick action. In a moment he was out in the hall and struggling into his overcoat. The astonished foot- man saw him clap his hat on his head, open the door, and dash out into the freezing, bitter night. He could not understand what madness had seiied upon so usually stately a prelate. The Bishop found himself facing a cutting wind. Before him the dark form of a woman flitted over the white snow and under the yellow gleam of the lamps. The street was almost deserted, and he hurried after her as quickly as he was able. She paused under a lamp at the top of the square and began to sing. Again it was his carol—that old quaint carol so filled with heartbreaking 'memories.</p>
        <p>"Heaven help me !" thought the Bishop, as be paused near the singer. The lamplight fell 'on her worn black dress, on her thin shawl, and on a face much changed from its early loveliness. But marred as it was by the cruel years he knew it only too well.</p>
        <p>"Winifred!" he gasped, and fell back a pace 'Winifred!"</p>
        <p>She broke off the carol abruptly and stared at lira in amazement. Who was the man with the ined face, the white hair, and the drooping thoulders that addressed her so familiarly ? 3carcely seen in the semi-gloom she failed to recognise him. "Who are you? You know my name. I do not know you." "Winifred!" . murmured the Bishop, and, rcn3oving his hat, he stood forward so that the tight should fall on his sadly changed face.</p>
        <p>The sob in his voice arres-ted hei:- attention, and when she saw him more clearly, a vague memory nrept into her frightened eyes. Then his appeal- ing look, his agitation, his pathetic repetition of her name enlightened her. "Oh, Mr. Hera!" she murmured, and covered her face.</p>
        <p>"Come," said the Bishop, taking her hand, "we must talk in a quieter place. Will you take me to —oh, my poor Winifred!" he burst out. "How have you fallen so low? Has that scoundrel deserted you?"</p>
        <p>She remoLved her hands and looked at him in surprise. 'hat scoundrel!" she repeated. "Whom do you mean?" "George. I cTo not know his other name. I speak of the man who urged you to fly with him on that Christmas Eve-"</p>
        <p>"When you left me," she flashed out. Her voice sank and she passed a thin hand across her hollow cheeks. "I do not understand. George! Yes! But what do you know of George, Mr. Hem n ? "</p>
        <p>The 'man's head drooped. "I overheard your conversation by the Park gates, Winifred. Oh, why did you say that you loved me when it was to him you gave your heart ? " "To that man—George?" She paused. that why you left me?" "I thought you were false. And it is twenty years since," groaned the man; "twenty years of exile for me and of misery for you. To-night I heard you sing my carol. I came--" "Your carol," she repeated, tenderly. "Is it that which has brought us together again, Michael ? " He looked at her in astonishment, not under- standing the frame of mind which made her speak so. She saw his surprise, and touched his arm entreatingly. "Willyou come to my home! "</p>
        <p>"Was</p>
        <p>"I will come," repliea the Bishop, obediently. "But tell me how is it that you are singing in the-"</p>
        <p>"When we reach home," was all her answer, and she glided away silent and ghost-like. Indeed, Horn could scarcely believe that she was not a ghost, so unexpected and strange had been the meeting. There was no anger in his heart now. She had betrayed him indeed; but here was evidence that she had suffered, that she was suffering, for her girlish folly. He saw now that it was merely folly and not the deadly sin he had believed it to be. Between the Christmas Eve when they had parted and this ODO when they met again, there was a gap of twenty years. But the carol bridged it—the carol that had been sung then, that she had been singing now. Strange indeed that she should have called him to her side with that old forgotten_ song.</p>
        <p>Down a narro-iv side sireet Winifred walked swiftly. She opened the door of a tiny house and mutely invited the Bishop to enter. Ile found himself in a dark passage; afterwards ins small sitting-room where an evil-smelling lamp revealed the poverty of this, her home. At the end of the room folding doors were closed, and the atmo- sphere was heavy and close. In the light Winifred looked aged and ill. There were grey hairs in her black locks, many wrinkles round her eyes. But the eyes themselves were still lustrous, though Infinitely sad. In silence she pointed to a chair, and in silence her guest obeyed her gesture. Ile could not witness the wreck of her beauty unmoved, and sighed as he looked. In her turn she swept an inquiring eye over him, and for the first time noted his distinctive dress. Hera under- stood her start.</p>
        <p>"I am the Bishop of the Islands," he said; "ths Islands of the Southern Seas. Have you no 4 heard ? "</p>
        <p>"From the time you left Englehurst I have heard —nothing.", The next words she spoke with an obvious effort and with her eyes on the ground. "Are you married, Dir. Hem n ? " "No. I have been faithful, Winifred—faithful to you." _ _</p>
        <p>Her eyes brightened and a colour warmed her wan cheeks. "I thought you deemed me false ? " she whispered. "I did, but even that could not make me faith- less. I tried to forget you. I threw myself into mission work, all in vain, in vain. I have suffered— but at least I have been spared one sorrow." He looked round the room. 'Had I known that you were in this plight, singing for bread in the streets —oh, you poor soul I" He restrained his emotion with an effort. "I believed you happy and prosperous with your husband," and ended with a sigh. "I have no husband," said Miss Austin, simply. The Bishop's face turned still whiter. "Then you did not fly with this man---,Greorge ? " "Had I done so I should still have been single —for your sake." "Single—for my sake ? Ido not understand." Winifred carried the lamp towards the folding- doors and opening these passed softly into the bed- room beyond. As in a dream the Bishop followed, and paused beside a tumbled bed whereon a man lay sleeping. It was a lean face that the light fell upon, a face with a hectic flush and closed eyes, the face that, ruddy with health, Hem n had seen in the winter moonlight, when he passed through the bitterest hour of his life. And the man he had hated and envied lay dying now of consumption. He stared at this wrecked creature who scarcely seemed to breathe, but remained silent.</p>
        <p>"My brother !" said Winifred, softly. She remained to adjust the clothes, but the Bishop was back in the sitting-room, his head bowed on his arms. A moment and she rejoined him. With a woman's undying pity she gently caressed his head. Ar the man shivered under that tender touch.</p>
        <p>"Had I only known. he moaned. "Alas alas! I was so ready to condemn. And you have been faithful to me through it al! "</p>
        <p>"I loved you," she said, simply. Hem lifted his head and saw truth in her eyes. She clasped his hand. "And I do love you, Michael." "Winifred, I am not worthy," and again he bowed his head tormented by the forgiveness of those eyes. In her deep love she did not think it necessary to say that his worth or unworth made no difference to her. Speaking softly she related that which he wished to know; which he had not dared to ask for—the story of her ruined life. "You remember my uncle," she said, gently. "He was a hard man. When my parents died my brother and I were left in charge of Uncle Vaughan. In his own way he was good to us. He fed us, clothed us, and gave us both a good education. But from him we neither of us received what we so greatly longed for—love and tenderness." "It was not his nature," muttered Hem, recalling that Christmas Eve conversation. And Winifred sighed. "He had his limitations. My brother dis- appointed him. He was angrx to find him wild and wayward, a spendthrift, and‘n idler. Oh, I do not gloss over the poor boy's faults, but think of the lack of training, Mr. Hera. At length, in a moment of madness, my brother "—she sank her voice—"forged Uncle Vaughan's name." "I begin to see," said the Bishop, glancing at her. "And you have had to pay for his sin?" "In part. But was he not my brother? When all forsook him, who but his sister should have clung to him? My uncle turned him away and said that if I held any communication with him he would place the matter in the hands of the police. In fear and trembling I obeyed him. But George sank lower and lower. He came to me for money. To whom else could he come, poor soul? 1 met him by stealth." "Oh, why were you not more open with me?" cried the Bishop in pain. "I asked if you walked with a stranger, and--" "And 1 replied that I did not. George was not a stranger. I admit it was a subterfuge, but I feared to speak out, even to you. Think of what depended upon my silence. On that Christmas Eve he came to urge me to go with him td Australia. He had obtained the money, he wanted me. I should have gone but that I loved you, Michael." Hern shivered 2nd looked pitiably old. "It was all a misundefstanding," he said, helplessly. "Had you been more frank, had I not been so ready to condemn--Oh 1 fool that I was not to interrupt your meeting." "Would that you had, should have told you all.</p>
        <p>Michael, for then 1 But it was not to be.</p>
      </div>
