      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <p>i. could not understand your flight. I waited for a letter, but none came. I could not write as it was rnpossible to obtain your address. I never guessed " —her voice faltered—" that you would so mis- understand me. Had I trusted you—" She paused and her eyes wandered round the room. "You see," she said, pathetically, "my lack of faith has been nunished." "How came ye.. int., this plight. Winifred ? " "Uncle Valighiiii (;;5,,,11,1.± ivy meetings with George, He would hase p.i.ci liin in the dock • but that Georizeetsf iti .111,:rralia. I was turned away with ;e feN.</p>
        <p>"Had he tin- In ? " Eel 11, with a groan. Winifred ensiled sadly. " y,,, know how hard he was. I elooe ;o .1..o)p00 and--" She shuddered. "An do nor we to tell you of my sufferings. Why shoo!ri I? Are they not written here ?" IMO Tointed to her face, so worn and old. "1 stiifereq terribly. I starved. I starve now." "Your brother. SnrelY</p>
        <p>She shook her head.- " 6eorge knew nothing. He went to America, and returned unexpectedly with a little money. We met by accident. He shared his all with me. r.ltien he fell ill, and—" —she made a helpless gL,:nre—"l went out to- night to sing your arol—" Her voice faltered and her hot tears fell.</p>
        <p>"Dear one, Heaven sent you out this night. That song has brought us together. You and I, my darling, can renew our old romance. George will be my care." "He will not live long, Michael, he is dying." "Therefore am i sent to be your protector. My dear wife!"</p>
        <p>"Michael, I am so old, SO INithered," and she sobbed.</p>
        <p>"You are the fairest and sweetest of women," said Hem, tenderly, and laid her weary head upon his breast.' As his lips met hers the bells of a near church rang out jubilantly. It was again Christmas. The sad years were gone, and the bolls rang in the days of peace and joy. The sick man moved in his sleep, but for once forgetful, Winifred, with the light of love in her eyes, clung to her lover. They were old and worn, past the a,,..;e of romance and weary with many sorrows. -BLit for them the days of old returned and the glory of their faithful love made them young once more. "God be thanked for His great mercies," said the Bishop. "Winifred !" She looked into his eyes and saw there what he wished. In a low voice, so that the sick man might not be disturbed, and with her head still on her lover's breast, she sang the carol that had brought them together.</p>
        <p>[Tuv END.]</p>
