      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Bold-Street Wesleyan Churn. The Rev. R. Wilkins Rees was the preacher at the morning service at the above church on Sunday, and took for his subject "The Supreme Name." The anthem "Arise and Shine "was well sung by tin choir. In the evening,the Rev. G. H. Crimson we the preacher his subject being" Jesus, the man." Mrs. Booth was the soloists and sang sweetly '1 knew 14211 my Redeemer liveth."</p>
        <p>Buckley-street Methodist Free Church. At the morning service at the above church, on Sunday, an address was delivered by Mr. E. Pester, and in the evening by Mr W. Shaw, both sermons being listened to by s fairly large congregation. On Monday, a public tea and social were held in the schoolroom, about 80 persons being present, Vai bus songs and solos were given and a pleasant time was spent. The pro- ceeds were in aid of the heating apparatus fund.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. The meeting of the above NN as rather thinly attended on Sunday in consequence of it being Christmas Sunday but a very hearty meeting was held, presided over by Mr. A. Downes. who spoke upon the filial attachment of cons to their parents. Mr. J. R. Woolstencroft of Swinton was the speaker, and spoke upon "Emmanuel, God with us." This, said he, mennt a great deal to mankind: God with us as our great Saviour, with us in allthe trials and difficulties of life, with us in guiding the great Ideals of life. With us in the grand mission to the world, preaching Christ to the world, also living Christ before the world. Ged with us all through this life, and inthe dark moment of deaf" never leaving rs, until we stand before Him, in our Eteicto Home. Mr. Payton Mus. Bac.rendered an organ solo. "Bethlehem" Mr. W. Beswick and Mr. Gz Smiley also took part in the meeting. Mr. Farrar wished all present as well as all the Brotherhood a very nappy Cnristiris time, and a prosperous New Year, and also tended I :•,st wishes to one of the brethren who had been ill a long time. The usual Christmas carols, were heartily joined in</p>
        <p>Hill Cliffe Baptist Church. Christmas is usually observed in some special way in the church and Sunday school at Hill Cliffe r al a ser- vice was held in the church at six o'clock on Suaday morning. At the usual morring service suit .ble hymns were sung, and the sermon wrs 1 sed on" A pilgrimage to Bethlehem." In the evening Mr. Hughes preached on the" revival " in Wale -, which Fad occupied so much attention in the religious world eluting the post month. This was he said the most wonderful thing that had happened in the religious world for nearly fifty yea rs. It was so mysterious, spore: tee eels, so marvenous in its manifestations so remarkable in its effects, so timely and so far-reaching in its infleence, 'eat it was likely to be the meads of checking the materialism and rationalism of the age to which it seemed a divine answer' and be a source of quicker'ng to the whole Church of Christ as well r- a me 79 of salvation to thousands of people. On Monday the anne-1 Sunday school tea and prize distribution were held in the school- room, which was lei .tly el; ;orate I with festoors of holly and evergreens pictures arid mottc es,the work hav- ing been done by some of the t?ac'ee-sred elder scholars. After tea to which a large neinibe- sat down, there was a public entercaintuent and prize distribution in the school room which us crowded. "1 ee Rev. J. S. Hughes took the chair. Mr. Pen Mosr the secretary, r xl a report, from which it appeared the school work had been well maintained cle-ing the ye -. The morning school had been efficient'y taken che-ge of by Mr. E. Lloyd-Jones. and the a fternoonselool by Yr Je -.Moss, jun., at:sister:l by a good staff of loyal t,acher There had be en an increase of scholae3 cturing the year, the number being now 111 of schoir -s. 'lneNarious societies in connection with the ehool had been well n- .ined viz., the mi-sionary fund, the pri-ae fune, the Interna- tional Bible Reading As eiatioa, and the sick and burial club for schola:3. Various items ware gone through, including di- -its by the Misst Alice Cliffe and Annie Mather, rid the Misses Winifred Jones and Marion England, and a song, entitled "Dinah," by Master Stanley Jones. Recita- tions were well given by the following scholars:— Misses Elsie Lyon Janie Dodd, Getty Cliffe, Agnes Sarah Robinson, and Annie Mather, and Master.; Jame- Robinson, Arthur Moores, Francis Philip: Walter Mather and Edward Phillips, whilst Mr. John James Worrall and Miss Kinsey gave respectively two exr llent recitations' Mr. George Moss and party contributed a dialogue entitled "Modern life," which created con- siderable diversion. About 50 handsome bcoks were distribute I to the scholars, most of them for full or good attendance. The prize for being the best boy in the school was given to Master Harold Jones, and that to the second best boy to Mast:e- Arnold Strange. Votes of thanks to all the workers were accorde 1, on the motion of Mr. Joseph Moss, jun , seconded by Mr. Wheeler, and the children dispersed, each one receiving an orange on leaving.</p>
