      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <p>Diagram 42 shows one of these positions. The cue ball and the first object ball are in a straight line between the centre pockets, and the second object ball is half a ball out of this line and above it. The run-through is quite a simple stroke, for all that is necessary is to play full on to the object ball. This ball will then catch. the second ball about half ball, and the two object balls will thus divide and leave plenty of fairly for the oncoming cue ball. There is no need to play the stroke at all strongly. A. strong stroke will leave one of the balls in baulk and the other at the top of the table. If the stroke be played slowly, good position will almost invariably be left to continue with from baulk. The intersected lines show the kind of position that may be left as the result of a medium-pace stroke. In playing a run-through at all similar to the one shown on the diagram, it is, of course, better to have the red ball as the second object than the white, so that the subsequent cannon from baulk may be from the white ball on to the . red.</p>
        <p>DIAGRAM 43.</p>
        <p>be the second object ball care must be taken to avoid this.</p>
        <p>(To be Continued.)</p>
        <p>Vol. I. of 'Billiards: The Strokcs of the Game." 100 illustrations. By 111S0 LEVI. Is now ready, is. net.</p>
        <p>Mr. Riso Levi will be pleased to answer in these columns all questions relative to the, article appearing weekly, and as far as possible any questions on other points in connection with the game of Billiards. Envelopes should be marked • Eitliiards."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>FREE CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>THE FREE CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>GREAT NATIONAL CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>The announcement has just been made of a forthcoming event which should be of great importance to the Free Churches. The reports from the many centres as to the working of the Education Acts have shown that after the pro- longed trial which has been given to the Government's Education legislation, the strong objections'which Free Churchmen made before its passing were well founded, and that the Education Acts have worked to the detriment of the children and played into the hands of the clerical party. In consequence of these reports, the National Free Church Council has decided to call a conference of Free Church managers. This conference is to be held in London on the eve • of the opening of Parliament, tire first week in February. All Free Church Members of Parliament and candi- dates will be invited, and it is expected that all the prominent Free Church leaders will be present and take part. Following the confer- ence there is to be a great demonstration in one of the principal halls of the metropolis.</p>
        <p>THE WELSH REVIVAL</p>
        <p>The Free Churchmen of England are mani- festing great interest in the progress of the revival in Wales. It is agreed on all , hands that nothing like this has been seen for a gene- ration. The movement is absolutely spon- taneous. It is without machinery of any kind. No bills have been printed, no advertisements have been issued', no choirs have been formed, and, more strange still, there has been prac- tically no preaching. It is a movement of the people and apart from ministers or committees. Of course, some of the accompaniments of the mission do not agree with our quieter Saxon ideas, but it must be remembered that similar manifestations have attended practically all revivals. Readers of John Wesley's "Journal" will remember that he encountered the same phenomena and was depressed by it, but he recognised that behind it was the work of the Holy Spirit. It is generally aareed, however, amongst Free Churchmen at least, that a movement which has emptied the Public-house, which has banished swearing from the streets, which has led drunkards to become sober, and which has made atheists burn their infidel books, and one and all to lead a new life, is surely the work of th Holy Spirit.</p>
        <p>LONDON AND THE MOVEMENT. It is to be doubted whether the Revival in its present form will sprf ad to England. *The Celtic temperament is essentially different to the English, so that what is natural to the one would be very strange to the other. Many are agreed, however, in thinking that the Revival spirit in some form or other will make its appearance among us in England. Some of those who entertain this idea have arranged a meeting at Christ Church, Westminster-bridge- road, at which many of those who have witnessed the Revival of Wales on the spot will be present and speak. The Rev. F. B. Meyer, 8.A., is to preside, and the meetings are under the auspices of the National Free Church Council, of which he is President. Among those who are expected to attend are the following: Dr. Campbell Morgan; Dr. Horton, Rev. Thomas Spurgeon, Gipsy Smith, Rev. Charles Brown, Rev. H. Elvet Lewis, Rev. Thomas Phillips, Rev. J. Tolefree Parr, Rev. J. Scott Lidgett and Mr. W. R. Lane.</p>
        <p>THE APOSTLES' CREED. Some consternation is felt in Free Church circles with regard to proposals to include the i use of the Apostles' Creed n public Elementary Schools. The County Council of Cornwall, in face of strong opposition, have granted permis- sion, under certain conditions, to the managers of the Council Schools to include the Creed in the syllabus. The same thing has also been proposed in Flintshire. But for the vigorous protests of one or two Nonconformist leaders, st would certainly have been carried. The Education Committee of the National Council has issued a manifesto to the local Councils throughout the country pointing out that it is most important that they should be on the alert. "It is important," says the circular, "to remember that the adoption of a creed like the Apostles' Creed paves the way for bringing into the schools of the ratepayers creeds and formu- laries of all kinds. If we acquiesce in the in- troduction of the Apostles' Creed, we deprive ourselves of the moral power to resist the introduction of the Roman Catholic or any other creed."</p>
        <p>THE WELSH REVOLT. There is no sign yet of the Government putting the Defaulting Authorities Act in force. It is known that several Church leaders in Wales are against it, for it would raise such a tumult as would overwhelm Church and Tory interest in Wales altogether. So the Govern- ment are holding back, and the Welsh authori- ties are administering the Act in their own way, maintaining the voluntary schools out of the grants, pressing for the execution of proper repairs to the school buildings, and doing what is possible in the circumstances to develop the public schools. For the present the Lloyd George policy holds the field, and the threatened attack upon it by the Government has not yet been delivered.</p>
        <p>FREE CHURCH PARLIAMENT. The arrangements for the Annual Meetings of the National Free Church Council which are to be held at Manchester, in March, are rapidly approaching completion. It is now announced that Dr. Rainy, Moderator of the United Free Church of Scotland, is to preach what is known as the Council sermon. In addition to the re- ception given by the Lord Mayor of Manchester on the Monday evening, Mrs. Rylands, the well-known Manchester philanthropist is to give a reception on Tuesday. This will be held at the renowned Rylands Library. It is felt on all hands that this Tenth National Council will be of national importance, and those intending to be present are advised to make early arrangements. TRADE UNIONS A..ND CHURCH BUILDINGS. The General Committee of the National Council has passed a resolution recommending that local Churches, where possible, should place accommodation for meeting's at the disposal of working people's associations, friendly and benefit societies, and others, to obviate the necessity of their meeting on licensed premises. SOCIAL REDEMPTIVE WORK. Conferences of Free Church Councils are being held in many parts to consider the pro- posals of the Social Questions Committee of the National Free Church Council. An account appears in this month's Free Church Chronicle of the Leeds Girls' Rescue Home. This is the first Rescue Home to be inaugurated by a Free Church Council, but it is expected that other Councils will soon follow suit.</p>
        <p>A MISSION OF PEACE.</p>
        <p>A book which should receive a good deal of attention is to be issued in January by the Publication Department of the National Free Church Council. It is the story of the remark- able tour which Gipsy Smith had in South Africa, and is entitled, "A Mission of Peace: Evangelistic Triumphs in South Africa, 1904." The price is 2s. 6d. net.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>NOTABLE WORKS.</head>
        <p>NOTABLE WORKS.</p>
        <p>Dr. Horton's remarkable devotional volume, "The Open Secret," which the National' Free Church Council issued a few weeks ago, has, met with remarkable success the first edition being sold right out soon after publication. The second edition (7th thousand) has been issued. This volume forms one of a series of Little Books on the Devout Life, the publica- tion of which will be resumed early in the New Year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <p>A run-through in-off into a top pocket. Red ball on the pyramid spot, cue ball 6 inches behind the red and in a line with the red and the pocket Object white 6 inches from the red, and slightly out of the line between the red ball and the pocket.</p>
        <p>Diagram 43 shows another position for a run- through in-off with the cue ball and object ball in a straight line with the pocket, and the second object ball partly blocking the path to the pocket. The position is an awkward looking one, and the run-through is not as simple as the one shown on Diagram 42, for the two object balls are not so easily got out of the way affer the kiss takes place. Nevertheless the stroke is by no means a difficult one even if a little uneer- tain at times. As a rule it is always worth going for, especially as in these positions there is sel- dom anything else at all easy. When the object balls are both some distance from the packet the stroke becomes easier than 11 they are not. very far firom the pocket, as the farther the balls are away from the pocket, the mon- time is there for the object balls to get cut of the wa3 of the oncoming cue ball. In playing strokes of this nature the second object ball mRy sometimes stop the cue ball from entering the pocket; when this happens, however, a cannon has been got instead.</p>
        <p>DIAGRAM 44</p>
        <p>TU. _gh in-off into a top pocket. and rel/4 aII 9 inches, and object white ,IL from the top cushion. Cue ball behind the s red bail 6 inches from the cue ball, and white 6 inches from the red ball.</p>
        <p>Diagram 44 shows a further variation of theso positions with the three balls in nearly astraight line. In this case the path to the pocket is not covered by the second object ball. The run- through should be played just as though the second object ball were not where it is.. A kiss will take place between the object balls and the first object ball will be sent down the table. With a slight variatiou of the pCsition a kiss may take place between the cue ball and, the second object ball, thus preventing,. the in-off being made. But, of course, when this happens a cannon has been made. In some pcsitions, .so the kiss between the object balls may pot the second ball, so that when the white happens to</p>
        <p>. --.. =am, -4-2- .. ',.. ..' s'r.:** ~ .• ..j .... 1 1 I 0 -.} --' ll' Cue ball 8 inches ho spot, ' ohjoct,</p>
        <p>n-throu .a</p>
