      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>.I,lThe Zielsby Waterworks Scheme.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural Dis- trict Council was held at Dutton on Tuesday when there were present :—Messrs. T. Baxter (in the chair),. A. Fryer, J. L. Jones, G. Egerton-Warburton, J. H. Davies, H. M. Davies, J. Bolshaw, J. Hallows, H. Eyes, C. Roylance, C. E. Linaker, C. E. Linaker, jun., G. Pilling, W. Harrison, J. Whitley, J. Mounfield, A. G. Guest, J. Whitlow, J. Smith, T. Antwis, S. Woodward, T. Wright, A. Broome, J. Stokes, S. Horton, E. Bretherton, R. Billington, G. F. Ashton (clerk), and J. Farrington (inspector). FORT SANATARY AUTHORITY FINANCES. - .</p>
        <p>The Clerk submitted the financial statement of the Manchester Port Sanitary Authority. The year was commenced with a balanoe in hand of £496 9s. 10d. The receipts amounted to £2,300 6s. id., and after meeting all expenditure there remained a balance in hand of £1042 17s.</p>
        <p>THE ISOLATION HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>Thelsolatiorillospital Committee recommended the acceptance of tenders from Mr. G. H. Cook for installa- tion of telephones and electric bells at the new hospital. The Committee pointed out that the time for comple tion of the contract for the building had expired and recommended that the contractor be urged to com- plete the work as early as possible or else the Council would have to enforce the penalty clause. The minutes of the committee were approved. HELSBY WATER WORK SCHEME. Messrs. W. and A. Ashton, contractors, Warrington, wrote stating they could not see their way to fall in with the agreement suggested by the Council with regard to the Helsby Water Works con- tract No. 1. Securities as suggested by the Council were a most unusual condition. The unsettled weather and various other circumstances which had arisen since the original tender was made compelled the firm to now neutralise it. They now tendered to carry out the amended contract from £235 16s. 4d., but would require a further £3O to undertake the extra supervision the work would necessitate during the winter months. If the Council could see their way clear to suspend building operations until March the firm would be pleased to, carry out their instructions providing they were allowed a solatium of £l. The letter was considered at a meeting of the Helsby Parochial Committee, who resolved that they could not i see their way to recommend the acceptance of Messrs. Ashton's tender and that in the event of the District Council taking that view, that the tender of Mr. Hugh Davies be accepted provided he agreed to accept reduc- tions made by the engineer in the event of the committee deciding to reduce the specification. Mr. Guest Moved that the minutes of the Helsby Parochial Committee with regard to tender for the Water Works,he referred back,tegether with a recommendation that,the engineer should reviselthe specifications and the, new tenders be advertised for. He thought the arrange- ment would be more satisfactory, and that under it more favourable terms could be obtained. Mr, Mounfield seconded. He thought Mr. Guest's proposal was the most business like way of dealing with the matter. Mr. Ashton pointed out that the matter had been carefully considered by the Parochial Committee. He thought that if the District started interfering in local work of that description they would get a large amount of such work thrown upon their hands. Mr. Guest said that with regard to the tender which it was proposed to accept another had been sent in which was considerably lower. If contractors recognized that local men were to be given the option, outsiders would refuse to tender for the work. Mr. GUESE'S proposition was carried.</p>
