      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>CHRISTMAS.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS.</p>
        <p>while imitation snow flakes heightened the effect. On the walls were several illuminated transparencies—the work of Mr. Sampson, the engineer,—one representing the Workhouse Infirmary being especially pleasing. The festivities were entered into with zest and will rank ; among the most successful and enjoyable ever held. At • noon 538 inmates sat down to a splendid dinner,including a Liberal allowance of roast beef and plum pudding. The unaccustomed fare was attacked with great avidity and it was evident from the happy faces of the inmates that they thoroughly appreciated it. Beer was provided for those who desired the beverage,while for teetotalers there was a plentiful supply of mineral waters. After dinner, the men settled down to a whiff of the fragrant weed The Guardians in attendance during the morning were Mr. A. Brooks (chairman of the Board), the Rev. Dr. Mercer, Mr. A. J. Jolley and Mr. T. H. Ashcroft, while others who lent assistance were the Revs. W. Bracecomp (chaplain), and R. H. Bowen, Mr. and Mrs. A. F. Awbery (master and matron) and Mrs. R. Forshaw. Dinner over, Mr. AWBERY, on behalf of the inmates, cordially returned thanks for the generosity shdwn to them once more by the Board of Guardians. (Applause.) He also wished to take the opportunity of publicly thanking Ithose officers who had given of their 'leisure and Italents in the decoration of the various wards (Applause.) The appearance of that room indicated very clearly that the officers were prepared to do their little best to make the inmates' Christmas as happy and joyous as possible, and it also denoted a very good feel- ing. The decorations were undoubtedly superior to any he had seen in that or other institutions he had been connected with during the last fifteen years. (Applause.) He very much questioned whether in the whole of the Workhouses throughout England the fare would be so liberal and substantial as that which had been provided in the Warrington Workhouse this Christmas time. Somehow they came, to regard the festivities as good old annuals, but they must bear in mind that the provision of extra fare was quite an ob- , tional matter with the Guardians. Therefore he thought the assembly would be wanting very sadly in gratitude if they departed without expressing their warmest thanks to the Board. (Applause). The REV. W. BRACECAMP seconded the vote of thamks which was enthusiastically accorded. • Mr. ASHCROFT responded and said the Guardians had only done their duty in providing the Christmas dinner. The Rev, Dr. MERCER proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. and Mrs. Awbery and the whole of the officers of the House for their self-sacrificing labours which had resulted insuch enjoyable festivities. He was sure that the inmates would look back upon that day as a red letter day for more reasons than one, but he hoped that next year many of them would be in a position to pro- vide their own Christmas dinners. He urged his hearers to endeavour to make a new start in life. It was very well that they should have those grand homes for the poor, but it was much better that the working man who was able-bodied should strike out for himself. He was sure many of those present would strike out for them- selves during the coming year. (Applause.) The Rev. R. H. BOWEN seconded, and said he was sure many of the inmates would take Dr, Mercer's words to heart, and strive their utmost in the year to come to fulfil his high aspirations. (Applause). The vote of thanks was heartily passed, and Mr. Awbery responded, asking the inmates to show their gratitude to the Board by orderly conduct throughout the day. Later in the afternoon oranges and apples were dis- tributed, and the festivities concluded with a smoking concert. Those whose lot it was to be confined to the Work- house hospital at Christmastide were not forgotten. The wards were gaily decorated, and seasonable fare was provided for those who were able to partake of it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>CHRISTMAS STOCKINGS FOR THE POOR. How the Distribution was Carried Out.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS STOCKINGS FOR THE POOR. How the Distribution was Carried Out.</p>
        <p>[By E. E.] On Friday night, being interested in the Rev. H. Dunnico's scheme and receiving an invitation to do so I visited Golborne Street Schoolroom about 8 o'clock. On entering the room my eyes were at once struck with a goodly sight. Improvised tables were loaded with children's toys, such as dolls, musical tops, ships, mouth organs, trumpets, balls, drawing slates, boxes of paints, and all kinds of mechanical toys too numerous- to mention, whilst round these tables busily placing the toys into paper bags were numerous ladies and gentle- men. Several of the latter I recognised as being prominent members of the newly formed Social Service League whilst others were members of the Golborne street church. On forms round the room there had already been placed some hundreds of these bags, which were termed "Xmas stockings." On the floor of the room there were boxes containing mince pies, oranges and there were also bags of apples. The work of filling the paper bags or "Christmas stockings was methodically carried out, for one helper would place a mince pie, another an orange, another an apple and another a couple of toys into it, and it was finally tied with string for safety. The workers, who numbered about 25 appeared to enjoy their work for their countenances were often wreathed in smiles, whilst to enliven the proceedings some of the more vigorous spirits would blow toy trumpets occa- sionally with more or less musical effect—chiefly less. Every few minutes some gentleman known to Mr. Dunnico or the others would come to the schoolroom and hand in a list of names and addresses of homes where he had discovered that owing to hard times the father was out of work and unable to buy his children a Xmas present or in otheticases he would hand in the name and address of a home where grim poverty existed. These names and addresses were subsequently entered into a book. To give a typical case—while I was in,a gentleman entered and stated that a mother with tears her eyes had informed him that her eight year old hild had said to her " Mother ! I am afraid Santa Claus won't come to our house this Christmas. I know we are poor for father has been out of work a long time now; but I am going to pray to Jesus and ask him to tell Santa Claus not to pass our house." I need scarcely add that that little girl's petition was answered. When the workers engaged in filling the bags had finished, the total number exceeded 2,000 and all were ready for delivery on Christmas Eve. SCENES IN THE STREETS A.ND THE HORES. On Saturday Christmas Eve,l again visited Golborne- street schoolroom and was asked to take part in the distribution of the Christmas stockings in what is known as St. John's Warta also the surrounding districts.—Mr. Edward Walker of Latchiord, the well-known Yeastr Factor,'gave his services arol lent his governess car fo the work and I cannot speak too highly of the help he rendered both on Christmas Eve and in the morning of Christmas Day and also on Tuesday when the distribu- tion was finished—lnto this car several hundreds of the Christmas stockings were placed and soon after 10 o'clock a band of about 12 of us started. The mocha operandi, when we reached streets named in our books and lists was as follows :—The Christmas stockings were handed to one of the helpers and off he or she would start for the homes where the little ones lived. Then knocking and receiving admittance, the parents would be asked if they would accept Chrissmas presents for their little ones as it was desired that their Christmas should be a joyous and happy one, and also to show that they were not forgotton. In every case we received a welcome, the joy of the parents being expressed in various ways, showers of blessings being poured on our heads to such an extent that we began to fancy ourselves real Santa Claus's. Foundry-street,Winwick-street, as far as Long- ford was worked in this manner, then along the Jockey, down Orford-lane across Sharp-street' into Lythgoes- lane and thence into Battersby-lane, and then on to the Howley districts, the helpers working also the streets running off the streets named. In the Battersby-lane district children evidently got wind of what was taking place for they came and beseiged us and although their names were not on our books, our hearts were moved at ' their pleadings and also at the evident povsrty, for some of them were without shoes and stockings, and so with a Christmas wish we gave them each a Christmas stocking. The work of distribution took up to 1.30 on Christmas morning and as we had all helped in some way to make over 700 children happy, we then wended our way home, with the words of the Great Inspirer of Men passing through our nd s —"For as much as ye did it unto one of these little ones, ye did it also unto Me." On Tuesday afternoon several of us visited the Latch- ford district, proceeding along Old-road, whoLe the children came swarming round the car con Mining the parcels like so many seagulls, and we handed out over 50 to the little ones. We were nearly pulled out of the car here by some of the mothers in their eagerness to obtain presents for their children and we had to drive off quickly to escape out of their midst, feeling sorry we were unable to satisfy their wants. We delivered altogether over 110 Christ- mas stockings in the Latchford district—but I may say that we could easily have got rid of 500 more, for it was evident that there were many children who had not known what it was to receive this year a Christmas present. I have written this iough description at the desire of several interested, in order that those who have been generous enough to subscribe to what was a noble idea and a practical work, should know that their gifts had been properly and wisely distributed. And may I add that they have been the means of importing much joy and happiness and comfort into many of the poor and dark households of Warrington. and that the gifts were keenly appreciated. I saw children myself literally leap for joy when they received the presents, and heard them laugh more merrily perhaps than they had done for many a long day. whilst the parents were devoutly ;tunkful and much touched at their children being -efiought of. Those who worked the district I have mentioned were Messrs, Morton, Heywood, E. Wynne- Jones, Lowe. Joynson, Morris, Love, Dawson, Cotterill and England. Mrs. Cotterill, Miss Heywood, Miss Cotterill, Miss Brobbin; Miss Ince and other ladies and gentlemen all worked with spirit and good grace.</p>
        <p>Shrubb's Departure. Alfred Shrubb, the champi4n runner, left Tilbury Dock, last evening, by the Ortona for Australia, to ompete in various Colonial championships.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>HOW IT WAS CE-LEBRATED LOCALLY.</head>
        <p>HOW IT WAS CE-LEBRATED LOCALLY.</p>
        <p>On every hand evidence was afforded tha.t.in Warring- ton, at any rate the observance of Christmas is not losing any Of that fervour which has always characterized the festive season. Although Christmas Day itself was generally quiet. Monday and, to a certain extent.Tuesalay were given over to rejoicing, the townsppple entering with zest into the spirit of the occa- sion. The departure of the fog brought great relief to the shopkeepers, and on Saturday night the town was thronged and business was brisk. For a week the " waits " had been busy, but on Christmas Eve they re-doubled their efforts arid reaped .a rich harvest, few of the bands returning before Christ- mas Day was a few hours old. One band of young ladies in a spirit of fun, went out singing carols for the enter- tainment of their friends whom they induced to sub- scribe sufficient to provide dinners for eight poor families. The sacred services in the churches on Sun- daY had a special significance and partook of a festal character : and large congregations assembled at all the places of worship. AT PADGATE SCHOOLS. For 'weeks the children at the Padgate Industrial schools had been busy decorating the interior of that building, with the result that on Christmas Day it pre- sented An unusually attractive appearance. Starting the day with divine worship, they afterwards sat down to a dinner of roast beef and plum pudding, followed by oranges apples, sweets, and nuts then they were free to play as 'they wished until tea time, when another sub- stantial meal was served. In the evening they had a full dress rehearsal of their annual concert, both per- formers and audience enjoying themselves to the full. BLUE COAT SCHOOL. The children attended divine service at Holy Trinity Church On Sunday L morning and on returning to the school, had their usual Christmas dinner of roast legs of pork with potatoes and plum pudding, followed by oranges and apples. Monday was observed as a holiday, when the children had their Christmas tea party. After tea, various games were enjoyed Uitui .01 44corated schoolrooms until nine o'clock.</p>
        <p>THE INFIR,NIARY. On Sunday the patients at the Infirmary who were in a sufficiently good state of health, enjoyed a sub- stantial dinner or roast beef and plum pudding and in the afternoon St. Paul's Church choir sang a number of hymns and carols in the wards. On 'Monday afternoon a substantial tea kindly provided by the nurses, was served, and in the even-mg in the Winmarleigh ward songs and instrumental music were given by the nurses and Hedley Taylor, Miss Taylor, Mr. Martin and others. Great'cradit is due to Miss Watkin, matron, and her staff of nurses, for their efforts for the enjoy- ment of the patients, and for the excellense of the decorations which made the appearance of the wards extremely pleasing. THE BARRACKS.</p>
        <p>No one enters with greater zest into the spirit of Christmas rejoicing than does Tommy Atkins and at Orford Barracks the festivities were attended with scenes of enthusiasm witnessed in profusely decorated rooms. The men were given the usual Christmas dinner, which consisted of turkey, geese, ham, plum pudding, and fruit. On Monday the men spent another pleasant evening, dancing being indulged in until a late hour, while in connection with the sergeants' mess, a social gathering was held) from eight until twelve o'clock. WARRINGTON RAGGED SCHOOL AND MISSION The annual treat and Christmas tree display were held on Monday evening. The rooms were prettily decor- ated with evergreens kindly sent by Mr. F. Monks. Altogether 450 children were provided with tea, bread and butter, cakes, &amp;c., and food was also distributed to several poor women and children who were unable to find a seat at the first sitting. An entertainment fol- lowed, consisting of selections on the gramophone (lent by Mr. B. Wadsworth, Sankey-Street), recitations and songs. Every child on leaving was presented with an article of clothing, a Christmas stocking (or a toy), and an orange. Thanks are due to Lady Greenall for the Christmas tree. Mrs.and Miss Crosfield and a few others for clothing, Mi. Jas. Harding for 10s., an anonymous friend for 2s. HOT-POT DISTRIBUTION. The poor people of the town were not overlooked in the general rejoicings as the Mayor again provided the usual hot-pot for those who were not able to provide their own dinner. The hot-pot was distributed from the Drill Hall on Christmas Day to some 1,300 applicants— an increase of 200 on the previous year. Mince pies were also given to the poor children who presented themselves, Through the kindness of the generously disposed,Nlr. Henry Taylor, P.C.M., was able to distribute the following amongst the poor :-501b5. of beef, four loads of potatoes, 20 plum puddings, 100 loaves of bread, 50 cakes and mince pies, 400 parcels of tea, coffee sugar and butter, 24 pairs of blankets and a quantity of cloth- ing, as well as oranges, sweets and crackers for the children. He was assisted in the work by Inspector Cox, N.S.P.C.C. SALVATION ARMY. - - _</p>
        <p>The Salvation Army, as usual, made special efforts on behalf of the poor, and on Monday evening a larg.e com- pany was entertained to a substantial tea at the Army Barracks .Afterwards a miscellaneous concert was given at which songs, sole, cornet, piano,band,and phonograph selections were given by Messrs. Bate, Tolen, and Deputy Bandmaster Rowe. A vote of thanks was passed at the close to the sisters who had provided the tea, and to all who laboured in the movement, all the arrange- ments passing off successfully. WORKINGMEN'S MISSION, BANK QUAY. The senior scholars connected with the above school held their annual Christmas treat on Tuesday last. A capital tea was provided, the wants of all being catered for by a willing host of workers. Afterwards a public meeting and concert were held over which Captain George Crosfield presided, 31r. H. Haliard proved himself an excellent humorist, his song "My little boy" and "Nomination day" being well received. Mr. George Gough was heard to advantage in "Thy sentinel an I," and in response to an enthusiastic encore gave "Out on the deep" The Secretary (Mr. A. Gleave) submitted his annual report, which showed that the Mission was in a flourish- ing condition. The total number of scholars on the books was 564 being an increase of 6 on the previous year. The various branches of the school,—the young ladies select class, the men's Bibleclass, and the Christian Endeavour, and Band of Hope Societies —all showed good progress, Captain Crosfteld congratulated the School Committee on the favourable report of the year's working. He assured the meeting that Mrs. Crosfield's interest in the Mission was as great as ever, and expressed the hope that the Mission i would still continue n the prosperous condition which had marked all previous years. Miss Crosfteld also addressed the meeting,, her remarks to the younger por- tion of the audience being listened to with marked attention. • Miss Stunner delighted her hearers with the song "Daddy,,' and received a well-merited encore. Messrs. Corm, Taylor, A. Spruce, and Gough favoured the audience with a humorous quartette, "There was a tack," and in response to an encore responded "Simple Simon," After a vote of thankshad been passed to the artistes, games were commencedand concluded an enjoy- able meeting. On Wednesday evening the junior portion of the school assembled for their Christmas treat. Tea was provided, after which the children were entertained by the members of the Men's Select Class, the band of which evoked considerable amusement. Games were indulged in and a very pleasant evening was spent. THE POST OFFIOE—CHRISTMAS PRESSURE. The amount of extra posting was greater this Christmas at Warrington than ithas ever been. When it is stated that inthis town alone something like approxi- mately 750.000 letters passed through the Post Office— inssard and outward—and 30,000 parcels during the few days conststuting Christmastide, an idea can be formed of the extra work thrown on the staff who, in order to give prompt service to the public, had to work at high pressure, in some instances, night and day. Even then the ordinary staff had to be augmented in a ratio of 50 per cent., and, as already stated, extra accommodationhad to be found for them at the Gymna- sium and the Conservative Club. The work of the Post Office, it may be stated, passed off without a hitch, and was promptly attended to b Warrington, despite its vast and unprecedented volume. Some delay necessarily occurred to mails, owing to the fogs which prevailed for several days throughout the country, causing, as a consequence, trains carrying mails to be belated. It has been gratifying to the Postal authorities to find how largely the public have availed themselves this Xinas of the system of "advance posting" in vogue here. As an experiment the system was of great value and assistance to the Postmaster, enabling him, as it did, to prepare the local letters for delivery here in advance, and thereby giving the public a more expeditions ser- vice than could otherwise have been obtained. The experiment of having a separate letter box for local posting—local letters only— was also found to be of considerable assistance to to the staff from a sorting point of view, the public also falling in readily with the arrangement'. It may be interesting to know that in order to cope with the extra parcel work, a number of additional horse vans had, of necessity, to be requisitioned. Altogether, the special facilities and extra accommodation afforded for the Chrisimas work this year have been fully justified, in that the result has been an expeditious service main- tained throughout the whole of the period without a single drawback. - THE WORKHOUSE FESTIVITIES.</p>
        <p>To the Workhouse inmates Christmas, brings its joys and on Saturday the usually sombre rooms at the in- , stitution were bright with lavish decorations. The dining hail in particular was most artistically ;embel- lished with a wealth of evergreens and artificial flowers,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
