      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, December 31, 1904.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 31, 1904.</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED'.</p>
        <p>ear give any reality to the situation. Parlia- ment when it meets ought to refuse to be , made a fool of any longer, and to be kept 'marking time." Even Mr. Chamberlain said at Limehouse the sooner it comes the better. All pairs should be declined, and "strained relationships" between the two Front Benches ought to begin at once and as a matter of course. I turn now to the 101 pages of Tory achievements. The Anglo-French Convention, signed in London on April Bth, 1904, was approved by an Act which obtained the Royal Assent on August 15th. The Convention contains nine articles, under the terms of some of which his Majesty has surrendered to France Garbu- tenda and the Iles de Los. My pride ought perhaps to be wounded, but it is not. The terribly-vexed Newfoundland Fishing Question and the Niger Boundary are, we fondly hope, if not settled put in the way of settlement. By an arrangement outside the Convention— but its real consideration—France obtains a free hand in Morocco. France is for the moment delighted with us, and we with France. We are bound to quarrel some day over something. Then will be the time to keep our tempers. Let us store up the bubbling emotions of to-day against that time. Friendship with France ought to be easy, or at all events easier than with certain other peoples who shall be nameless but are numerous.</p>
        <p>THE EDUCATION (LOCAL AUTHORITY DEFAULT)</p>
        <p>ACT is a sequela of the ill-starred Act of 1900. It contemplates a refusal of a local authority to perform the duties imposed by statute upon it with respect to Church of England Schools, which by the Act of 1900 were dumped down upon the rates for the first time. The statute proposes to get over the difficulty 2f putting a whole County Council into prison by empower- ing Mr. Morant, or the Board of Education Secretary for the time being, to take over and perform the administrative duties which the recalcitrant authority has neglected to dis- charge. Wales, of course, is not mentioned. This is a dear little Balfourian Act, contain- ing but one effective clause. Up to date nothing has happened under its provisions.</p>
        <p>The mountains look at Marathon, And Marathon looks at the sea. The Board of Education watches Wales, and Wales awaits the Board of Education. In the meantime, in the one part of our home- dominions where the people really love and value education for its own sake, where they make sacrifices to obtain it, and have the best system of secondary education in the world, the Church of the minority, by her refusal to come to terms, has endangered the whole structure of elementary education. THE BUDGET OF 1904</p>
        <p>was the Free Trade Budget of a Protectionist Chancellor of the Exchequer, the first, and I trust the last, of his breed. He raised the duty on tea to Bd. per lb, he largely increased the duty on cigars and cigarettes, he came to terrible grief over stripped tobacco, and he clapped a penny on the Income Tax. Corn and meat he had to leave alone. His fingers must have itched to tax them; but the time is not yet. ANOTHER IRISH LAND ACT</p>
        <p>made it plain that the bribe provided by the Act of 1903 to induce landlords to sell is to go straight into the pockets of tenants for life, and not to be treated as capital trust-money. It was by a provision of this kind that Lord Goschen assured the success of his great Consol Conversion scheme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>SOCIAL SERMONETTES FOR WORKING MEN.</head>
        <p>SOCIAL SERMONETTES FOR WORKING MEN.</p>
        <p>BY JOHN TAYLOR Author of "Search Lights on Una, Labour and Social Problems."</p>
        <p>JOSEPH'S LITTLE EMPIRE " Mr. Chambeilain's scheme proposes to help only the self-governing colonies such as Canada, Australia, and New Zealand. He would treat India, Ceylon, the West 1 ndies, and all our other Colonies as he treats foreign countries. Their goods would be shut out in the same way as he proposes to keep out German goods. And yet he calls himself aMissionary of Empire."—R. S. Mansfield, M.P. Solomon in the Book of Proverbs tells us that "the eyes of a fool are in the ends of the earth," and I had a pod illustration of this truth when I was in college. We had a conceited student there who boasted how much he knew about astronomy, and every star-light night he would be looking for Saturn, and Mars and Jupiter, when he ought to have been at his stuJies. One night, when he was moving about as usual, and looking for planets millions of miles away, he nearly broke his neck over a wheelbarrow that we had obligingly left in his ' way. He could see things far beyond the end of the earth, but he could not see a whole wheelbarrow under his nose. "Good old Joe," our "self-appointed Missionary of Empire " is cursed with this kind of telescopic vision, He can see whatever he wants to see, even if the thing has no existence, and he can be blind to the most palpable fact, if it does not happen to suit his conveni- ence or his purpose. He asks us to " think Imperially" about that vast British Empire on which the sun never sets, but he thinks and acts Parochially about the vast majority of its people. He has got an infallible pill for the making of a vast Colonial Empire, but nineteen twentieths of our Colonists are not allowed to take it. I.—HE IGNORES THE CLAIMS OF OUR HOME</p>
        <p>GOVERNED COLONIES. He has been in office for eight or nine years as Colonial Secretary, at a salary of one hundred pounds a week, so he ought to know a thing or two about them. Let me remind him that our Colonial population amounts to 361,000,000 including India, and our scattered dependencies in all parts of the world. But 345,000,000 of these have neither vote nor voice in the management of their own affairs. They cannot choose their own rulers, or elect representatives to tax them- selves, and spend their own money. They have to pay whatever rates and taxes are levied, and submit to any kind of ruler or governor that may be sent to rule over them. Their business is to pay and submit, and do as they are told, and ask no questions. India, which is less than half the size of Canada, has a population of 1300,000,000 and these people are ruled by puppets sent from England, and the ministers in London pull the strings. Lord Curzon is the Tory nominee who rules India, and the Hindoos have no voice in his selection and no control over his policy. All they have to do is to fight his battles and pay his expenses. This is a sample of how India and Ceylon and the Straits Settlements and the West Indies, and all our Home Governed Colonies are ruled, and they number nineteen twentieths of the people outside the country who submit to our authority. Now I want to know why Joseph's infallible pill should be given to Canada and Australia and New Zealand, and be denied to India and Ceylon and the West Indies. If Protection and Retaliation and Hostile Tariffs are good things for 16,000,000 Colonists why are they not equally good for the other 345,000,000 ? If you can levy import duties, and make the foreigner pay those duties without raising the price of his articles why not buy him up and make him pay all our taxes? And if this idiotic game can be made to pay, why should not our Home Governed Colonies have a look in on their own account. In the days when "Good old Joe" was a Radical he used to plead for "the greatest good to the greatest number." But he is a Tory now, and his greatest number is number one, and the gospel he preaches is the gospel of selfishness. It teaches men to look after them-elves, and "take that take can, for the biggest rogue is the best man." Its motto is "Our Empire first, and the devil take the Foreigners." Now I have met with a few men who were crazy enough, and wicked enough to preach such sentiments as these, but they are the kind of people that I would not trust near my coal heap on a dark night. And I would not trust the welfare of the British Empire to any statesman who would frame tariffs to tax and burden nineteen out of every twenty citizens for t he benefit of the twentieth member. lI.—JOSEPH THINKS MORE OF TERRITORY</p>
        <p>THAN HE DOES OF PEOPLE. He has been %casting his telescopic eyes over the illimitable veld, and thinking imperially. He is labour- ing under an insane delusion that territory will make an Empire, so he co vets all the arid deserts, and pestilential swamps, and worthless possessions in the world. He thinks that if he can only secure a sufficient number of hills, and valleys, and plains, and mountains, and rivers, and forests, and fields, he will be able to make an empire. But that is just where he makes his mistake. These things will make a waste howling wilderness, and a worthless desert, if there are no workmen to develope them, and cultivate them, and make the earth yield her increase. Did you ever think how much territory had fallen into the hands of our self-governing colonies, and what little use they are making of it? Let me give you a few facts and figures that will open your eyes, and set you thinking imperially. You can verify my figures by a reference to " Whitakers Almanac" or the "Statesman's year book" in the articles on our Colonies. Canada the largest of them. has an area of 3,619,819 square miles, and it has a population of 5,450,000, or less than one person, for each square mile of territory. In other words it is as large as European Russia, and France, and Germany, and Austria, all put together, and it has not as many people in it as there are in London. It is as large as the whole of the United States, and it has about a million more people than there are in Lancashire. Australia comes next in size, with an area of 2,972,906 square miles, and a population of 3,856,000. Or to put it in another way, she has a territory nearly as large as Europe, and about as many people as there are in Yorkshire. New Zealand has 101,471 square miles of land, and only 815,000 people. It is half the size of France or Germany, and it has fewer people than there are in the two cities of Glasgow and Edinburgh. It is larger than England, Scotland and Wales put together, and it has not as many inhabitants as there are in Leeds and Sheffield. Now add these figures together, and you will see that Canada, Australia and New Zealand t ogether control 6,697,196 square miles of territory, and their united populationlis only 10,130,000 all told. They own about one 'eighth of the whole world, and there are not so many of them as you can find in London and Lancashire any day. If you add to these, Cape Colony, and the smaller self governing colonies that have any form of represen- tative government, you will find that:their total popula- tion us only 16,000,000 which is less than one twentieth of the whole of oar Colonial empire. And these 16,080,000 are to enjoy all the blessings of Protection and Beta liatiOn while the other 345,080,000 are to be denied t hese privileges. He is fond of calling some o us " Lit tie Englanders," but he is certainly a little Empire builder if he is going to leave nineteen out of every twenty of our people outside his calculations,and think only of those self-governing colonies who are both willing and able to look after their own interests. He says he wants £1,600,000 a year to be given to the sixteen millions of favoured colonists who manage their own affairs, and make their own tariffs, to induce them to give 'is preferential treatment. Why does he not give it to them, and have done with it? It would only be two shillings per head all round, and it would be about as honest as his Sugar Bounties, or his sops to Landlords and Parsons. But Joe loves crooked and deivous ways so he pro- poses to tax our con, and our manufactured goods,and everything else that comes from foreign conntries The 42,000,000 people who live in free England are to be taxed to raise this bribe to the 16,800,000, and it will liaise the price of everything they buy one or two shillings in the pound. What will the 345,000,000 say when they find they are to have no share in the plunder. And what will the English tax payers say when the cost of living has gone up from five to ten per cent and their wages have gone down? And.where are we to get a big volume of trade from such a small handful of people as are to be found in oar self-governing Colonies. In 1902 their trade with us was little more than half what was done with our Home-Governed Colonies, and it was about one-fifth of our total trade for the year. Are we going to throw over four-fifths of our total trade, to oblige the people who Asnd us the other fifth? , Shall we tax ourselves to the tune of twenty or thirty millions a year, to raise a paltry £1,600,000 for the benefit of 16,000,000 favoured Colonists? The House of Commons is no place for the author of a scheme like this. He should be in Colney Hatch, or Bedlam, with his head shaved, and in a padded room. He knows very well that the Tories lost half our Colonies by the American War of Independence, because they would not let us tax them, and he is under the delusion that we shall lose the other half if we do not let them tax us. Let me commend to him the wise and weighty words of of Lord Goschen, who says: "Our statesaneig must endeavour to realise the fair dream of a cemented empire without the nightmare of tampering with the people's food."</p>
        <p>Extra Postal Deliveries. As a result of a local petition to the Postmaster General an additional postal delivery in the forenoon has been granted. Previously there had been no delivery between 7 o'clock and 12 noon, and, an addi- tional one in the middle of the morning -.sill, no doubt prove a great convenience. The new delivery will commence on Monday next, January 2nd. Messrs. J. and W. Dutton were the originators of the petition and to them, as well as the Postmaster, Mr. Lister, the thanks of the public are due.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>LIBERAL POLITICS IN 1904. BY AUGUSTINE BIRRELL, K.O</head>
        <p>LIBERAL POLITICS IN 1904. BY AUGUSTINE BIRRELL, K.O</p>
        <p>"The petty done, the undone vast" are the words that rise naturally to one's lips when</p>
        <p>handling, for the purposes of review, the meagre, atrophied volume of the Statutes for 1904. One hundred and one pages are</p>
        <p>_ sufficient to contain all the public general Statutes for this year of grace 4 Edward VII. Why, in 1867 1,480 pages were not enough to hold within bounds the legislative exube-</p>
        <p>ranee of that year. Judged by bulk, Mr. Balfour's Administration is, when compared with those of his predecessors in recent times, as Slender to Falstaff. Four slimmer, more ladylike volumes than the Statutes 1900-1904 are not to be found in the Temple or Lincoln's Inn. And yet they contain perilous matter, explosive materials. Short as are Mr. Balfour's Acts, same of them have made more noise in the world, and created more dissension than any of Mr. Gladstone's, the Home Rule Bills, perhaps, excepted. One peculiar feature of the present political situation, fierce and acrimonious as it is, and must continue to be until a dissolution, is that no record of it is to be found in the Statute Book for the session, which is chiefly remarkable for what it does not contain. With the exception of the Licensing Act and the Welsh Local Authority Default Act, there is nothing in the Statute Book for 1904 to suggest storm and discord, fierce strife and contention, the breaking up of a great party, the rending of a Cabinet, and widespread exacerbation of feeling. It is a sleepy volume, suggestive of drowsy afternoons and evenings steeped in boredom. All the most important things have happened outside 4 Edward VII., Chapters 1-36. The most important thing of all has been the career of Mr. Chamberlain, the Tariff rlleformer, compared with the attitude of the Prime Minister, the Retaliating Free Trader. We are not reputed to be a subtle race here in beefy England. We have let the Germans go ahead of us in their analyses of the character of Hamlet. But our Scottish Premier is doing us great good in this way. We have all attended his classes at Edinburgh and Sheffield; and to-day it is no uncommon thing to see some blunt-witted Southron body seriously forcing his untutored mind to grapple with such nice problems of mixed morals and language as, e.g., whether Balfour and Chamberlain are working in secret concert, or whether the Edinburgh speech does not necessarily involve at some date or another a final rupture between these two potentates. We love to personify our disputes, and it is, I suppose, no use putting men on one side, and considering the rival Niscal schemes seriously on their merits; and yet ultimately this will have to be done. The Protection heresy was never destroyed, and never will be. Perhaps it is not, properly speaking, a heresy at all, unless it be heretical for an individual trader to prefer his own interests to those of the country at large. The individual trader starts with his own article, for which he wishes to procure a high price and a large consumption. He, therefore, quite naturally, quarrels with foreign com- petition. But as he must rest his case on his country's good, and not exclusively on his own, he proceeds.to say that if the foreigner's goods which compete with his were to be kept out or discouraged more workmen would be employed at home making the additional articles hitherto made abroad. It is this latter argument which is the heresy exploded by Adam Smith and his school of thinkers. The real interest of a country is to increase its trade all roun, and to do nothing legislatively which shall either hamper its natural strength and advantages or divert its capital from the things that pay best to those which pay badly or do not pay at all. Adam Smith hated politicians, regarding them as mischievous, ignorant animals, whose powers of doing harm to trade and business were enormous; whilst it was only very occa- sionally they ever had the opportunity of doing good. Were Smith to live again he would not, I expect, be greatly disposed to modify his language. But if Adam Smith's principles had really ever been grasped by the electors of the country, Mr. Chamberlain would not have placed himself, as he has done, at the head of the Protection party. The Protectionists have never been demolished. They have remained within the ranks waiting for ,st leader. Lord Salisbury would not listen to them, Sir Michael Hicks-Beach kicked them out of the Treasury, Lord Goschen shuddered at their dangerous delusions. They had nobody to espouse their cause, to procure them the support of the Press, to excite the hopes of the socially ambitious, to gain the ear of the Empire. At last they have got their man, and, having got him, they are ready to rally round him, and to proclaim him their great deliverer. But between them and him stands the figure, dubious masked, but "sly, devilish sly," of the Prime Minister. Is he friend or foe Nobody knows. He has said he is not the man to lead a Protection party, because he is (for Great Britain and Ireland) a Free Trader. But that is not enough. The Protectionists have got a leader of their own, big enough and disagree- able enough to fight anybody. They are quite prepared to transfer their allegiance—indeed, thy have in great measure already done so— from Mr. Balfour to Mr. Chamberlain. What they want to know is whether Mr. Balfour is going to oppose Mr. Chamberlain, and that is just what the Prime Minister will not tell them: It is a queer position. It may be con- sistent with straightforward English politics, ! but it is hard to understand how it can be. In the meantime, the delay and the confusion between Colonial Preference 2nd Tariffs to protect our own manufactures have gone a long way to knock the bottom out of Mr. Chamberlain's pilgrimage of passion. A man who told us eighteen months ago that our trade was threatened and our chief industries either gone or going, and yet remains quite content to lot things alone for half-a•dozen years, cannot expect to be taken seriously. Several of the King's Ministers, including, of course, the Chancellor of the Exchequer, are known to profess Mr. Chamberlain's opinions. Yet they name 1906 as the probable date of the Dissolution, which is to begin a controverag which must involve (if the Protectionists are to win) at least two subsequent Dissolutions. They have already brought in one Free Trade Budget—they propose to Lirincr in two more. The patient lies on the point of death, a new treatment will, they know, cure him, but they go on with the old treatment as long as ever they can. What patriots! This delay has taken the edge off the pro- paganda. The wheels of Mr. Chamberlain's chariot arce clogged. He must see it is going to be a long business. Still I doubt not he has his encouragements. Reports, coloured somewhat, I daresay, may reach him of the spread of hi views in some of our larger towns.. As against that, the vast army of con- sumers, of poor people' with small fixed in- comes and expensive tastes fostered by Free Trade, is beginning to ask, What good will it do ME? Even Mr. Chamberlain's audacity will not be equal to giving the poor consumer the necessary assurances. Nor is it good policy for people with huge incomes to go about deriding cheapness. The great, the overwhelm- ing majority of British voters are deadly poor. Without cheap things they will become as badly off as are the poor folk in the protected countries of Russia and Germany and Italy. I do not suppose our great Apostle of Protec- tion went into a single poor man's cottage in Perugia, or Pisa, or Verona. He might have learnt something had he done so, but he is in no mood for learning. The tyranny of single ideas is destructive of the love of truth. Nothing but a dissolution can clear the airs</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>LICENSING ACT</head>
        <p>THE LICENSING ACT</p>
        <p>is the great work of the session. It reverses 1 Sharpe v. Wakefield, and now the law is that I no publican's annual licence can be taken from him on the ground that it is not wanted except by Quarter Sessions, and on the payment of compensation. This is at once a revolution and the creation of a new vested interest. We are told that the Act has not raised the price of brewery shares. It may be so, but would they not have fallen lower had the bill been defeated? The second feature of the bill As the Com- pensation Fund, which is raised by the imposition of taxation on licences according to the annual value of the premises. The minimum tax is £1 in respect of premises under the annual value of £l5; the maximum tax is £lOO in respect of premises over the annual value of £9OO. When a licence is not renewed on the ground that it is not wanted, the persons interested in the premises are to receive a sum equal to the difference between the value of the premises as a drink-shop and as an unlicensed house. It follows from this that no licence can be taken away because it is not wanted by Quarter Sessions unless the taxes they have imposed on licences provide them with the necessary funds out of which the compensation is to be paid. This makes the reduction of the drink-shops a very slow process. In places where they greatly abound thirty years and upwards must roll by before any appreciable reduction can be made. This particular road to temperance by way of reduction is closed by the Act. A further criticism is that licences ought to be taxed, and certainly would soon have been taxed for general revenue purposes, whereas now they will escape such liability, and are taxed for the purpose of buying up vested interests created by the Act itself. The Act strikes a useful distinction between old, that is, existing licences, and any new licences that may hereafter be granted. In the case of new licences, conditions may be imposed by Quarter Sessions so as to secure to the public the difference between the value of the premises as a drink-shop and as an un- licensed house. The Justices also may grant new licences for seven years, at the end of which time any application for a re-grant shall be treated as an application for a new licence. Another clause provides that the refusal by a publican to supply non-intoxicating refresh- ments of a suitable character, and at a reason- able price, shall entitle the Justices to treat the house as ill-conducted, and refuse a renewal of its licence. Those temperance reformers who are Tories first profess themselves satisfied with the measure.</p>
        <p>THE PREVENTION OF CRUELTY TO CHILDREN Act is a godel measure, for it both amends and consolidates the law on this painful subject. Foolish optimists who shut their eyes and their ears to the horrors of human nature will do well to study the reports of the Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children before they sneer at legislation of this character. Happily the victory is won, and neither foolish optimists nor pig-headed judges can do much harm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>SHOP HOURS ACT</head>
        <p>A SHOP HOURS ACT</p>
        <p>deserves a word of notice. This new Act iro what is called an enabling Act. It does not threaten and command. ft enables. Loeal authorities are enabled to fix the hours on the several days of the week at which, either throughout the area of the local authority or any specified part of it, all shops, or shops:sof any specified class, are to be closed for serving customers. The local authority has first to be satisfied that a prima fade ease for closing liNs been made out; then it gives public notice and invites objections; aufd if eventually it is satis- fied that at least two-thirds of the skopkeepers affected approve of the proposed closing, it may make the order, which, however, must be con- firmed, and may be disallowed by a Secretary of State. As soon as the Secretary of State has confirmed, the closing ordei has the effect of an Act of Parliament; but the King in Council may, in certain events, annul the order. It is worth while to note the first appearance of an Act to provide for "the rec.-illation of wireless telegraphy."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>AUGUSTINE BIRRELL.</head>
        <p>AUGUSTINE BIRRELL.</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>(By " Ovalist.",</p>
        <p>The Bank Holiday week end has produced three points from two matches, so that Warrington have not done badly. Yet none of their supporters are satisfied, and it n..ust be admitted that the team might have and ought to have done better. Wakefield Trinity were only beaten by a try to nil, and at St. Helens on Mon- day, Warrington had to be content with a pointless draw when a win was fully expected.</p>
        <p>There is no avoiding the fact that at present the Warrington team is performing poorly. The defence still stands as the best in the League, but the attack has fallen away considerably. During the last four matches only two tries have been scored and not a single goal has been kicked. In none of these games have the Warrington men been thoroughly beaten. They have • had as much of the play as their opponents, but they have failed to bring their attacking movements to a satistactory conclusion. The fault lies somewhere, and fortunately is is not far to seek. Primarily it rests with the threequarters. who have performed indifferently all the season. Not one of the regular four has been in his best form, and the play of Isherwood, Dickenson and Harris had been particularly disappointing. Just prior to his injury, Isherwood seemed to be im- proving,but the right wing pair have gone from bad to worse. In the matches this week end they have been practically useless except on the defence, and it is not Possible for Fish to do all the scoring. He himself would probably have been of more effective servicelif the other wing had been able to do its share on the attack. The speed and dash of both Dickinson and Harris, however, seems to have completely vanished. Let us hope that the failing is only temporary.</p>
        <p>I Determined measures have become imperative and I am glad to see that the Committee have taken the bull by the horns at last. For the match with Leigh to-day—a most important one—radical changes have been made. Only Fish of the regular threequarters retains his place, the other three being Hookenhall ! Brooks, and Kenyon. Isherwood is, of course, incapaci- tated by injury or he would probably have been chosen. The halves are still weak, and it is a pity that neither R. Bate nor J. Davies is available, for D. Davies and Duckworth cannot be expected to form a I very strong combination. However, an experiment I was necessary and I hope the new men will justify the confidence placed in them. Kenyon has shown excellent form since his recovery from illness, and everyboL'.y is trusting to his being, this time, able to display some of his ability with the first team. The trials he has previously had have left much to be desired, but experience ought to be standing him in good stead.</p>
        <p>There is not much to be said in regard to Saturday's 1 match with Wakefield. Apart from a flash of the old brilliance by the backs in the first quarter of an hour, the game was as dull as ditchwater. Fish obtained the winning point from the opening passing bout, and thereafter there was little to enthuse about. Isher- wood sustained a severe injury to the nose which compelled his retirement at the interval, and the 1 second half was slightly in favour of the Trinitarians. They, however, showed very little scoring ability and were easily beaten back whenever they got too near ! the home line for comfort.</p>
        <p>The St. Helens match was far more interesting and exciting. A good thousand Warrington enthusiasts accompanied the team, and they got plenty of lively football for their money. Iu the opening stages of the game Warrington were" all over "the Saints, and the home line was crossed no fewer than four times after clever football. Unfortunately a forward pass or some other infringement caused the referee to nullify every point,and I think his decisions were fairly reasonable. Once, however, he took a peculiar course. Fish had run down touch and kicked across for Preston to score an easy try under the posts. The kicker was prevented by obstruction from putting his men on side, an instead of allowing the point for this reason, the referee, Mr. Moore of Oldham, gave War- rington a penalty in front of goal. To the chagrin of the Warrington crowd Fish failed at the easiest of place-kicks. In my opinion, if a penalty was incurred by the obstruction of Fish, the referee should have had no hesitation in allowing the try which was perfectly fairly gained.</p>
        <p>Whatever has come over Fish's goal-kicking powers? To my own recollection he has missed at least four shots right in front of the posts, and close in, viz., against Oldham, Yorkshire,Broughton, and St. Helens. Evidently he requires a rest from this branch of the game. Preston has not been given much chance so far and the state of affairs demands that he should have a fair opportunity of justifying the reputation he gained with Swinton. Will Captain Hallam please note!</p>
        <p>The second half of the game at St. Helens saw the home lot in much better trim, while Warrington's play deteriorated. Several times a seore seemed imminent for either side, but on the whole the Saints became the more dangerous as, time went on. The forwards lasted exceedingly well, and the Warrington eight were by no means the superior in the closing stages, though they possessed an advan- tage in weight. St. Helens are not very classy in the rear or they would have. pulled the game out of the fire, but Traynor and Liversage played a good game. Really, both teams were lucky to draw, for in the first half St. Helens were morally three tries inferior, while in the concluding portion Warrington were perilously near defeat more than once. The game was very fast and open, and travelled from end to end with a rapidity seldom seen on a Rugby field.</p>
        <p>The League table has undergone some changes during the holidays. Bradford have had a very warm time of it in the Leeds district. On Saturday the town team just got ahead of the Park Avenue brigade,while on Tuesday Hunslet simply smashed the champions by 17 points L. nil. Consequently they have left Oldham in undisturbed possession of the leadership. while Hunslet bid fair to oust them even from second place. Runcorn and Batley have sunk further into the mire, and nothing short of a football miracle can save either club from the second division.</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows :- -For— —Against— P. W. L. D. G. T. Pts. G. T. Pts Pts. Oldham 17...13... 4... 0...25 29-137...17 13— 73...26 Bradford 19...12... 5... 2...20 30-130-18 22-102...26 Hunslet , 19...12... 6... 0...38 22-142...13 15— 71...24 Brou't'n R. 16...11... 4... 1...18 30-126...19 14— 80...23 Leeds Warr'gt'n 17... 9.. 6... 2...18 23-105...11 13— 67...20 Wigan 17... 9... 7... 1...16 22 98...23 162 94...19 Salford Hull 16... 6... 2...16 23-101...18 22-102...18 Hull K.R 18... 9... 9... 0...25 23-119...15 20— 95...18 Swinton 18... 8... 9... 1...11 21— 85...21 21-105...17 Leigh Hal:fax Wakefield 17... 5...11... 1...20 8— 64...18 18— 90...11 Widnes St. Helens 16... 5...10... 0...21 18— 96...25 42-176...11 Runcorn 17... 4...13... 0...14 16— 76...23 30-136... 8 &amp;tau 17... 3...13... 1...14 13— 67...24 30-138... 7</p>
        <p>The South-West Lancashire League table up-to-da is as follows :</p>
        <p>For. Against. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. Pts Warrington 5... 4... 0... 1... 9 7 39... 2 1-7 7... 9 Leigh Wigan St. Helens G... 2... 3... 1... 4 7 29... 3 9 30... 5 Widnes Runcorn 0... 4.. 0... 3 4 18— 5 10— 40... 0</p>
        <p>The loss of 2, point at St. Helens has made:War- rington's position in the above a precarious one, and they will require to beat Leigh to-day if they are to retain possession of the cup. Leigh have improved greatly since the opening match of the season when. Wayington trounced them so, and the result of to- day's game is somewhat rncertain on current form. Warrington should certainly maintain their unbeaten home certificate, hut they will have to improve on recent displays to do so.</p>
        <p>The Warrington team will be: Hallam, fall back; Fish, Hockenhall, Brookes, and Kenyon, threequarterso; D. Davies and Duckworth, half backs; Preston, Boardman, Shugars, Belton, Thomas, Harmer, Cook, and Swift, forwards.</p>
        <p>The Leigh team will be as follows :—Pearson, Johnson, Messer, Gartland, Neville ; Dunbavin</p>
        <p>Burgess ; Berry, Blackburn, Kelly, Lee, O'Neil, Roberts, Silcock, and Webster. Reserves : Kight aud. O'Brien.</p>
        <p>The" A "team had a very easy journey on Monday and improved their point average at the expense of the weak side sent from St. Helens. Three goals, nine tries to nil was the sum total of the score, and Warrington " A " still challenge Wigan" A" fDr the leadership.</p>
        <p>The combination table up-to-date is as follows P. W. L. D. G. T. Pts. G. T. Pts. Pts Wigan (A)..11... 9... 1... 1...28 48-200... 5 5 25...19 W'gton (A)10... 9... 1... 0...16 39-149... 3 10— 36...18 Chadderton.lo... 7... 3... 0...11 15— 67...12 15— 69...14 Widnes (A).10... 5... 3... 2... 7 9 41... 7 17— 65...12 St.H. (A)...12... 5... 6... 1...17 24-106...14 27-109...11 Oldham (A)10... 4... 5... 1... 9 26 96...13 15— 71... 9 B'gh'n R(A) 9... 4... 5... 0... 8 21— 79... 4 8— 32... 8 Runcorn(A) 9... 4... 5... 0... 5 14— 52...18 16— 81... 8 Swinton (A)10... 4... 6... 0...11 19— 79... 7 19— 71... 8 Leigh (A)...12... 3... 7... 2... 7 15— 59...13 19— 83... 8 Salford (A)10... 2... 6... 2... 5 8— 34...18 31-129... 6 R'd'leH.(A)l2... 2... 9... 1... 8 8— 40...24 49-195... 5</p>
        <p>The team selected to visit Leigh to-day is as follows :•—Knight, full back ; Savage, Berry, Jordan, and Roberts, three-quarters ; Forster and Shortr. halves ; Jolley, Morrison, Naylor, Lunt, and. Coleman, forwards.</p>
