      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-12-31_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>Parish Council and the Question.</head>
        <p>Parish Council and the Question.</p>
        <p>The decision of the Warrington Tramways Committee to change INd. for the tram ride to Stockton Heath upon the completion of the route, formed the principal item on the agenda at the meeting of the Stockto n Heath Parish Council which was held in the schools o n Thursday. Over this meetinc, Mr. Clarke presided and there were also present Messrs. Whitley, Fairhurs t, Bouchier, Mclntyre, Hallows, Warburton, T. R. West- brook, Welsby and Bradshaw (clerk). The Chairman introduced the question and remarked that when the preliminary negotiations for the construc- tion of the line from Stafford-road to Victoria-square, were in progress the Town Clerk gave the Parish Council his faithful assurance that the fare on the route would not exceed • the sum charged for a similar distance on other routes. He was perfectly satisfied that Nlr. Whittle gave the under- taking in goodlfadth and was confident that if the- matter of the fare was left to him he would carry out his promise. It was in consequence of that assurance that the Parish Council allowed the clause originally in the Bill which stipulated a penny fare, to be deleted, it being argued by the Corporation that if the route did not pay it would be unfair if they had not the option of revising the fare. Events were proving, however, that the Stockton Heath route was the best paying route and pointed to the fact that it would continue to pay when the line was completed. So long as that was the case he thought the fare should never exceed one penny—(hear. hear)—and he would be very sorry if the undertaking entered into by the Town Clerk was not carried out. The Parish Council had done their best to help the Corporation and after the arrangement made with the Town Clerk it would be an injustice if lid. were charged. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Westbrook: The Corporation can never charge more than a penny to Stafford-road. ' The remainder of the route can be dealt with by persons using the cars. Mr. Bouchier said they did not desire to appear to be at enmity with the Corporation, but they were all well aware of the assurance given by the Town Clerk—an assurance which he did not think was disputed. He moved "That this Parish Council, having in view the assur- ance given by the Town Clerk that the train fare to Victoria-square should never be any more than for an equal distance from Rylands-street to any other portion of the system,regrets that the Tramways Committeehave decided to charge lid." Mr. Warburton seconded, and the motion was unani- mously carried.</p>
