        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton on Tuesday when there were presant Messrs. A. R. Norman (presiding), T. Baxter, A. Fryer, J. L. Jones, G. Egerton-Warburton, J. H. Davies, W, M. Davies,. J. Bolshaw, J. Hallows, J. W. Temperley. H. Eyes, W. M. Morris, C. Roylance, C. E. Linaker, C. E. Livaker, Jun., E. Pilling, W. Harrison, J. W. Vithitley, J. Mounfield, A. G. Guest. J. Whitlow, J: Smith, T. i.ntwis, S. Woodward, T. Wright, A. Broome, J. Stokes, G: Horton, E, Bretherton, R. Billington, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough, (assistant clerk), and J. Cunnah (workhouse master). APPOINTMENT TO THE ASSESSMENT COMMITTEE. Mr. Woodward moved the appointment of Mr. W. Harrison to the Assessment Committee in place of the i late Mr. Lewis. He remarked that Mr. Harrison was a man who was well qualified to discharge the duties which would devolve upoulqm An amendment was moved by Mr. Morris and seconded by Mr. Hallows that Mr. Davies, Frodsham, should be elected to fill the vacancy. On a vote being taken Mr. Harrison was elected. THE APPOINTMFNT OF WORKHOUSE CHAPLAIN. Mr. Woodward reported that the sub-committee appointed to. consider the question of the appointment of a chaplain to the Workhouse in succession to the late Mr. Bennett, had gone into the matter very carefully. They had carefully gone over the whole of the ground and were of opinion that Nonconformity and Roman Catholicism needed some slight recognition and that some recompense should be given for ministrations which had hitherto been given gratuitously. They therefore ' recommended that the vicar of Little Leigh should be appointed chaplain at a salary of £3O per annum, that the Rev. Father Donlevy be paid £5 per annum for; ministrations to Roman Catholics and that £5 per annum be paid to the Preston Brook mission for i ministrations to Nonconformists. The payments were I in proportion t? the persons in the house professing the , particular religious views. Mr, Roylance moved the appointment of some other Roman Catholic priest as if the Rev. Father Donlevy was appointed be would have to resign his seat upon the Board. With regard to the Preston Brook Mission he I would point out that there was no resident minister while at Little Leigh there was a Baptist Chapel where there was a resident minister. He moved that the £lO be paid to the minister at Little Leigh and that £lO be paid to the Catholic Priest appointed. On a vote being taken the recommendations of the sub-committee were unanimously endorsed. Mr. Norman said he personally regretted that they could not call the Rev. Father Donlevy the Roman Catholic cb.aplainas he thought that the position should be dignified by that name. The Local Government Board had stipulated that the Workhouse chaplain must he an Anglican, whereas he thought both Roman Catholics and Nonconformists had a right to the same dignity of title. Seeing that what he desired he could not be thine without the sanction of the Local Go velar mrnt Board he gave notice that at the next meeting hb would move that a petition be sent to the Local Government Board asking them for equality in the</p>
