        <p>THE REMARKABLE BURNING CASE AT RUNCORN. Verdict of the Coroner's Jury. At Runcorn on Tuesday, before Mr. Fisher, deputy coroner, an inquest was held upon Annie Butterworth 05), wife of William Butterworth, captain os the Ship Canal Company's lighter No. 203,wh0 was terribly burnt in the 'cabin of the brigantine Kestrel, at Runcorn docks on Saturday, and died in the hospital on Sunday. The husband deposed that his wife was in the habit of taking a great deal of drink, and was troubled with fits. She had some drink on Saturday morning, aud when he left her at the Ship Canal Company's Office at two o'clock in the afternocli Ahe was far from sober. He next saw her at 10 p/m., when she was brought aboard badly burned. He was not " entered " on the Kestrel. Joha Frances Storey said he was a master mariner, in charge of the Kestrel. About four o'clock on Christ- mas Eve the deceased came into his cabin. She had no business there. Her husband came along with her or came immediately afterwards. Tne Deputy Coroner; The husband says he left her at two o'clock.—He has made a mistake. Was she sober? She was under the influence of drink, but she talked sensibly enough. She had two drinks of whisky, and the husband had a drink as well. The husband stopped a quarter of an hour, and after he went they had tea. Afterwards he sent a man twice to the lighter for the husband, but he could get no answer. He went into his stateroom to sleep, and left the deceased sitting on the bunk in the cabin. He was awakened by a noise, and he got up, and saw the woman inflames, and the mate tiling to put the flames out. He did not know how she got on the fire, unless she had a fit and fell on the fire. Cha ries Soraneer, mate of the Kestrel, gave corrobora tive evidence. Superintendent Ennion also gave evidence, and said it was three hours and a half before the woman's injuri es were attended to. The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death." FAILURES IN EN GLAND AND WALES.—According to "Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the total number of failures in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, December 23rd was 185, viz.:—New bankruptcy proceedings published in the London Gazette, 84; deeds of arrangement registered, 101. The respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were: bank- ruptcies, 92 : deeds of arrangement, Ts—total 167.; being a net increase in bankruptcies of 260,and a net increase in deeds of arrangement in1904,t0 date, of 481</p>
